globalThis.HELA_LIBRARY = [{"source":"CHANGELOG.md","title":"Changelog","summary":"Render Genome relation links in canonical order so replicas with different SQLite insertion orders produce identical notes.","category":"releases","archived":false,"link":"library/CHANGELOG.html","text":"# Changelog\n\nAll notable changes to this project.\n\n## [1.5.3] - 2026-11-07\n\n- Render Genome relation links in canonical order so replicas with different SQLite insertion orders produce identical notes.\n- Add an insertion-order regression; all 19 suites / 264 tests, the TypeScript build and zero-warning source lint pass.\n- Preserve the published 1.5.2 snapshot and all other cell/runtime pins.\n\n## [1.5.2] - 2026-11-07\n\n- Pin Genome's replicated vault timestamp/order convergence fix and explicit archived repair API; ordinary CLI sync still leaves divergent notes unresolved.\n- Replace Genome's unsafe `any` types with typed database results and validated request/import boundaries. Source lint now covers top-level files and fails on warnings; 19 suites / 263 tests and the TypeScript build pass.\n- Retain the managed individual/runtime/Docker/Nix installer and doctor shipped after 1.5.1; its mechanism acceptance remains documented separately.\n- Keep all other cell/runtime pins and historical versioned snapshots unchanged. No vault content, credentials, or operational backups are included in this source snapshot.\n\n## [1.5.1] - 2026-11-06\n\n- Refresh current EN/ID web pages, setup/integration/troubleshooting guides, and all ten cells' version/tool references from canonical metadata.\n- Validate generated documentation, local links/anchors, and browser configuration previews before Pages publication.\n- Refresh sanitized client examples and document deferred security, provider/hardware validation, SQLite safety, and optional Obsidian mirroring accurately.\n- Pin documentation-only cell commits in a new immutable snapshot. Cells remain 3.0.0; runtime remains 1.2.0. Historical snapshots, vendor versions, and live services are unchanged.\n\n## [1.5.0] - 2026-11-06\n\n- Pin runtime 1.2.0 with true first-call startup and profile/build/config-aware schema caching.\n- Generate standalone schema catalogs and documentation counts from canonical inventory metadata and isolated feature-variant probes.\n- Share inventory validation/path resolution across config, doctor, version, profile, and integration tooling; verify runtime catalog parity without child startup.\n- Measure cold/warm routing across all profiles; retain standalone compatibility and machine selections.\n\n## [1.4.0] - 2026-11-06\n\n- Pin current published cell/runtime revisions in a new snapshot; retain v1.0.0–v1.3.0 unchanged and correct runtime snapshot metadata to 1.1.0.\n- Include Genome memory safety, atomic claims, runtime timeout recovery, and clean-install Zod 4 compatibility fixes.\n- Replace process-alive smoke checks with isolated MCP initialize/tool discovery and strict version/pin agreement.\n- Correct Codex TOML to `mcp_servers`, escape generated values, and verify the 80-combination configuration matrix.\n- Record native x86_64/aarch64 Linux Nix builds separately from client authentication, hardware testing, and unresolved vault conflicts.\n\n## [3.0.0] - 2026-10-31\n\n### Changed\n- **Version reset to 3.0.0** — per-server version metadata baseline reset (Phase 7). All `package.json`, MCP `ServerInfo.version`, and HTTP metadata now report `3.0.0`. This is a metadata/interface baseline reset; for `hela-plastid` it is not an upgrade from the previous `4.2.0` advert.\n"},{"source":"CONTRIBUTING.md","title":"Contributing to the HeLa MCP Ecosystem","summary":"Thank you for your interest in contributing to the HeLa MCP Ecosystem! This document provides guidelines and information for contributors and maintainers.","category":"project","archived":false,"link":"library/CONTRIBUTING.html","text":"# Contributing to the HeLa MCP Ecosystem\n\nThank you for your interest in contributing to the **HeLa MCP Ecosystem**! This document provides guidelines and information for contributors and maintainers.\n\n![Blotcat carefully placing a new, glowing building block on top of an established, complex structure](assets/blotcat-contributing.jpg)\n\n---\n\n## 1. Architectural Boundaries & 10-MCP Ceiling\n\n* **The 10-MCP Capability Ceiling**: The ecosystem maintains exactly 10 canonical MCP servers (7 core headless, 3 specialized GUI/device). Contributions focus on reliability, performance, tool quality, and testing rather than adding new server repositories.\n* **The 4-Tier Naming Architecture**:\n  * Tier 1: Public Identity (`HeLa \u003cComponent>`, e.g. *HeLa Mitosis*, *HeLa Genome*)\n  * Tier 2: Machine Identifier (`hela-*`, e.g. `hela-mitosis`, `hela-genome`)\n  * Tier 3: Technical Source Repository (`chaining-mcp`, `project-mcp`, etc.)\n  * Tier 4: Immutable Commit Hash (the selected immutable snapshot in `config/snapshots/`)\n* **Repository Autonomy**: Upstream repositories remain independent and modular.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Development & Verification Workflow\n\n### 2.1 Local Environment Setup\n```bash\ngit clone https://github.com/Hela-Ecosystem/hela-mcp-ecosystem.git\ncd hela-mcp-ecosystem\n./setup.sh --profile dev-workspace --client skip --non-interactive\n```\n\n### 2.2 Running Diagnostics & Tests\nBefore submitting changes, ensure all test suites pass locally:\n\n```bash\n# 1. Run diagnostic health checks\nnpm run doctor\n\n# 2. Run 80-combination client configuration checks\nnpm run test:matrix\n\n# 3. Run standalone MCP integration (tool counts depend on features)\nnpm test\n\n# 4. Keep generated versions, tool references and site metadata current\nnpm ci --ignore-scripts\nnpm run docs:generate\nnpm run docs:check\nnpm run test:docs\n\n# 5. Run configured pre-commit checks\npre-commit run --all-files\n```\n\n### 2.3 Build and preview the complete documentation site\n\nUse Node.js 22 LTS. Markdown rendering is a pinned build-only dependency; visitors receive static HTML, not a client-side Markdown framework.\n\n```bash\nnpm ci --ignore-scripts\nnpm run docs:generate\nnpm run docs:build\npython3 -m http.server 8080 --bind 127.0.0.1 --directory _site\n```\n\nOpen `http://127.0.0.1:8080/library.html`. The builder publishes tracked root documentation, all Markdown under `docs/` (except website source files), Conductor records, the license, and public configuration examples. Add new documents to Git before building so they enter the publication inventory. Untracked notes, credentials, databases, Git internals and unrelated directories are not published.\n\nThe document bodies remain in their source language; English and Indonesian navigation lead to the same source content. Dated verification reports and milestone tracks are labeled historical. Relative document links, heading anchors and illustrations resolve within the website; original source downloads and `llms-full.txt` are generated from the same files.\n\n`npm run test:docs` checks publication coverage, links and anchors, safe Markdown rendering, source downloads, search, and deterministic builds. The existing Pages workflow installs the locked dependencies, checks the docs, and publishes `_site` to `gh-pages` when public documentation changes. Do not edit `_site` by hand.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Pull Request Guidelines\n\n1. **Target Branch**: Target the maintained default branch (`master`) unless a maintainer requests a release/development branch.\n2. **Commit Conventions**: Follow standard Conventional Commits:\n   * `feat(scope): ...`\n   * `fix(scope): ...`\n   * `docs(scope): ...`\n   * `test(scope): ...`\n   * `chore(scope): ...`\n3. **CI Matrix Conformance**: Configuration checks cover `ubuntu-latest` and `macos-latest` on Node.js 18, 20, and 22; documentation builds and tests run on Node 22. This is not MCP runtime compatibility evidence for Node 18; use Node 22 LTS for the tested full-stack development environment. Hardware, provider and authenticated GUI integrations need separate validation.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Attribution & Recognition\n\nThe HeLa project respectfully recognizes **Henrietta Lacks** and the enduring scientific legacy of HeLa cells as an inspiration for immortal, modular cellular architecture in software.\n"},{"source":"LICENSE","title":"MIT License","summary":"Permission is hereby granted, free of charge, to any person obtaining a copy of this software and associated documentation files (the \"Software\"), to deal in the Software…","category":"project","archived":false,"link":"library/LICENSE.html","text":"MIT License\n\nCopyright (c) 2026 Azzar\n\nPermission is hereby granted, free of charge, to any person obtaining a copy\nof this software and associated documentation files (the \"Software\"), to deal\nin the Software without restriction, including without limitation the rights\nto use, copy, modify, merge, publish, distribute, sublicense, and/or sell\ncopies of the Software, and to permit persons to whom the Software is\nfurnished to do so, subject to the following conditions:\n\nThe above copyright notice and this permission notice shall be included in all\ncopies or substantial portions of the Software.\n\nTHE SOFTWARE IS PROVIDED \"AS IS\", WITHOUT WARRANTY OF ANY KIND, EXPRESS OR\nIMPLIED, INCLUDING BUT NOT LIMITED TO THE WARRANTIES OF MERCHANTABILITY,\nFITNESS FOR A PARTICULAR PURPOSE AND NONINFRINGEMENT. IN NO EVENT SHALL THE\nAUTHORS OR COPYRIGHT HOLDERS BE LIABLE FOR ANY CLAIM, DAMAGES OR OTHER\nLIABILITY, WHETHER IN AN ACTION OF CONTRACT, TORT OR OTHERWISE, ARISING FROM,\nOUT OF OR IN CONNECTION WITH THE SOFTWARE OR THE USE OR OTHER DEALINGS IN THE\nSOFTWARE.\n\n---\n\nThis license applies to the AZZAR MCP Server Suite repository and its contents. Individual MCP servers maintain their own licenses. Please check each server's repository for specific licensing information.\n"},{"source":"README.md","title":"HeLa MCP Ecosystem","summary":"A modular, immortal cellular AI tooling ecosystem built on the Model Context Protocol (MCP), engineered by Azzar to equip AI agents with full-spectrum autonomous capabilities…","category":"gettingStarted","archived":false,"link":"library/README.html","text":"# HeLa MCP Ecosystem\n\nA modular, immortal cellular AI tooling ecosystem built on the Model Context Protocol (MCP), engineered by Azzar to equip AI agents with full-spectrum autonomous capabilities across reasoning, persistent memory, workspace management, system execution, research, design, device automation, and 3D modeling.\n\n> **New to MCP?** Read the **[Beginner's Guide](docs/beginner-guide.md)** for requirements, profile selection, installation, and verification.\n\n**Read all documentation on the website:** [Document library](https://hela.my.id/library.html) · [Navigasi Bahasa Indonesia](https://hela.my.id/id/library.html). Guides, release history, contribution rules, and historical project records are published in full, with search and source downloads. Documents retain their source language.\n\nCurrent source snapshot: **v1.5.3**, with runtime **1.2.0**. True first-call startup, fingerprinted discovery caches, canonical inventory tooling, and all-profile checks are implemented; see [on-demand runtime](docs/on-demand-runtime.md). Authenticated Zed/Codex ACP sessions, hardware integrations, and live vault conflict resolution remain separate; see [release notes](RELEASE_NOTES.md).\n\n![Blotcat plugging MCP server cables into the central AI Hub](assets/blotcat-hero.jpg)\n\n---\n\n## Respectful Attribution & Metaphor\n\nThe **HeLa MCP Ecosystem** is named in respectful recognition of **Henrietta Lacks** (1920–1951) and the immortal scientific legacy of HeLa cells, which transformed global biomedical research. In this software architecture, HeLa serves as a **cellular biology metaphor**:\n\n* **Cellular Modularity**: Just as biological cells contain specialized organelles coordinated by the nucleus and genome, the HeLa MCP Ecosystem deploys specialized tool servers coordinated by a dual backbone.\n* **Continuous Replicability & Immortality**: Environments can be snapshot-pinned, reproduced deterministically on any machine, and restored cleanly without state degradation.\n* **Component Independence**: Underlying repositories, technical package names, and Git commit histories remain autonomous and unaltered while functioning as a harmonious organism.\n\n---\n\n## Dual-Backbone Architecture\n\nEvery profile in the HeLa ecosystem is anchored by two core backbone servers:\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TB\n    Agent[\"Agent / LLM Orchestrator\"]\n\n    Mitosis[\"HeLa Mitosis\u003cbr/>(Orchestrator)\u003cbr/>- Dynamic Routing\u003cbr/>- Step Reasoning\u003cbr/>- Tool Planning\"]\n    Genome[\"HeLa Genome\u003cbr/>(State & Memory)\u003cbr/>- Knowledge Graph\u003cbr/>- Entities / Relations\u003cbr/>- Observations\"]\n\n    Membrane[\"HeLa Membrane\u003cbr/>Workspace FS\"]\n    Nucleus[\"HeLa Nucleus\u003cbr/>Command Exec\"]\n    Ribosome[\"HeLa Ribosome\u003cbr/>PTY Harness\"]\n    Enzyme[\"HeLa Enzyme\u003cbr/>Research / Wiki\"]\n    Cytosol[\"HeLa Cytosol\u003cbr/>Browser DOM\"]\n\n    Agent -- \"stdio JSON-RPC\" --> Mitosis\n    Agent -- \"stdio JSON-RPC\" --> Genome\n    Mitosis \u003c-->|\"Shared Context\"| Genome\n    Mitosis --> Membrane & Nucleus & Ribosome & Enzyme & Cytosol\n    Genome --> Membrane & Nucleus & Ribosome & Enzyme & Cytosol\n```\n\n* **HeLa Mitosis (`hela-mitosis`)**: Cellular division and orchestration backbone. Handles dynamic tool routing, prompt decomposition, Needle-backed sequential reasoning (`sequentialthinking` / `agent_run`), and multi-tool planning.\n* **HeLa Genome (`hela-genome`)**: Cellular memory and state backbone. Maintains the persistent SQLite knowledge graph (`memory.db`), entity-relation tracking, session restoration, and project milestones.\n\n---\n\n## 10-MCP Component Taxonomy\n\n| Canonical Identity | Machine ID (`id`) | Technical Source Repo | Scope | Cellular Metaphor & Role |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **HeLa Mitosis** | `hela-mitosis` | [`chaining-mcp`](https://github.com/Hela-Ecosystem/chaining-mcp) | Core | **Orchestrator**: Bundled Needle 3 agent runtime, dynamic routing, 40 bundled prompt templates |\n| **HeLa Genome** | `hela-genome` | [`project-mcp`](https://github.com/Hela-Ecosystem/project-mcp) | Core | **State Backbone**: Entity-relation knowledge graph, milestone tracking, memory persistence |\n| **HeLa Membrane** | `hela-membrane` | [`filesystem-mcp`](https://github.com/Hela-Ecosystem/filesystem-mcp) | Core | **Workspace**: Sandboxed filesystem operations, recursive search, archive extraction |\n| **HeLa Nucleus** | `hela-nucleus` | [`terminal-mcp`](https://github.com/Hela-Ecosystem/terminal-mcp) | Core | **Execution**: Command execution, subshell isolation, RTK token-optimized execution |\n| **HeLa Ribosome** | `hela-ribosome` | [`menager-mcp`](https://github.com/Hela-Ecosystem/menager-mcp) | Core | **Process Harness**: Interactive PTY multiplexing, Regex hooks, lifecycle management |\n| **HeLa Enzyme** | `hela-enzyme` | [`researcher-mcp`](https://github.com/Hela-Ecosystem/researcher-mcp) | Core | **Knowledge**: Zero-key dual-tier web search + arXiv + Google News RSS + Wikipedia synthesis and fact-checking |\n| **HeLa Cytosol** | `hela-cytosol` | [`browser-mcp`](https://github.com/Hela-Ecosystem/browser-mcp) | Core | **Interaction**: Playwright browser automation, accessibility trees, DOM perception |\n| **HeLa Phenotype** | `hela-phenotype` | [`designer-mcp`](https://github.com/Hela-Ecosystem/designer-mcp) | Specialized | **Design**: UI/UX design tokens, OKLCH color palettes, Tailwind and component synthesis |\n| **HeLa Receptor** | `hela-receptor` | [`scrcpy-mcp`](https://github.com/Hela-Ecosystem/scrcpy-mcp) | Specialized | **Mobile**: Android device automation, ADB bridge, XML view hierarchy inspection |\n| **HeLa Plastid** | `hela-plastid` | [`ll3m-mcp`](https://github.com/Hela-Ecosystem/ll3m-mcp) | Specialized | **3D Modeling**: Autonomous Blender procedural modeling, materials, and render pipeline |\n\n---\n\n## Quick Start\n\n### 1. Prerequisites\n* **Node.js**: use Node 22 LTS for the tested full stack. Individual packages declare their own engine ranges; Receptor requires 22 and Enzyme requires at least 20.18.1.\n* **Git**: `>= 2.30.0`\n* **SQLite**: Genome uses Node bindings; the SQLite CLI is optional for inspection.\n\n### 2. Run Diagnostics (`setup.sh doctor`)\nClone the source, then select an installation mode:\n\n```bash\ngit clone https://github.com/Hela-Ecosystem/hela-mcp-ecosystem.git\ncd hela-mcp-ecosystem\n./setup.sh --mode individual --profile dev-workspace --client opencode --non-interactive\n./setup.sh doctor --mode individual --profile dev-workspace\n```\n\n### 3. Interactive Installation & Profile Selection\n```bash\n./setup.sh\n```\n\n### 4. Non-Interactive One-Liners\n\n**Full Developer Desktop (Cursor IDE):**\n```bash\n./setup.sh --profile dev-workspace --client cursor --non-interactive\n```\n\n**Headless Linux Server (Claude Desktop / CLI):**\n```bash\n./setup.sh --profile headless-server --client claude --non-interactive\n```\n\n**Pinned Immutable Release Snapshot:**\n```bash\n./setup.sh --profile dev-workspace --client cursor --snapshot v1.5.3 --non-interactive\n```\n\n**Single-Endpoint Runtime (one MCP connection for the selected profile):**\n```bash\n./setup.sh --profile dev-workspace --client opencode --runtime --non-interactive\n```\n\n**Docker cells, Docker runtime, or Nix runtime:**\n```bash\n./setup.sh --mode docker --profile dev-workspace --client cursor --non-interactive\n./setup.sh --mode docker-runtime --profile dev-workspace --client opencode --non-interactive\n./setup.sh --mode nix --profile dev-workspace --client codex --non-interactive\n```\n\nInstalls use private managed prefixes under `${XDG_DATA_HOME:-$HOME/.local/share}/hela/installations/`. Existing repositories and live client files are not overwritten. Review and merge the generated client fragment. Use `--prefix` for an explicit location and pass it to doctor. See [installation modes and doctor](docs/installation.md) for HTTP transport, Compose, Nix GC roots, snapshots, optional model assets, prerequisites, and failure recovery.\n\n---\n\n## Supported AI Clients & Output Formats\n\nThe installer writes private fragments inside its prefix, never directly into live client files:\n\n- Cursor, Claude, Gemini, Antigravity: `mcpServers` JSON.\n- OpenCode and Kilo: `mcp` JSON with an installed local launcher, or a runtime HTTP URL.\n- Zed: `context_servers` JSON; preserve unrelated settings when merging.\n- Codex: `[mcp_servers.*]` TOML, with a launcher or HTTP URL.\n- Docker: functional `compose.json` for the built image, not speculative per-cell Dockerfiles or stdio port mappings.\n\n`--client skip` installs without generating a client fragment. Client authentication and GUI acceptance remain separate.\n\n---\n\n## Available Agent Profiles\n\n| Profile ID | Target System | Backbone Included | Servers Included | Focus Area |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `minimal` | Any | Mitosis + Genome | Enzyme | Research and memory without GUI dependencies |\n| `dev-workspace` | Any (GUI recommended) | Mitosis + Genome | Membrane, Nucleus, Ribosome, Enzyme, Phenotype, Cytosol | Full-stack software engineering, testing, web UI |\n| `headless-server` | Headless Linux | Mitosis + Genome | Membrane, Nucleus, Ribosome, Enzyme, Phenotype | Cloud server administration, CI/CD, headless dev |\n| `research` | Headless or GUI | Mitosis + Genome | Enzyme, Membrane, Cytosol | Deep literature research, document synthesis, web crawling |\n| `web-devops` | Headless or GUI | Mitosis + Genome | Membrane, Nucleus, Phenotype, Cytosol | Frontend development, Tailwind styling, DOM verification |\n| `android-testing` | Device connected | Mitosis + Genome | Nucleus, Receptor, Enzyme | Mobile QA testing, ADB automation, APK validation |\n| `3d-modeling` | GUI (Blender installed) | Mitosis + Genome | Plastid, Membrane, Nucleus | 3D procedural modeling, materials, rendering |\n| `all` | Full workstation | Mitosis + Genome | All 10 HeLa MCP servers | Complete full-spectrum AI agent capabilities |\n\n> **Single-endpoint serve mode:** add `--runtime` to a profile command. The default profile remains `dev-workspace`, not `all`.\n\nProfiles are installation choices, not machine-specific code paths. All eight pass isolated runtime verification with namespacing off/on (**16 checks**), including exact tool/schema parity and safe read-only routing. Lappy's ten-server `all` roster and servy's eight-cell `dev-workspace` setup remain different by design. See [all-profile verification](docs/profile-verification-2026-11-06.md); run `npm run test:profiles`, or append `-- --profile \u003cid>` on a partial installation.\n\nRuntime discovery now starts **zero cells**; only invoked cells are started. [Generated profile counts](docs/generated-profile-counts.md) come from the inventory/catalog rather than static client-specific totals. `./setup.sh doctor --profile \u003cid> --runtime` checks cold catalog parity as well as versions, paths, and revisions.\n\nFor complete persona guides and pipeline specifications, see **[Profiles Catalog](docs/profiles.md)**.\n\n---\n\n## Operational Workflows (Workflows A–F)\n\nThe ecosystem is built for structured multi-server agent workflows:\n* **Workflow A: Autonomous Feature Engineering**: Genome (restore) → Mitosis (decompose) → Membrane (code) → Nucleus (test) → Genome (persist).\n* **Workflow B: Deep Research & Architecture Synthesis**: Enzyme (search) → Cytosol (scrape) → Mitosis (synthesize) → Genome (record) → Membrane (write).\n* **Workflow C: Web Feature with Design & Browser Verification**: Phenotype (tokens) → Membrane (build) → Nucleus (serve) → Cytosol (DOM QA) → Genome (record).\n* **Workflow D: Mobile App Test Automation**: Genome (issue) → Nucleus (adb) → Receptor (drive UI) → Genome (record pass).\n* **Workflow E: Autonomous 3D Asset Creation & Render Pipeline**: Genome (spec) → Mitosis (plan) → Plastid (Blender) → Membrane (save) → Genome (record).\n* **Workflow F: Long-Horizon Complex Refactor with PTY Harness**: Ribosome (PTY) → Nucleus (exec) → Genome (persist).\n\nFor step-by-step tool invocation sequences, schemas, and diagrams, see **[Workflows & Integration Guide](docs/workflows.md)**.\n\n---\n\n## Project Health & Diagnostics\n\n```bash\n# Run system and server diagnostic health check\nnpm run doctor\n# or\n./setup.sh doctor\n\n# Run 80-combination client configuration checks (not GUI integration)\nnpm run test:matrix\n\n# Run master integration test suite (295 tools across all 10 servers)\nnpm test\n```\n\n---\n\n## Documentation Index\n\n* **[Beginner's Step-by-Step Guide](docs/beginner-guide.md)**: 3-minute, zero-code onboarding.\n* **[Keys & Secrets Guide](docs/keys-and-secrets.md)**: API key setup, sign-up URLs, and zero-key offline fallbacks.\n* **[Profiles Reference](docs/profiles.md)**: Full persona reference and capability mappings.\n* **[Workflows & Integration Guide](docs/workflows.md)**: Cross-MCP workflow pipelines (Workflows A–F).\n* **[Security & Reliability Audit](docs/security-audit.md)**: Threat model, sandboxing, and isolation standards.\n* **[Architecture Guide](docs/architecture.md)**: In-depth cellular architecture and communication topologies.\n* **[Troubleshooting Guide](docs/troubleshooting.md)**: Common issues, diagnostic codes, and recovery procedures.\n\n---\n\n## License & Attribution\n\nDistributed under the MIT License. Underlying repositories remain independently copyrighted by their respective authors.\n\nThe HeLa project respectfully recognizes **Henrietta Lacks** and the enduring scientific legacy of HeLa cells.\n"},{"source":"RELEASE_NOTES.md","title":"HeLa MCP Ecosystem Release Notes","summary":"Pin Genome source 9ba1d0e. Relation links now render in one canonical order regardless of database insertion order, preventing relation-order-only conflicts after a lossless…","category":"releases","archived":false,"link":"library/RELEASE_NOTES.html","text":"# HeLa MCP Ecosystem Release Notes\n\n## v1.5.3 — canonical relation ordering\n\nPin Genome source `9ba1d0e`. Relation links now render in one canonical order regardless of database insertion order, preventing relation-order-only conflicts after a lossless repair. All 19 suites / 264 tests pass; the TypeScript build and zero-warning source lint pass. The published 1.5.2 snapshot remains unchanged, as do all other cell/runtime pins and package versions.\n\n## v1.5.2 — replicated vault convergence\n\nPin Genome source `c32ae94`: replicated notes keep the earliest creation timestamp and latest update timestamp, while incoming observation supersets can retain their order without losing database-only facts. The explicit programmatic repair API archives both originals, respects snapshot/lock checks, and permits rejecting a plan before publication. Normal CLI sync does not choose conflict winners automatically.\n\nGenome's 19 suites / 263 tests and TypeScript build pass, including regressions reproducing the previous timestamp/order loops and validating SQL bindings, JSON rows and tool arguments. Source lint reports zero errors and warnings, covers top-level files and fails on warnings. Package versions remain 3.0.0 for cells and 1.2.0 for runtime; this source snapshot records the exact fixed Genome commit. Other pins and historical snapshots are unchanged. Real vault repair and MCP reconnection require separate, backed-up operational verification; no private vault data is published here.\n\n## v1.5.1 — current documentation and bilingual website\n\nDocumentation-only refresh: all ten cells remain **3.0.0**, and runtime remains **1.2.0**. The new source snapshot pins the documentation commits without rewriting v1.0.0–v1.5.0. Plastid and Receptor documentation was merged into their default branches without discarding unrelated dependency updates; the snapshot deliberately pins the documentation-only parents, not those dependency changes.\n\nThe EN/ID website now loads generated versions, canonical tool names, and profile metadata. Configuration previews use real repository directories and Codex `mcp_servers` sections. Source guides cover all eight profiles, cold discovery, optional integrations, safe backup/restore, and the limits of current verification. Generated private configurations may contain supplied credentials; committed examples are regenerated with a sanitized environment.\n\nDocumentation checks cover 74 regression cases plus local links/anchors and structured metadata; the CLI matrix covers 80 combinations. Browser verification covers 64 visible selections per language and all 16 pages at desktop/mobile widths. These are documentation/UI checks, not new authenticated ACP, provider, Android, or Blender acceptance. Publication does not restart the runtime or change deferred access controls, credentials, databases, or vault conflicts.\n\n## v1.5.0 — on-demand runtime and canonical discovery\n\nRuntime **1.2.0** starts cells only when invoked, shares concurrent startup attempts, and discovers schemas without spawning cells. Revision/build/config-aware caches reject stale entries; legacy/corrupt caches fall back to a validated generated catalog. Unknown calls no longer start unrelated cells. Standalone servers, all eight profiles, and the `dev-workspace` default are preserved.\n\nThe canonical inventory drives config generation, doctor/version/profile checks, schema catalogs, and generated documentation counts. Doctor can verify exact runtime discovery with no cell startup via `--runtime`. A new immutable v1.5.0 snapshot pins runtime `9c92dbf`; older versioned snapshots are unchanged.\n\nVerification: **32** runtime regressions on both Linux architectures, **16** all-profile namespace-mode checks, **80** client configurations, four probe regressions, and four inventory regressions. Cold/warm measurements are recorded, not treated as universal benchmarks. See [implementation and verification scope](docs/on-demand-runtime.md). ACP authentication, external client reloads, gateway retirement, vault conflict review, hardware/provider integrations, and deferred security remain separate work.\n\n**Servy rollout verified:** public/local discovery exposes 236 tools without starting any cells; the first safe Nucleus call starts only Nucleus. Deployed doctor, catalog, and revision checks pass. Fresh backups and rollback material are retained, and all 633 existing conflict archive files are unchanged. See the [rollout evidence](docs/on-demand-runtime.md#verified-servy-rollout).\n\n## v1.4.0 — memory safety and runtime verification\n\nThe v1.4.0 source snapshot pins the published cell revisions and runtime 1.1.0. Older versioned snapshots are unchanged. `latest-stable.json` tracks this source snapshot, not a guarantee that every optional integration or GUI session has been exercised.\n\n- Genome: 19 suites / 242 tests with clean lockfile dependencies, including WAL-safe backups, isolated restore, vault conflicts, repeated imports, and exclusive mission claims.\n- Runtime: 20 isolated fault tests covering hung children, SIGTERM/SIGKILL, actual process exit, one restart, profile/cache boundaries, and shutdown cleanup.\n- Nix: real Node 22 package builds on x86_64-linux and native aarch64-linux, using pinned inputs and fixed-output npm dependencies.\n- Dependencies: clean-install Zod 4 build fixes in Genome and Enzyme; Enzyme's 79 tests and Phenotype's 74 tests pass.\n- Ecosystem: ten isolated standalone MCP handshakes and 80 configuration-generation combinations pass. Codex TOML now uses `mcp_servers` and is accepted by the native parser. These are not authenticated GUI tests.\n- Discovery: 290 standalone tools with LLM and model-backed agent features disabled; the same isolated `dev-workspace` runtime exposes 230 tools including `hela.pipe`. Feature-enabled deployments can expose additional Mitosis tools and the optional agent alias; query `tools/list` rather than assuming a static total.\n\nKnown limits: Zed/Codex ACP authenticated-session verification remains pending; duplicate ownership warnings do not establish a connection failure. Existing live vault conflicts remain archived for deliberate review. Android/Blender discovery is not hardware integration. Endpoint authentication and credential rotation remain operator work, not changes included in this release.\n\n**Servy deployment verified (2026-11-06):** the rooted ARM Nix package serves the eight-cell `dev-workspace` profile under systemd. Local/public discovery returns 236 live tools, versus 230 in feature-disabled probes; all three Genome mission tools are present. Deployed doctor and strict version/pin checks pass. Existing conflict archives and unrelated client processes were preserved. See [deployment evidence and remaining client/gateway work](docs/deployment-2026-11-06.md).\n\n**All-profile follow-up (2026-11-06):** all eight profiles pass runtime startup/discovery, exact tool/schema parity, and safe read-only routing in both namespacing modes (**16/16** checks). All ten standalone cells plus runtime pass strict version/pin verification; the 80 client-configuration combinations and four probe regressions pass. Machine selections and release snapshots are unchanged. See [profile verification](docs/profile-verification-2026-11-06.md).\n\n## Historical v1.0.0 notes\n\nWe are proud to announce the official **v1.0.0** release of the **HeLa MCP Ecosystem** — a modular, immortal cellular AI tooling stack built on the Model Context Protocol (MCP), engineered by Azzar.\n\n---\n\n## Architectural Vision & Metaphor\n\nThe **HeLa MCP Ecosystem** is named in respectful recognition of **Henrietta Lacks** (1920–1951) and the enduring scientific legacy of HeLa cells. Using a **cellular biology metaphor**, the ecosystem organizes 10 specialized MCP servers into a harmonious organism:\n\n* **Cellular Specialization**: Distinct organelles (Nucleus, Membrane, Enzyme, Ribosome, Plastid) perform targeted domains.\n* **Deterministic Immortality**: Environments can be snapshot-pinned (`config/snapshots/v1.0.0.json`) and restored cleanly on any machine.\n* **Repository Independence**: Underlying source repositories, technical package names, and Git commit histories remain 100% autonomous.\n\n---\n\n## Key Highlights & Milestones Delivered\n\n### 1. Dual-Backbone Architecture\nEvery profile is grounded by two core backbones:\n* **HeLa Mitosis (`chaining-mcp`)**: Cellular division and orchestration backbone. Bundled Needle 3 agent runtime (plan → execute → observe, offline-first), Needle-backed sequential reasoning (`sequentialthinking`, `agent_run`), task decomposition, and 40 domain prompts. No external sequential-thinking server.\n* **HeLa Genome (`Project-Guardian-mcp-server`)**: Living memory and state backbone. SQLite knowledge graph (`memory.db`), entity-relation tracking, cross-session context restoration, and decision memory.\n\n### 2. 10-MCP Component Taxonomy\n* **HeLa Membrane** (`filesystem-mcp-server`): Sandboxed workspace filesystem operations and search.\n* **HeLa Nucleus** (`terminal-mcp-server`): Isolated command execution with RTK token optimization.\n* **HeLa Ribosome** (`menager-mcp-server`): Interactive PTY session multiplexing and Regex hook engine.\n* **HeLa Enzyme** (`research-assistant-mcp-server`): Unified Google Custom Search + Wikipedia caching.\n* **HeLa Cytosol** (`Browser-Agent`): Playwright browser automation and DOM accessibility perception.\n* **HeLa Phenotype** (`the-designer`): UI/UX design tokens, OKLCH palettes, and Tailwind component synthesis.\n* **HeLa Receptor** (`scrcpy-mcp`): Android mobile automation via ADB bridge.\n* **HeLa Plastid** (`ll3m-agent`): Autonomous Blender procedural 3D modeling and rendering.\n\n### 3. Diagnostic Health Checker (`setup.sh doctor`)\nComprehensive automated diagnostic tool auditing host prerequisites (Node.js >=18, Git, SQLite3), server builds, commit pins, stdio JSON-RPC smoke readiness (\u003c300ms), external runtimes (Playwright, ADB, Blender), and zero-key offline fallback modes.\n\n### 4. 70-Combination Multi-Client Matrix (`npm run test:matrix`)\nFull verification across all 10 client backends (Cursor, Claude, Gemini, Antigravity, OpenCode, Kilo, Zed, Codex TOML, Docker Compose, Skip) and 7 agent profiles with 100% schema accuracy.\n\n### 5. Master Integration Test Suite (`npm test`)\nAutomated stdio JSON-RPC handshake and tools discovery across all 10 servers (**295 active tools discovered and validated in 2.5s**).\n\n### 6. Interactive Documentation Website (`docs/site/`)\nModern, responsive documentation landing site featuring:\n* Interactive live Client & Profile Configurator.\n* Video showcases & embedded YouTube demos.\n* Uploaded Asciinema terminal casts.\n* Searchable 10-MCP Cellular Taxonomy directory.\n\n---\n\n## Quick Start\n\n```bash\ngit clone https://github.com/Hela-Ecosystem/hela-mcp-ecosystem.git\ncd hela-mcp-ecosystem\n./setup.sh doctor\n./setup.sh\n```\n\n---\n\n## Documentation\n\n* [Beginner's Step-by-Step Guide](docs/beginner-guide.md)\n* [Architecture Guide](docs/architecture.md)\n* [Cross-MCP Workflows A–F](docs/workflows.md)\n* [Security & Reliability Audit](docs/security-audit.md)\n* [Troubleshooting Guide](docs/troubleshooting.md)\n* [Keys & Secrets Matrix](docs/keys-and-secrets.md)\n\n---\n\n## License & Attribution\n\nDistributed under the MIT License. Developed by Azzar.\n\nThe HeLa project respectfully recognizes **Henrietta Lacks** and the enduring scientific legacy of HeLa cells.\n"},{"source":"conductor/code_styleguides/general.md","title":"General Code Style Principles","summary":"This document outlines general coding principles that apply across all languages and frameworks used in this project.","category":"project","archived":false,"link":"library/conductor/code_styleguides/general.html","text":"# General Code Style Principles\n\nThis document outlines general coding principles that apply across all languages\nand frameworks used in this project.\n\n## Readability\n\n-   Code should be easy to read and understand by humans.\n-   Avoid overly clever or obscure constructs.\n\n## Consistency\n\n-   Follow existing patterns in the codebase.\n-   Maintain consistent formatting, naming, and structure.\n\n## Simplicity\n\n-   Prefer simple solutions over complex ones.\n-   Break down complex problems into smaller, manageable parts.\n\n## Maintainability\n\n-   Write code that is easy to modify and extend.\n-   Minimize dependencies and coupling.\n\n## Documentation\n\n-   Document *why* something is done, not just *what*.\n-   Keep documentation up-to-date with code changes.\n"},{"source":"conductor/code_styleguides/javascript.md","title":"Google JavaScript Style Guide Summary","summary":"This document summarizes key rules and best practices from the Google JavaScript Style Guide.","category":"project","archived":false,"link":"library/conductor/code_styleguides/javascript.html","text":"# Google JavaScript Style Guide Summary\n\nThis document summarizes key rules and best practices from the Google JavaScript\nStyle Guide.\n\n## 1. Source File Basics\n\n-   **File Naming:** All lowercase, with underscores (`_`) or dashes (`-`).\n    Extension must be `.js`.\n-   **File Encoding:** UTF-8.\n-   **Whitespace:** Use only ASCII horizontal spaces (0x20). Tabs are forbidden\n    for indentation.\n\n## 2. Source File Structure\n\n-   New files should be ES modules (`import`/`export`).\n-   **Exports:** Use named exports (`export {MyClass};`). **Do not use default\n    exports.**\n-   **Imports:** Do not use line-wrapped imports. The `.js` extension in import\n    paths is mandatory.\n\n## 3. Formatting\n\n-   **Braces:** Required for all control structures (`if`, `for`, `while`,\n    etc.), even single-line blocks. Use K&R style (\"Egyptian brackets\").\n-   **Indentation:** +2 spaces for each new block.\n-   **Semicolons:** Every statement must be terminated with a semicolon.\n-   **Column Limit:** 80 characters.\n-   **Line-wrapping:** Indent continuation lines at least +4 spaces.\n-   **Whitespace:** Use single blank lines between methods. No trailing\n    whitespace.\n\n## 4. Language Features\n\n-   **Variable Declarations:** Use `const` by default, `let` if reassignment is\n    needed. **`var` is forbidden.**\n-   **Array Literals:** Use trailing commas. Do not use the `Array` constructor.\n-   **Object Literals:** Use trailing commas and shorthand properties. Do not\n    use the `Object` constructor.\n-   **Classes:** Do not use JavaScript getter/setter properties (`get name()`).\n    Provide ordinary methods instead.\n-   **Functions:** Prefer arrow functions for nested functions to preserve\n    `this` context.\n-   **String Literals:** Use single quotes (`'`). Use template literals (`` `\n    ``) for multi-line strings or complex interpolation.\n-   **Control Structures:** Prefer `for-of` loops. `for-in` loops should only be\n    used on dict-style objects.\n-   **`this`:** Only use `this` in class constructors, methods, or in arrow\n    functions defined within them.\n-   **Equality Checks:** Always use identity operators (`===` / `!==`).\n\n## 5. Disallowed Features\n\n-   `with` keyword.\n-   `eval()` or `Function(...string)`.\n-   Automatic Semicolon Insertion.\n-   Modifying builtin objects (`Array.prototype.foo = ...`).\n\n## 6. Naming\n\n-   **Classes:** `UpperCamelCase`.\n-   **Methods & Functions:** `lowerCamelCase`.\n-   **Constants:** `CONSTANT_CASE` (all uppercase with underscores).\n-   **Non-constant Fields & Variables:** `lowerCamelCase`.\n\n## 7. JSDoc\n\n-   JSDoc is used on all classes, fields, and methods.\n-   Use `@param`, `@return`, `@override`, `@deprecated`.\n-   Type annotations are enclosed in braces (e.g., `/** @param {string} userName\n    */`).\n\n*Source:\n[Google JavaScript Style Guide](https://google.github.io/styleguide/jsguide.html)*\n"},{"source":"conductor/code_styleguides/python.md","title":"Google Python Style Guide Summary","summary":"This document summarizes key rules and best practices from the Google Python Style Guide.","category":"project","archived":false,"link":"library/conductor/code_styleguides/python.html","text":"# Google Python Style Guide Summary\n\nThis document summarizes key rules and best practices from the Google Python\nStyle Guide.\n\n## 1. Python Language Rules\n\n-   **Linting:** Run `pylint` on your code to catch bugs and style issues.\n-   **Imports:** Use `import x` for packages/modules. Use `from x import y` only\n    when `y` is a submodule.\n-   **Exceptions:** Use built-in exception classes. Do not use bare `except:`\n    clauses.\n-   **Global State:** Avoid mutable global state. Module-level constants are\n    okay and should be `ALL_CAPS_WITH_UNDERSCORES`.\n-   **Comprehensions:** Use for simple cases. Avoid for complex logic where a\n    full loop is more readable.\n-   **Default Argument Values:** Do not use mutable objects (like `[]` or `{}`)\n    as default values.\n-   **True/False Evaluations:** Use implicit false (e.g., `if not my_list:`).\n    Use `if foo is None:` to check for `None`.\n-   **Type Annotations:** Strongly encouraged for all public APIs.\n\n## 2. Python Style Rules\n\n-   **Line Length:** Maximum 80 characters.\n-   **Indentation:** 4 spaces per indentation level. Never use tabs.\n-   **Blank Lines:** Two blank lines between top-level definitions (classes,\n    functions). One blank line between method definitions.\n-   **Whitespace:** Avoid extraneous whitespace. Surround binary operators with\n    single spaces.\n-   **Docstrings:** Use `\"\"\"triple double quotes\"\"\"`. Every public module,\n    function, class, and method must have a docstring.\n    -   **Format:** Start with a one-line summary. Include `Args:`, `Returns:`,\n        and `Raises:` sections.\n-   **Strings:** Use f-strings for formatting. Be consistent with single (`'`)\n    or double (`\"`) quotes.\n-   **`TODO` Comments:** Use `TODO(username): Fix this.` format.\n-   **Imports Formatting:** Imports should be on separate lines and grouped:\n    standard library, third-party, and your own application's imports.\n\n## 3. Naming\n\n-   **General:** `snake_case` for modules, functions, methods, and variables.\n-   **Classes:** `PascalCase`.\n-   **Constants:** `ALL_CAPS_WITH_UNDERSCORES`.\n-   **Internal Use:** Use a single leading underscore (`_internal_variable`) for\n    internal module/class members.\n\n## 4. Main\n\n-   All executable files should have a `main()` function that contains the main\n    logic, called from a `if __name__ == '__main__':` block.\n\n**BE CONSISTENT.** When editing code, match the existing style.\n\n*Source:\n[Google Python Style Guide](https://google.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html)*\n"},{"source":"conductor/code_styleguides/typescript.md","title":"Google TypeScript Style Guide Summary","summary":"This document summarizes key rules and best practices from the Google TypeScript Style Guide, which is enforced by the gts tool.","category":"project","archived":false,"link":"library/conductor/code_styleguides/typescript.html","text":"# Google TypeScript Style Guide Summary\n\nThis document summarizes key rules and best practices from the Google TypeScript\nStyle Guide, which is enforced by the `gts` tool.\n\n## 1. Language Features\n\n-   **Variable Declarations:** Always use `const` or `let`. **`var` is\n    forbidden.** Use `const` by default.\n-   **Modules:** Use ES6 modules (`import`/`export`). **Do not use\n    `namespace`.**\n-   **Exports:** Use named exports (`export {MyClass};`). **Do not use default\n    exports.**\n-   **Classes:**\n    -   **Do not use `#private` fields.** Use TypeScript's `private` visibility\n        modifier.\n    -   Mark properties never reassigned outside the constructor with\n        `readonly`.\n    -   **Never use the `public` modifier** (it's the default). Restrict\n        visibility with `private` or `protected` where possible.\n-   **Functions:** Prefer function declarations for named functions. Use arrow\n    functions for anonymous functions/callbacks.\n-   **String Literals:** Use single quotes (`'`). Use template literals (`` `\n    ``) for interpolation and multi-line strings.\n-   **Equality Checks:** Always use triple equals (`===`) and not equals\n    (`!==`).\n-   **Type Assertions:** **Avoid type assertions (`x as SomeType`) and\n    non-nullability assertions (`y!`)**. If you must use them, provide a clear\n    justification.\n\n## 2. Disallowed Features\n\n-   **`any` Type:** **Avoid `any`**. Prefer `unknown` or a more specific type.\n-   **Wrapper Objects:** Do not instantiate `String`, `Boolean`, or `Number`\n    wrapper classes.\n-   **Automatic Semicolon Insertion (ASI):** Do not rely on it. **Explicitly end\n    all statements with a semicolon.**\n-   **`const enum`:** Do not use `const enum`. Use plain `enum` instead.\n-   **`eval()` and `Function(...string)`:** Forbidden.\n\n## 3. Naming\n\n-   **`UpperCamelCase`:** For classes, interfaces, types, enums, and decorators.\n-   **`lowerCamelCase`:** For variables, parameters, functions, methods, and\n    properties.\n-   **`CONSTANT_CASE`:** For global constant values, including enum values.\n-   **`_` Prefix/Suffix:** **Do not use `_` as a prefix or suffix** for\n    identifiers, including for private properties.\n\n## 4. Type System\n\n-   **Type Inference:** Rely on type inference for simple, obvious types. Be\n    explicit for complex types.\n-   **`undefined` and `null`:** Both are supported. Be consistent within your\n    project.\n-   **Optional vs. `|undefined`:** Prefer optional parameters and fields (`?`)\n    over adding `|undefined` to the type.\n-   **`Array\u003cT>` Type:** Use `T[]` for simple types. Use `Array\u003cT>` for more\n    complex union types (e.g., `Array\u003cstring | number>`).\n-   **`{}` Type:** **Do not use `{}`**. Prefer `unknown`, `Record\u003cstring,\n    unknown>`, or `object`.\n\n## 5. Comments and Documentation\n\n-   **JSDoc:** Use `/** JSDoc */` for documentation, `//` for implementation\n    comments.\n-   **Redundancy:** **Do not declare types in `@param` or `@return` blocks**\n    (e.g., `/** @param {string} user */`). This is redundant in TypeScript.\n-   **Add Information:** Comments must add information, not just restate the\n    code.\n\n*Source:\n[Google TypeScript Style Guide](https://google.github.io/styleguide/tsguide.html)*\n"},{"source":"conductor/index.md","title":"Project Context","summary":"","category":"project","archived":false,"link":"library/conductor/index.html","text":"# Project Context\n\n## Definition\n\n- [Product Definition](./product.md)\n- [Product Guidelines](./product-guidelines.md)\n- [Tech Stack](./tech-stack.md)\n\n## Workflow\n\n- [Workflow](./workflow.md)\n- [Code Style Guides](./code_styleguides/)\n\n## Tracks\n\n- [Tracks Registry](./tracks.md)\n"},{"source":"conductor/product-guidelines.md","title":"Product Guidelines: HeLa MCP Ecosystem","summary":"Clarity & Precision: Concise, high-density technical communication without marketing fluff or speculative claims.","category":"project","archived":false,"link":"library/conductor/product-guidelines.html","text":"# Product Guidelines: HeLa MCP Ecosystem\n\n## Voice, Tone & Formatting\n\n- **Clarity & Precision**: Concise, high-density technical communication without marketing fluff or speculative claims.\n- **Uniform Formatting**: Professional GitHub-flavored Markdown. Zero decorative emojis across all documentation and output.\n- **Respectful Attribution**: Consistent recognition of Henrietta Lacks and HeLa cells as a biological cellular metaphor for modular systems.\n- **Transparent 4-Tier Naming**:\n  1. Public Identity (`HeLa \u003cComponent>`)\n  2. Machine Identifier (`hela-*`)\n  3. Technical Source Repository (`\u003crepo-name>`)\n  4. Immutable Implementation Revision (`\u003cgit-commit>`)\n\n## Technical & Quality Standards\n\n1. **Backbone Separation of Concerns**:\n   - HeLa Mitosis handles *execution planning, tool selection, and routing*.\n   - HeLa Genome handles *persistent state, memory tracking, and knowledge retrieval*.\n   - Capability servers remain independently executable and focused on single domains.\n2. **Deterministic Reproducibility**:\n   - All production profiles pin exact Git commit hashes.\n   - Setup and update scripts must be idempotent and fail fast on errors.\n3. **Context Window Protection**:\n   - Capability servers must bound output sizes using pagination, limits, and explicit truncation markers (`[TRUNCATED: showing 100/1500 lines]`).\n4. **Security-First Architecture**:\n   - Shell execution, path traversal, PTY processes, and browser boundaries must enforce strict input sanitization.\n   - API keys and credentials must never be logged, echoed, or stored in world-readable locations.\n5. **Zero-Key Resilience**:\n   - Every tool must operate gracefully when optional API keys are absent, falling back to local heuristics or bundled catalogs without process hangs.\n"},{"source":"conductor/product.md","title":"Product Definition: HeLa MCP Ecosystem","summary":"The HeLa MCP Ecosystem is a hardened, reproducible, 10-server Model Context Protocol (MCP) distribution suite. Built around a dual-backbone architecture, it couples HeLa…","category":"project","archived":false,"link":"library/conductor/product.html","text":"# Product Definition: HeLa MCP Ecosystem\n\n## Vision & Summary\n\nThe **HeLa MCP Ecosystem** is a hardened, reproducible, 10-server Model Context Protocol (MCP) distribution suite. Built around a dual-backbone architecture, it couples **HeLa Mitosis** (intelligent multi-tool orchestration, task decomposition, and routing) and **HeLa Genome** (persistent project memory and SQLite knowledge graphs) with 8 specialized capability MCPs spanning workspace management, research, browser automation, UI/UX design, Android control, and 3D modeling.\n\nThe ecosystem provides a profile-driven distribution layer with deterministic commit-based snapshot releases, automated diagnostic health checking, and native configuration generation across 8 major AI agent platforms.\n\n## Core Architectural Layers\n\n1. **Backbone Plane (The Core Duo)**:\n   - **HeLa Mitosis (`hela-mitosis` / `chaining-mcp-server`)**: Multi-tool discovery, route ranking, task decomposition, and sequential thinking workflows.\n   - **HeLa Genome (`hela-genome` / `Project-Guardian-mcp-server`)**: Living SQLite knowledge graph (`memory.db`), task tracking, architectural decision records, and session continuity.\n2. **Capability Plane (8 Specialized Servers)**:\n   - **HeLa Membrane (`hela-membrane` / `filesystem-mcp-server`)**: Workspace filesystem boundary and file operations.\n   - **HeLa Nucleus (`hela-nucleus` / `terminal-mcp-server`)**: System command execution and remote shell interaction.\n   - **HeLa Ribosome (`hela-ribosome` / `menager-mcp-server`)**: POSIX pseudo-terminal (PTY) process harness multiplexing.\n   - **HeLa Enzyme (`hela-enzyme` / `research-mcp-server`)**: Unified Google & Wikipedia research, analysis, and fact-checking.\n   - **HeLa Cytosol (`hela-cytosol` / `browser-agent`)**: Web browser automation, DOM interaction, and visual verification.\n   - **HeLa Phenotype (`hela-phenotype` / `the-designer`)**: UI/UX design tokens, Tailwind CSS, and component systems.\n   - **HeLa Receptor (`hela-receptor` / `scrcpy-mcp`)**: External Android mobile device control.\n   - **HeLa Plastid (`hela-plastid` / `ll3m-agent`)**: Autonomous 3D Blender modeling.\n\n## Key Objectives & Success Criteria\n\n1. **Strict 10-MCP Ceiling**: Exactly 10 MCP servers without architectural bloat or feature creep.\n2. **Deterministic Reproducibility**: Commit-based snapshot management (`config/snapshots/v1.0.0.json`) guaranteeing identical environments on Ubuntu, macOS, and WSL.\n3. **Automated Diagnostics**: Comprehensive `./setup.sh doctor` health command validating prerequisites, external binaries, and runtime permissions.\n4. **End-to-End Workflow Automation**: Multi-agent integration validated across Workflows A through F.\n5. **Zero-Key Graceful Degradation**: 100% operational offline fallback modes across all servers with zero crashes.\n"},{"source":"conductor/tech-stack.md","title":"Technology Stack: HeLa MCP Ecosystem","summary":"Runtime: Node.js (>= 18.0.0, recommended 20.x or 22.x LTS, ES Modules)","category":"project","archived":false,"link":"library/conductor/tech-stack.html","text":"# Technology Stack: HeLa MCP Ecosystem\n\n## Core Environment & Runtime\n\n- **Runtime**: Node.js (>= 18.0.0, recommended 20.x or 22.x LTS, ES Modules)\n- **Language**: TypeScript (server implementations) & Modern JavaScript (Node.js ESM)\n- **Scripting & Automation**: Bash (POSIX shell / `set -e`)\n- **Package Management**: npm (`package.json`, `package-lock.json`)\n\n## Protocols & Interfaces\n\n- **Communication Protocol**: Model Context Protocol (MCP) over stdio JSON-RPC\n- **Persistence & Knowledge Base**: SQLite3 (`memory.db`), JSON (`memory.json`)\n- **Client Configuration Targets**: JSON / TOML (Cursor, Claude, Antigravity/Gemini, OpenCode, Kilo, Zed, Codex, Docker)\n\n## External Runtime Drivers (Profile-Specific)\n\n- **Browser Automation**: Playwright / Chromium (HeLa Cytosol)\n- **Mobile Automation**: Android Debug Bridge (`adb`) & `scrcpy` (HeLa Receptor)\n- **3D Modeling & Rendering**: Blender CLI (`blender --version`) (HeLa Plastid)\n- **Process Orchestration**: POSIX pseudo-terminals / PTY (HeLa Ribosome)\n\n## Quality Assurance, CI & Formatting\n\n- **Linting & Hooks**: Pre-commit hooks (`.pre-commit-config.yaml`, `trailing-whitespace`, `end-of-file-fixer`, `check-yaml`)\n- **Continuous Integration**: GitHub Actions (`.github/workflows/ci.yml`)\n- **Testing**: Custom Node.js smoke test suites & cross-MCP integration tests (`test-smoke.js`, `test-integration.sh`)\n"},{"source":"conductor/tracks.md","title":"Tracks Registry","summary":"This document tracks all Conductor development tracks for the HeLa MCP Ecosystem.","category":"project","archived":false,"link":"library/conductor/tracks.html","text":"# Tracks Registry\n\nThis document tracks all Conductor development tracks for the HeLa MCP Ecosystem.\n\n---\n\n## Completed Tracks\n\n- [x] **[Track: Milestone 1 — Baseline Audit & Canonical HeLa Aliasing](./tracks/m1-baseline-audit/index.md)**\n  - Scope: Complete 10-MCP repository audit, canonical HeLa inventory update, agent profiles, and profile pre-validation engine.\n  - Status: Completed\n\n- [x] **[Track: Milestone 2 — Deterministic Snapshots & Doctor Command](./tracks/m2-determinism-doctor/index.md)**\n  - Scope: Ecosystem snapshot engine (`config/snapshots/v1.0.0.json`), deterministic setup, idempotency guards, and diagnostic health check (`./setup.sh doctor`).\n  - Status: Completed\n\n- [x] **[Track: Milestone 3 — Backbone Integration Layer & Workflows A–F](./tracks/m3-integration-workflows/index.md)**\n  - Scope: Backbone integration layer, output bounds, `test-integration.sh` suite, and Workflows A through F in `docs/workflows.md`.\n  - Status: Completed\n\n- [x] **[Track: Milestone 4 — Security Audit, Client Verification & CI Hardening](./tracks/m4-security-ci-clients/index.md)**\n  - Scope: Security & reliability audit (`docs/security-audit.md`), multi-client configuration matrix validator (70 combinations), and hardened GitHub Actions CI matrix.\n  - Status: Completed\n\n- [x] **[Track: Milestone 5 — Real Showcase Recordings, Documentation Site & Release](./tracks/m5-showcase-release/index.md)**\n  - Scope: Real workflow video showcases (YouTube), Asciinema terminal recordings, dedicated documentation landing website (`docs/site`), and v1.0.0 release finalization.\n  - Status: Completed\n"},{"source":"conductor/tracks/m1-baseline-audit/index.md","title":"Track: Milestone 1 — Baseline Audit & Canonical HeLa Aliasing","summary":"Scope: Complete 10-MCP repository audit, canonical HeLa inventory, agent profiles, and profile pre-validation engine.","category":"archive","archived":true,"link":"library/conductor/tracks/m1-baseline-audit/index.html","text":"# Track: Milestone 1 — Baseline Audit & Canonical HeLa Aliasing\n\n## Documents\n\n- [Specification](./spec.md)\n- [Implementation Plan](./plan.md)\n\n## Metadata\n\n- **Track ID:** `m1-baseline-audit`\n- **Status:** In Progress\n- **Scope:** Complete 10-MCP repository audit, canonical HeLa inventory, agent profiles, and profile pre-validation engine.\n"},{"source":"conductor/tracks/m1-baseline-audit/plan.md","title":"Implementation Plan: Milestone 1 — Baseline Audit & Canonical HeLa Aliasing","summary":"[x] Task: Audit GitHub remote states and record commit revisions for all 10 MCP servers","category":"archive","archived":true,"link":"library/conductor/tracks/m1-baseline-audit/plan.html","text":"# Implementation Plan: Milestone 1 — Baseline Audit & Canonical HeLa Aliasing\n\n## Phase 1: 10-MCP Remote Audit & Baseline Verification\n\n- [x] Task: Audit GitHub remote states and record commit revisions for all 10 MCP servers\n  - [x] Inspect remote repo, latest commit, entrypoint, build scripts for `chaining-mcp-server`\n  - [x] Inspect remote repo, latest commit, entrypoint, build scripts for `filesystem-mcp-server`\n  - [x] Inspect remote repo, latest commit, entrypoint, build scripts for `Project-Guardian`\n  - [x] Inspect remote repo, latest commit, entrypoint, build scripts for `terminal-mcp-server`\n  - [x] Inspect remote repo, latest commit, entrypoint, build scripts for `menager-mcp-server`\n  - [x] Inspect remote repo, latest commit, entrypoint, build scripts for `research-mcp-server`\n  - [x] Inspect remote repo, latest commit, entrypoint, build scripts for `the-designer`\n  - [x] Inspect remote repo, latest commit, entrypoint, build scripts for `browser-agent`\n  - [x] Inspect remote repo, latest commit, entrypoint, build scripts for `scrcpy-mcp`\n  - [x] Inspect remote repo, latest commit, entrypoint, build scripts for `ll3m-agent`\n- [x] Task: Run baseline smoke tests across all servers and record baseline status\n- [x] Task: Phase Verification & Checkpoint\n\n---\n\n## Phase 2: Canonical HeLa Inventory & Alias Translation Engine\n\n- [x] Task: Update `config/inventory.json` with canonical HeLa metadata and alias mapping\n  - [x] Add top-level HeLa ecosystem metadata\n  - [x] Structure all 10 servers under `hela-*` IDs with full taxonomy, role, and revision fields\n  - [x] Add alias lookup mapping for legacy source repository keys\n- [x] Task: Update `scripts/lib.sh` for dual-key resolution (HeLa ID and source name)\n- [x] Task: Update `scripts/generate-config.mjs` to render client configs with clean HeLa identifiers\n- [x] Task: Update `setup.sh` and `update.sh` to display canonical `HeLa \u003cComponent>` names\n- [x] Task: Phase Verification & Checkpoint\n\n---\n\n## Phase 3: Agent-Oriented Profiles & Pre-Validation\n\n- [x] Task: Update `config/profiles.json` to reference canonical `hela-*` server IDs\n  - [x] Verify both backbones (`hela-mitosis`, `hela-genome`) in all 7 profiles\n  - [x] Verify core headless vs. specialized GUI server allocations\n- [x] Task: Document agent personas and workflow pipelines in `docs/profiles.md`\n- [x] Task: Implement profile pre-validation engine in `scripts/lib.sh` / `setup.sh`\n- [x] Task: Phase Verification & Checkpoint\n\n---\n\n## Phase 4: Verification, Example Config Generation & CI Validation\n\n- [x] Task: Re-generate all checked-in example configs in `config/` with canonical HeLa configs\n- [x] Task: Run full 70-combination matrix tests (`generate-config.mjs`) across all profiles and backends\n- [x] Task: Run pre-commit hooks and verify 100% pass rate\n- [x] Task: Update Project Guardian memory graph and commit changes\n- [x] Task: Phase Verification & Checkpoint\n"},{"source":"conductor/tracks/m1-baseline-audit/spec.md","title":"Specification: Milestone 1 — Baseline Audit & Canonical HeLa Aliasing","summary":"Milestone 1 establishes the canonical foundation for the HeLa MCP Ecosystem. It audits the exact remote GitHub states of all 10 MCP servers, introduces the 4-tier naming model…","category":"archive","archived":true,"link":"library/conductor/tracks/m1-baseline-audit/spec.html","text":"# Specification: Milestone 1 — Baseline Audit & Canonical HeLa Aliasing\n\n## 1. Overview & Context\n\nMilestone 1 establishes the canonical foundation for the **HeLa MCP Ecosystem**. It audits the exact remote GitHub states of all 10 MCP servers, introduces the 4-tier naming model (Public Identity, Machine Identifier `hela-*`, Technical Source Repo, Implementation Commit Pin), builds transparent alias translation into inventory and config generators, refines agent-oriented profiles in `config/profiles.json`, and implements strict profile pre-validation.\n\n## 2. Functional Requirements\n\n1. **Remote Repository & Baseline Audit**:\n   - Audit all 10 MCP repositories against GitHub remotes (`Hela-Ecosystem/*`).\n   - Verify build commands, package entrypoints, runtime requirements, and environment variables.\n   - Record exact Git commit hashes as current baseline revisions.\n   - Run baseline test suite across all servers.\n2. **Canonical HeLa Inventory Schema (`config/inventory.json`)**:\n   - Add top-level ecosystem metadata (`project_name: \"HeLa MCP Ecosystem\"`, `total_servers: 10`).\n   - Define canonical `hela-*` identifiers for all 10 servers with fields: `id`, `alias`, `source`, `scope`, `role`, `targetRequirement`, `dir`, `repo`, `entry`, `build`, `runtime`, `env`, `revision`.\n   - Maintain full backward compatibility for legacy repository keys.\n3. **Core Tooling Alias Translation**:\n   - Update `scripts/lib.sh` to resolve server keys by both `hela-*` ID and source repository name.\n   - Update `scripts/generate-config.mjs` to render client configurations using clean HeLa names while executing underlying source entries.\n   - Update `setup.sh` and `update.sh` CLI output to display canonical `HeLa \u003cComponent>` names with underlying source attribution.\n4. **Agent-Oriented Profiles (`config/profiles.json` & `docs/profiles.md`)**:\n   - Update all 7 profiles in `config/profiles.json` to use canonical `hela-*` server IDs.\n   - Ensure every profile explicitly includes both backbone servers (`hela-mitosis` and `hela-genome`).\n   - Document agent persona, purpose, backbone, capabilities, and target requirements in `docs/profiles.md`.\n5. **Profile Pre-Validation Engine**:\n   - Implement pre-validation in `scripts/lib.sh` / `setup.sh` to check profile existence, inventory references, target compatibility, and binary prerequisites before modifying disk state.\n\n## 3. Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] All 10 MCP repositories audited and baseline commit hashes recorded.\n- [ ] `config/inventory.json` updated with canonical HeLa metadata and alias mapping.\n- [ ] `scripts/generate-config.mjs` successfully renders configs for both `hela-*` and legacy keys across all 10 backends.\n- [ ] `config/profiles.json` updated with canonical HeLa identifiers across all 7 profiles.\n- [ ] `docs/profiles.md` documents complete agent personas for all profiles.\n- [ ] Profile pre-validator correctly detects valid profiles and rejects invalid / missing dependencies.\n- [ ] GitHub Actions CI workflow and pre-commit hooks pass 100%.\n"},{"source":"conductor/tracks/m2-determinism-doctor/index.md","title":"Track: Milestone 2 — Deterministic Snapshots & Doctor Command","summary":"Scope: Ecosystem snapshot engine (config/snapshots/v1.0.0.json), deterministic setup, idempotency guards, and diagnostic health check (./setup.sh doctor).","category":"archive","archived":true,"link":"library/conductor/tracks/m2-determinism-doctor/index.html","text":"# Track: Milestone 2 — Deterministic Snapshots & Doctor Command\n\n## Documents\n\n- [Specification](./spec.md)\n- [Implementation Plan](./plan.md)\n\n## Metadata\n\n- **Track ID:** `m2-determinism-doctor`\n- **Status:** In Progress\n- **Scope:** Ecosystem snapshot engine (`config/snapshots/v1.0.0.json`), deterministic setup, idempotency guards, and diagnostic health check (`./setup.sh doctor`).\n"},{"source":"conductor/tracks/m2-determinism-doctor/plan.md","title":"Implementation Plan: Milestone 2 — Deterministic Snapshots & Doctor Command","summary":"[x] Task: Create config/snapshots/ directory and define snapshot schema","category":"archive","archived":true,"link":"library/conductor/tracks/m2-determinism-doctor/plan.html","text":"# Implementation Plan: Milestone 2 — Deterministic Snapshots & Doctor Command\n\n## Phase 1: Ecosystem Snapshot Management (`config/snapshots/`)\n\n- [x] Task: Create `config/snapshots/` directory and define snapshot schema\n- [x] Task: Generate `config/snapshots/v1.0.0.json` and `config/snapshots/latest-stable.json` with exact commit hashes from Milestone 1\n- [x] Task: Update `scripts/lib.sh` and `setup.sh` to support `--snapshot \u003ctag|file>` flag\n- [x] Task: Phase Verification & Checkpoint\n\n---\n\n## Phase 2: Deterministic & Idempotent Installation Engine\n\n- [x] Task: Update `setup_server` in `setup.sh` to checkout exact pinned commit hashes\n- [x] Task: Implement idempotency guard: verify commit hash and build artifact timestamps before triggering re-builds\n- [x] Task: Add `--dev` flag to track moving `main`/`master` branches for development\n- [x] Task: Implement rollback state tracking (`.rollback_state.json`) for safe updates and rollbacks\n- [x] Task: Phase Verification & Checkpoint\n\n---\n\n## Phase 3: Comprehensive Diagnostic Engine (`scripts/doctor.sh` & `./setup.sh doctor`)\n\n- [x] Task: Implement `scripts/doctor.sh` with host environment inspection (OS, Node.js >=18, Git, SQLite3)\n- [x] Task: Implement server component verification (commit pins, build artifacts, stdio startup smoke check)\n- [x] Task: Implement external runtime detectors (`playwright` for Cytosol, `adb` for Receptor, `blender` for Plastid, PTY for Ribosome)\n- [x] Task: Implement safe API key status reporting without credential logging\n- [x] Task: Integrate `doctor` command into `setup.sh` (`./setup.sh doctor [--profile \u003cid>]`)\n- [x] Task: Phase Verification & Checkpoint\n\n---\n\n## Phase 4: Verification, Doctor Testing & CI Matrix Validation\n\n- [x] Task: Run `./setup.sh doctor` and verify formatted output across various profiles\n- [x] Task: Test `--snapshot v1.0.0` installation and idempotency on the local environment\n- [x] Task: Run pre-commit hooks and update Project Guardian memory\n- [x] Task: Phase Verification & Checkpoint\n"},{"source":"conductor/tracks/m2-determinism-doctor/spec.md","title":"Specification: Milestone 2 — Deterministic Snapshots & Doctor Command","summary":"Milestone 2 introduces two core capabilities to the HeLa MCP Ecosystem:","category":"archive","archived":true,"link":"library/conductor/tracks/m2-determinism-doctor/spec.html","text":"# Specification: Milestone 2 — Deterministic Snapshots & Doctor Command\n\n## 1. Overview & Context\n\nMilestone 2 introduces two core capabilities to the **HeLa MCP Ecosystem**:\n1. **Ecosystem Snapshot Management & Deterministic Installation**: Immutable Git commit-pinned snapshot system (`config/snapshots/v1.0.0.json`) guaranteeing 100% reproducible environments across diverse machines with idempotent installation and rollback support.\n2. **Ecosystem Diagnostic Health Command (`setup.sh doctor` / `scripts/doctor.sh`)**: An automated system diagnostic tool that validates host prerequisites, Node.js version, SQLite3 CLI, server build artifacts, profile compatibility, external runtimes (`playwright`, `adb`, `blender`, PTY), and API key status without exposing credentials.\n\n## 2. Functional Requirements\n\n### 2.1 Ecosystem Snapshot Specification\n- Create `config/snapshots/` directory.\n- Create `config/snapshots/v1.0.0.json` and `config/snapshots/latest-stable.json` capturing:\n  - `snapshot_version`: `1.0.0`\n  - `release_date`: `2026-08-29`\n  - `ecosystem_commit`: Exact Git commit hash of `mcp-ecosystem`\n  - `servers`: Pinned Git commit hashes for all 10 HeLa MCP servers (`hela-mitosis`, `hela-genome`, `hela-membrane`, `hela-nucleus`, `hela-ribosome`, `hela-enzyme`, `hela-cytosol`, `hela-phenotype`, `hela-receptor`, `hela-plastid`).\n  - `verified_profiles`: Array of verified profile IDs.\n- Support `--snapshot \u003cfile|tag>` flag in `setup.sh` to install or verify against a specific snapshot.\n\n### 2.2 Deterministic & Idempotent Installation Engine\n- In `setup.sh` and `scripts/lib.sh`:\n  - When a commit revision is specified in `inventory.json` or snapshot, fetch and checkout the exact commit hash.\n  - If server directory is already at the target commit hash and built (`dist/index.js` or `build/index.js` exists and is newer than source files), reuse without re-building.\n  - Support `--dev` flag to track moving `main`/`master` branches for active development.\n  - Provide safe rollback: retain previous known commit hash on updates in `.rollback_state.json`.\n\n### 2.3 Comprehensive Diagnostic Health Command (`./setup.sh doctor` & `scripts/doctor.sh`)\n- Provide `./setup.sh doctor [--profile \u003cid>] [--json]` and `scripts/doctor.sh`:\n  - **Host System Diagnostics**:\n    - OS type, Architecture, Kernel version.\n    - Node.js (>= 18.0.0, recommend 20+ LTS).\n    - npm / pnpm version.\n    - Git version (>= 2.25.0).\n    - SQLite3 CLI availability & writable test.\n  - **Server Component Health Checks**:\n    - Directory existence & Git commit hash verification against pinned revision.\n    - Entrypoint and build artifact verification (`dist/index.js` or `build/index.js`).\n    - Permission checks.\n    - Stdio JSON-RPC startup smoke test (node subprocess start, transport check, terminate in \u003c500ms).\n  - **External Binary & Hardware Dependency Checks**:\n    - `hela-cytosol`: Playwright / Chromium browser binary check.\n    - `hela-receptor`: Android Debug Bridge (`adb`) binary & connected device detection.\n    - `hela-plastid`: Blender executable check (`blender --version`).\n    - `hela-ribosome`: POSIX pseudo-terminal (PTY) support.\n  - **API Key & Secret Status (Safe Diagnostics)**:\n    - `OPENROUTER_API_KEY`: Detected / Offline fallback mode active.\n    - `GITHUB_TOKEN`: Detected / Offline bundled catalog active.\n    - `GOOGLE_API_KEY` & `GOOGLE_CSE_ID`: Detected / Wikipedia offline fallback active.\n    - **Security Rule**: Never log or print full credentials.\n  - **Actionable Output**:\n    - Output structured, aligned table with `[READY]`, `[OPTIONAL MISSING]`, and `[ERROR - REQUIRED MISSING]`.\n    - Provide exact copy-paste fix commands for any missing dependencies.\n    - Exit code 0 if all required components pass, non-zero if required components fail.\n\n## 3. Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] `config/snapshots/v1.0.0.json` and `config/snapshots/latest-stable.json` created and validated.\n- [ ] `./setup.sh doctor` executes and produces clear, actionable diagnostics.\n- [ ] `./setup.sh --snapshot v1.0.0` installs exact pinned commit hashes.\n- [ ] Idempotent re-runs skip unnecessary builds when commits match and artifacts are valid.\n- [ ] Doctor exits with code 0 on healthy setup and non-zero on missing required dependencies.\n- [ ] Pre-commit checks and CI matrix pass 100%.\n"},{"source":"conductor/tracks/m3-integration-workflows/index.md","title":"Track: Milestone 3 — Backbone Integration Layer & Workflows A–F","summary":"Scope: Backbone integration layer, output bounds, test-integration.sh suite, and Workflows A through F in docs/workflows.md.","category":"archive","archived":true,"link":"library/conductor/tracks/m3-integration-workflows/index.html","text":"# Track: Milestone 3 — Backbone Integration Layer & Workflows A–F\n\n## Documents\n\n- [Specification](./spec.md)\n- [Implementation Plan](./plan.md)\n\n## Metadata\n\n- **Track ID:** `m3-integration-workflows`\n- **Status:** In Progress\n- **Scope:** Backbone integration layer, output bounds, `test-integration.sh` suite, and Workflows A through F in `docs/workflows.md`.\n"},{"source":"conductor/tracks/m3-integration-workflows/plan.md","title":"Implementation Plan: Milestone 3 — Backbone Integration Layer & Workflows A–F","summary":"[x] Task: Implement scripts/test-integration.mjs supporting JSON-RPC stdio handshake and tool discovery","category":"archive","archived":true,"link":"library/conductor/tracks/m3-integration-workflows/plan.html","text":"# Implementation Plan: Milestone 3 — Backbone Integration Layer & Workflows A–F\n\n## Phase 1: Integration Test Runner Specification & Implementation\n\n- [x] Task: Implement `scripts/test-integration.mjs` supporting JSON-RPC stdio handshake and tool discovery\n- [x] Task: Implement server-level handshake tests (`initialize`, `tools/list`, schema validation) across all 10 servers\n- [x] Task: Implement cross-MCP workflow simulation tests (Backbone Genome + Mitosis, Research Enzyme + Genome, Design Phenotype + Membrane)\n- [x] Task: Add bash launcher `scripts/test-integration.sh` and hook into package scripts\n- [x] Task: Phase Verification & Checkpoint\n\n---\n\n## Phase 2: Master Workflows Documentation (`docs/workflows.md`)\n\n- [x] Task: Document Architectural Integration Principles (Dual-Backbone orchestration model)\n- [x] Task: Document Workflow A: Autonomous Feature Engineering (Genome -> Mitosis -> Membrane -> Nucleus -> Genome)\n- [x] Task: Document Workflow B: Deep Research & Architecture Synthesis (Enzyme -> Cytosol -> Mitosis -> Genome -> Membrane)\n- [x] Task: Document Workflow C: Web Feature with Design & Browser Verification (Phenotype -> Membrane -> Nucleus -> Cytosol -> Genome)\n- [x] Task: Document Workflow D: Mobile App Test Automation (Genome -> Nucleus -> Receptor -> Genome)\n- [x] Task: Document Workflow E: Autonomous 3D Asset Creation & Render Pipeline (Genome -> Mitosis -> Plastid -> Membrane -> Genome)\n- [x] Task: Document Workflow F: Long-Horizon Complex Refactor with PTY Harness (Ribosome -> Nucleus -> Genome)\n- [x] Task: Phase Verification & Checkpoint\n\n---\n\n## Phase 3: Verification, Pre-Commit & Memory Graph Synchronization\n\n- [x] Task: Run `scripts/test-integration.sh` and verify 100% pass across active servers\n- [x] Task: Run pre-commit hooks across all files\n- [x] Task: Update Project Guardian memory and commit changes\n- [x] Task: Phase Verification & Checkpoint\n"},{"source":"conductor/tracks/m3-integration-workflows/spec.md","title":"Specification: Milestone 3 — Backbone Integration Layer & Workflows A–F","summary":"Milestone 3 solidifies the dual-backbone architecture of the HeLa MCP Ecosystem:","category":"archive","archived":true,"link":"library/conductor/tracks/m3-integration-workflows/spec.html","text":"# Specification: Milestone 3 — Backbone Integration Layer & Workflows A–F\n\n## 1. Overview & Context\n\nMilestone 3 solidifies the **dual-backbone architecture** of the HeLa MCP Ecosystem:\n- **HeLa Mitosis (`hela-mitosis`)**: Dynamic workflow routing, sequential reasoning, and tool chain orchestration.\n- **HeLa Genome (`hela-genome`)**: Entity-relation-observation knowledge graph, structured memory, and session context.\n\nThis track implements:\n1. End-to-end multi-server integration verification test suite (`scripts/test-integration.sh` / `scripts/test-integration.mjs`).\n2. Output bounds & context-window preservation enforcement.\n3. Master Workflows Guide (`docs/workflows.md`) covering canonical end-to-end workflows (Workflows A through F).\n\n## 2. Functional Requirements\n\n### 2.1 Automated Integration Test Suite (`scripts/test-integration.sh` / `scripts/test-integration.mjs`)\n- Implement a standalone Node.js integration test runner that:\n  - Spawns each server over stdio JSON-RPC transport (`initialize`, `tools/list`, `ping`).\n  - Tests Cross-MCP tool invocation and protocol conformance.\n  - Tests Workflow A simulated flow: `hela-genome` (entity creation) -> `hela-mitosis` (chaining plan) -> `hela-membrane` (file read/write simulation).\n  - Tests Workflow B simulated flow: `hela-enzyme` (Wikipedia search) -> `hela-genome` (record research observation).\n  - Tests Workflow C simulated flow: `hela-phenotype` (token generation) -> `hela-cytosol` (DOM inspection readiness).\n  - Emits TAP/structured output with execution time per test and exit code 0 on complete pass.\n\n### 2.2 Output Bounds & Context Window Protection\n- Ensure integration tests enforce output truncation guidelines (large outputs capped, summary fields provided) to safeguard LLM context windows against bloat.\n\n### 2.3 Comprehensive Workflows Documentation (`docs/workflows.md`)\n- Author detailed technical workflow specifications with step-by-step tool sequences, JSON schemas, persona mappings, and example prompts for:\n  - **Workflow A: Autonomous Feature Engineering** (`dev-workspace` / `headless-server`): Genome -> Mitosis -> Membrane -> Nucleus -> Genome.\n  - **Workflow B: Deep Research & Architecture Synthesis** (`research`): Enzyme -> Cytosol -> Mitosis -> Genome -> Membrane.\n  - **Workflow C: Web Feature with Design & Browser Verification** (`web-devops` / `dev-workspace`): Phenotype -> Membrane -> Nucleus -> Cytosol -> Genome.\n  - **Workflow D: Mobile App Test Automation** (`android-testing`): Genome -> Nucleus -> Receptor -> Genome.\n  - **Workflow E: Autonomous 3D Asset Creation** (`3d-modeling`): Genome -> Mitosis -> Plastid -> Membrane -> Genome.\n  - **Workflow F: Long-Horizon Complex Refactor with PTY Harness** (`dev-workspace` / `headless-server`): Ribosome -> Nucleus -> Genome.\n\n## 3. Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] `scripts/test-integration.mjs` and `scripts/test-integration.sh` implemented and passing all tests across active servers.\n- [ ] `docs/workflows.md` authored with complete sequence diagrams, prompts, and tool sequences for Workflows A–F.\n- [ ] Pre-commit checks and CI matrix pass 100%.\n"},{"source":"conductor/tracks/m4-security-ci-clients/index.md","title":"Track: Milestone 4 — Security Audit, Multi-Client Matrix & CI Hardening","summary":"Scope: Security & reliability audit (docs/security-audit.md), multi-client configuration matrix validator (70 combinations), and hardened GitHub Actions CI matrix.","category":"archive","archived":true,"link":"library/conductor/tracks/m4-security-ci-clients/index.html","text":"# Track: Milestone 4 — Security Audit, Multi-Client Matrix & CI Hardening\n\n## Documents\n\n- [Specification](./spec.md)\n- [Implementation Plan](./plan.md)\n\n## Metadata\n\n- **Track ID:** `m4-security-ci-clients`\n- **Status:** In Progress\n- **Scope:** Security & reliability audit (`docs/security-audit.md`), multi-client configuration matrix validator (70 combinations), and hardened GitHub Actions CI matrix.\n"},{"source":"conductor/tracks/m4-security-ci-clients/plan.md","title":"Implementation Plan: Milestone 4 — Security Audit, Multi-Client Matrix & CI Hardening","summary":"[x] Task: Author docs/security-audit.md covering path traversal, command execution, PTY cleanup, credential safety, and network isolation","category":"archive","archived":true,"link":"library/conductor/tracks/m4-security-ci-clients/plan.html","text":"# Implementation Plan: Milestone 4 — Security Audit, Multi-Client Matrix & CI Hardening\n\n## Phase 1: Security Audit & Reliability Assessment (`docs/security-audit.md`)\n\n- [x] Task: Author `docs/security-audit.md` covering path traversal, command execution, PTY cleanup, credential safety, and network isolation\n- [x] Task: Audit filesystem and terminal tool boundary behaviors\n- [x] Task: Phase Verification & Checkpoint\n\n---\n\n## Phase 2: Multi-Client Matrix Test Suite (`scripts/test-client-matrix.mjs`)\n\n- [x] Task: Implement `scripts/test-client-matrix.mjs` verifying all 10 client backends across 7 profiles (70 matrix combinations)\n- [x] Task: Implement parser validators for JSON, OpenCode, Zed, Codex TOML, and Docker Compose YAML\n- [x] Task: Add `scripts/test-client-matrix.sh` bash wrapper and register `npm run test:matrix` in `package.json`\n- [x] Task: Phase Verification & Checkpoint\n\n---\n\n## Phase 3: Hardened GitHub Actions CI Matrix (`.github/workflows/ci.yml`)\n\n- [x] Task: Create `.github/workflows/ci.yml` matrix covering Node 18, 20, 22 on Linux and macOS\n- [x] Task: Implement automated steps (doctor check, setup smoke, matrix tests, integration tests)\n- [x] Task: Phase Verification & Checkpoint\n\n---\n\n## Phase 4: Verification, Pre-Commit & Memory Graph Synchronization\n\n- [x] Task: Run `npm run test:matrix` and verify 70/70 passing matrix tests\n- [x] Task: Run `npm test` and `./setup.sh doctor`\n- [x] Task: Run pre-commit hooks across all files\n- [x] Task: Update Project Guardian memory and commit changes\n- [x] Task: Phase Verification & Checkpoint\n"},{"source":"conductor/tracks/m4-security-ci-clients/spec.md","title":"Specification: Milestone 4 — Security Audit, Multi-Client Matrix & CI Hardening","summary":"Milestone 4 hardens the HeLa MCP Ecosystem for production deployment across diverse developer environments and continuous integration pipelines:","category":"archive","archived":true,"link":"library/conductor/tracks/m4-security-ci-clients/spec.html","text":"# Specification: Milestone 4 — Security Audit, Multi-Client Matrix & CI Hardening\n\n## 1. Overview & Context\n\nMilestone 4 hardens the **HeLa MCP Ecosystem** for production deployment across diverse developer environments and continuous integration pipelines:\n1. **Security & Reliability Audit**: Comprehensive security audit document (`docs/security-audit.md`) establishing sandboxing guidelines, secret redaction, and terminal command isolation.\n2. **Multi-Client Configuration Test Suite (`scripts/test-client-matrix.mjs`)**: Automated verification for all 10 supported AI coding clients across all 7 profiles (70 matrix combinations).\n3. **Hardened GitHub Actions CI Pipeline (`.github/workflows/ci.yml`)**: End-to-end multi-platform, multi-Node CI matrix with automated doctor health checks and integration testing.\n\n## 2. Functional Requirements\n\n### 2.1 Security & Reliability Audit (`docs/security-audit.md`)\n- Author comprehensive security audit analyzing:\n  - **Path Traversal & Sandboxing**: `hela-membrane` (filesystem operations boundary enforcement).\n  - **Command Execution Safety**: `hela-nucleus` (command validation, safe shell execution).\n  - **PTY Session Lifecycle**: `hela-ribosome` (process group cleanup, SIGTERM/SIGKILL hierarchy, orphan prevention).\n  - **Zero-Exposure Credential Policy**: Redaction of API keys and tokens across stdout, logs, error dumps, and snapshot exports.\n  - **Network & SSRF Boundaries**: `hela-cytosol` (browser automation network filtering).\n\n### 2.2 Multi-Client Matrix Automated Validator (`scripts/test-client-matrix.mjs`)\n- Implement comprehensive client matrix test script:\n  - Tests all 10 client formatters (`cursor`, `claude`, `gemini`, `antigravity`, `opencode`, `kilo`, `zed`, `codex`, `docker`, `skip`) against all 7 profiles.\n  - Parses generated output formats:\n    - Standard JSON (`cursor`, `claude`, `gemini`, `antigravity`, `kilo`)\n    - Nested OpenCode format (`opencode`)\n    - Zed context servers schema (`zed`)\n    - TOML configuration format (`codex`)\n    - Docker Compose v3 format (`docker`)\n  - Asserts valid syntax, non-empty server blocks, correct command/args, and valid environment variables.\n\n### 2.3 Hardened GitHub Actions CI Pipeline (`.github/workflows/ci.yml`)\n- Create `.github/workflows/ci.yml` supporting:\n  - Matrix across Node.js versions `18.x`, `20.x`, `22.x` on `ubuntu-latest`.\n  - macOS platform verification (`macos-latest` on Node 20.x).\n  - Steps:\n    1. Checkout repository.\n    2. Set up Node.js with cache.\n    3. Run pre-commit style and yaml linters.\n    4. Verify snapshot consistency (`config/snapshots/v1.0.0.json`).\n    5. Run `./setup.sh doctor --json`.\n    6. Run `./setup.sh --profile dev-workspace --non-interactive --client cursor`.\n    7. Run multi-client matrix test suite (`npm run test:matrix`).\n    8. Run master integration test suite (`npm test`).\n\n## 3. Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] `docs/security-audit.md` authored with complete vulnerability analysis and mitigation standards.\n- [ ] `scripts/test-client-matrix.mjs` and `scripts/test-client-matrix.sh` pass 70/70 matrix combinations with 0 errors.\n- [ ] `.github/workflows/ci.yml` authored and verified locally.\n- [ ] Pre-commit hooks pass 100%.\n"},{"source":"conductor/tracks/m5-showcase-release/index.md","title":"Track: Milestone 5 — Real Showcase Recordings, Documentation Site & Release","summary":"","category":"archive","archived":true,"link":"library/conductor/tracks/m5-showcase-release/index.html","text":"# Track: Milestone 5 — Real Showcase Recordings, Documentation Site & Release\n\n- [Specification](./spec.md)\n- [Implementation Plan](./plan.md)\n- [Metadata](./metadata.json)\n"},{"source":"conductor/tracks/m5-showcase-release/plan.md","title":"Implementation Plan: Milestone 5 — Showcase Recordings, Documentation Landing Site & v1.0.0 Release","summary":"[x] Task: Record ecosystem diagnostics cast (./setup.sh doctor) via asciinema","category":"archive","archived":true,"link":"library/conductor/tracks/m5-showcase-release/plan.html","text":"# Implementation Plan: Milestone 5 — Showcase Recordings, Documentation Landing Site & v1.0.0 Release\n\n## Phase 1: Terminal Cast Recordings & Showcase Asset Preparation\n\n- [x] Task: Record ecosystem diagnostics cast (`./setup.sh doctor`) via asciinema\n- [x] Task: Record 70-combination matrix test cast (`npm run test:matrix`) via asciinema\n- [x] Task: Record master integration suite cast (`npm test`) via asciinema\n- [x] Task: Record deterministic snapshot installation cast (`./setup.sh`) via asciinema\n- [x] Task: Upload or bundle `.cast` recordings and organize showcase metadata (YouTube + Asciinema)\n- [x] Task: Phase Verification & Checkpoint\n\n---\n\n## Phase 2: Interactive Documentation Landing Site (`docs/site/`)\n\n- [x] Task: Scaffold modern responsive landing page structure in `docs/site/index.html`\n- [x] Task: Implement modern design tokens, typography, dark/light cellular aesthetic in `docs/site/style.css`\n- [x] Task: Build interactive Client & Profile Configurator in `docs/site/app.js` with live copyable configs\n- [x] Task: Build Video & Asciinema Showcase section embedding YouTube videos (`BnbDg1-be4g`, `O6nYKjmlaGk`) and terminal players\n- [x] Task: Build 10-MCP Cellular Taxonomy Directory with instant search and tool breakdown\n- [x] Task: Phase Verification & Checkpoint\n\n---\n\n## Phase 3: Release Documentation, Verification & Git Synchronization\n\n- [x] Task: Author `RELEASE_NOTES.md` capturing v1.0.0 milestone achievements and architecture\n- [x] Task: Verify landing page rendering and link integrity across all browsers\n- [x] Task: Run pre-commit hooks, verify matrix tests and integration suites\n- [x] Task: Update Project Guardian memory and Conductor registry\n- [x] Task: Commit, push to `development`, and merge to `master`\n- [x] Task: Phase Verification & Checkpoint\n"},{"source":"conductor/tracks/m5-showcase-release/spec.md","title":"Specification: Milestone 5 — Showcase Recordings, Documentation Landing Site & v1.0.0 Release","summary":"Milestone 5 delivers the public-facing showcase and interactive landing experience for the HeLa MCP Ecosystem. It integrates real recorded workflow demonstrations (YouTube…","category":"archive","archived":true,"link":"library/conductor/tracks/m5-showcase-release/spec.html","text":"# Specification: Milestone 5 — Showcase Recordings, Documentation Landing Site & v1.0.0 Release\n\n## Overview\nMilestone 5 delivers the public-facing showcase and interactive landing experience for the **HeLa MCP Ecosystem**. It integrates real recorded workflow demonstrations (YouTube video showcases and uploaded Asciinema terminal casts), creates a self-contained, responsive documentation landing site in `docs/site/`, and finalizes the release documentation.\n\n---\n\n## Functional Requirements\n\n### 1. Workflow Showcase Assets & Terminal Recordings\n* **External Video Showcases**:\n  * Integrate user's YouTube showcase recordings:\n    * `https://youtu.be/BnbDg1-be4g`\n    * `https://youtu.be/O6nYKjmlaGk`\n* **Asciinema Terminal Casts**:\n  * Record terminal sessions using `asciinema rec` covering:\n    1. **Ecosystem Health & Diagnostics**: `./setup.sh doctor` demonstrating system checks, entrypoint validation, and offline fallbacks.\n    2. **Multi-Client Matrix Verification**: `npm run test:matrix` validating 70 client x profile combinations in real-time.\n    3. **Master Integration Suite**: `npm test` verifying stdio JSON-RPC handshake across all 10 servers and 295 active tools.\n    4. **Deterministic Setup & Profile Engine**: `./setup.sh --profile dev-workspace --non-interactive` showing idempotent installation.\n  * Upload terminal casts via `asciinema upload` or embed lightweight JSON cast players.\n\n### 2. Dedicated Documentation Landing Site (`docs/site/`)\n* Create a modern, standalone static website in `docs/site/`:\n  * **Hero Section**: Cellular AI tooling narrative, Henrietta Lacks respectful tribute, and quick copy-paste install commands.\n  * **Dual-Backbone Architecture Explorer**: Visual interactive diagram of HeLa Mitosis (orchestrator) and HeLa Genome (memory) dispatching to the 8 capability cells.\n  * **Interactive Profile Configurator**: Interactive UI allowing users to select their target client (Cursor, Claude, Gemini, Antigravity, OpenCode, Kilo, Zed, Codex, Docker) and profile to generate live configuration snippets.\n  * **Video & Terminal Showcase Gallery**: Responsive grid embedding YouTube videos and Asciinema casts.\n  * **Component Directory**: Searchable reference of all 10 canonical HeLa components with tools, source repos, and fallback behaviors.\n  * **Zero External Build Step Dependency**: Pure HTML5, CSS (modern CSS grid, flexbox, custom properties), and vanilla JS that runs directly or via GitHub Pages without npm build overhead.\n\n### 3. Release Finalization\n* Update `docs/site` deployment instructions.\n* Prepare `RELEASE_NOTES.md` and update `conductor/tracks.md`.\n"},{"source":"conductor/workflow.md","title":"Project Workflow","summary":"The Plan is the Source of Truth: All work must be tracked in plan.md","category":"project","archived":false,"link":"library/conductor/workflow.html","text":"# Project Workflow\n\n## Guiding Principles\n\n1.  **The Plan is the Source of Truth:** All work must be tracked in `plan.md`\n2.  **The Tech Stack is Deliberate:** Changes to the tech stack must be\n    documented in `tech-stack.md` *before* implementation\n3.  **Test-Driven Development:** Write unit tests before implementing\n    functionality\n4.  **High Code Coverage:** Aim for >80% code coverage for all modules\n5.  **User Experience First:** Every decision should prioritize user experience\n6.  **Non-Interactive & CI-Aware:** Prefer non-interactive commands. Use\n    `CI=true` for watch-mode tools (tests, linters) to ensure single execution.\n\n## Task Workflow\n\nAll tasks follow a strict lifecycle:\n\n### Standard Task Workflow\n\n1.  **Select Task:** Choose the next available task from `plan.md` in sequential\n    order\n\n2.  **Mark In Progress:** Before beginning work, edit `plan.md` and change the\n    task from `[ ]` to `[~]`\n\n3.  **Write Failing Tests (Red Phase):**\n\n    -   Create a new test file for the feature or bug fix.\n    -   Write one or more unit tests that clearly define the expected behavior\n        and acceptance criteria for the task.\n    -   **CRITICAL:** Run the tests and confirm that they fail as expected. This\n        is the \"Red\" phase of TDD. Do not proceed until you have failing tests.\n\n4.  **Implement to Pass Tests (Green Phase):**\n\n    -   Write the minimum amount of application code necessary to make the\n        failing tests pass.\n    -   Run the test suite again and confirm that all tests now pass. This is\n        the \"Green\" phase.\n\n5.  **Refactor (Optional but Recommended):**\n\n    -   With the safety of passing tests, refactor the implementation code and\n        the test code to improve clarity, remove duplication, and enhance\n        performance without changing the external behavior.\n    -   Rerun tests to ensure they still pass after refactoring.\n\n6.  **Verify Coverage:** Run coverage reports using the project's chosen tools.\n    For example, in a Python project, this might look like: `bash pytest\n    --cov=app --cov-report=html` Target: >80% coverage for new code. The\n    specific tools and commands will vary by language and framework.\n\n7.  **Document Deviations:** If implementation differs from tech stack:\n\n    -   **STOP** implementation\n    -   Update `tech-stack.md` with new design\n    -   Add dated note explaining the change\n    -   Resume implementation\n\n8.  **Commit Code Changes:**\n\n    -   Stage all code changes related to the task.\n    -   Propose a clear, concise commit message e.g, `feat(ui): Create basic\n        HTML structure for calculator`.\n    -   Perform the commit.\n\n9.  **Attach Task Summary with Git Notes:**\n\n    -   **Step 9.1: Get Commit Hash:** Obtain the hash of the *just-completed\n        commit* (`git log -1 --format=\"%H\"`).\n    -   **Step 9.2: Draft Note Content:** Create a detailed summary for the\n        completed task. This should include the task name, a summary of changes,\n        a list of all created/modified files, and the core \"why\" for the change.\n    -   **Step 9.3: Attach Note:** Use the `git notes` command to attach the\n        summary to the commit. `bash # The note content from the previous step\n        is passed via the -m flag. git notes add -m \"\u003cnote content>\"\n        \u003ccommit_hash>`\n\n10. **Get and Record Task Commit SHA:**\n\n    -   **Step 10.1: Update Plan:** Read `plan.md`, find the line for the\n        completed task, update its status from `[~]` to `[x]`, and append the\n        first 7 characters of the *just-completed commit's* commit hash.\n    -   **Step 10.2: Write Plan:** Write the updated content back to `plan.md`.\n\n11. **Commit Plan Update:**\n\n    -   **Action:** Stage the modified `plan.md` file.\n    -   **Action:** Commit this change with a descriptive message (e.g.,\n        `conductor(plan): Mark task 'Create user model' as complete`).\n\n### Task Correction & Plan Amendment Workflows\n\nWhen an implemented task or phase requires corrections, amendments, or additions, follow these standard workflows to maintain plan integrity and avoid untracked code drift:\n\n1.  **In-Flight Refinements:** If minor gaps are found while a task is actively\n    in-progress (`[~]`), make the adjustments directly in the active\n    implementation stream and ensure passing tests before committing.\n2.  **Code Review Corrections (`conductor-review`):** If issues are identified\n    during or after a code review, instruct the agent to review your changes\n    (e.g., *\"run a review\"* or triggering the action manually in compatible\n    clients). The review agent will automatically append a `Review Fixes` phase\n    to `plan.md` so that correction tasks are formally tracked and\n    checkpointed.\n3.  **Logical State Reversions (`conductor-revert`):** If a task implementation\n    is fundamentally flawed or needs to be redone, instruct the agent to revert\n    the changes (e.g., *\"revert the last task\"* or triggering the action\n    manually in compatible clients). This safely rolls back associated git\n    commits and resets the task state in `plan.md` back to pending `[ ]` to\n    allow a clean restart.\n\n### Phase Completion Verification and Checkpointing Protocol\n\n**Trigger:** This protocol is executed immediately after a task is completed\nthat also concludes a phase in `plan.md`.\n\n1.  **Announce Protocol Start:** Inform the user that the phase is complete and\n    the verification and checkpointing protocol has begun.\n\n2.  **Ensure Test Coverage for Phase Changes:**\n\n    -   **Step 2.1: Determine Phase Scope:** To identify the files changed in\n        this phase, you must first find the starting point. Read `plan.md` to\n        find the Git commit SHA of the *previous* phase's checkpoint. If no\n        previous checkpoint exists, the scope is all changes since the first\n        commit.\n    -   **Step 2.2: List Changed Files:** Execute `git diff --name-only\n        \u003cprevious_checkpoint_sha> HEAD` to get a precise list of all files\n        modified during this phase.\n    -   **Step 2.3: Verify and Create Tests:** For each file in the list:\n        -   **CRITICAL:** First, check its extension. Exclude non-code files\n            (e.g., `.json`, `.md`, `.yaml`).\n        -   For each remaining code file, verify a corresponding test file\n            exists.\n        -   If a test file is missing, you **must** create one. Before writing\n            the test, **first, analyze other test files in the repository to\n            determine the correct naming convention and testing style.** The new\n            tests **must** validate the functionality described in this phase's\n            tasks (`plan.md`).\n\n3.  **Execute Automated Tests with Proactive Debugging:**\n\n    -   Before execution, you **must** announce the exact shell command you will\n        use to run the tests.\n    -   **Example Announcement:** \"I will now run the automated test suite to\n        verify the phase. **Command:** `CI=true npm test`\"\n    -   Execute the announced command.\n    -   If tests fail, you **must** inform the user and begin debugging. You may\n        attempt to propose a fix a **maximum of two times**. If the tests still\n        fail after your second proposed fix, you **must stop**, report the\n        persistent failure, and ask the user for guidance.\n\n4.  **Propose a Detailed, Actionable Manual Verification Plan:**\n\n    -   **CRITICAL:** To generate the plan, first analyze `product.md`,\n        `product-guidelines.md`, and `plan.md` to determine the user-facing\n        goals of the completed phase.\n    -   You **must** generate a step-by-step plan that walks the user through\n        the verification process, including any necessary commands and specific,\n        expected outcomes.\n    -   The plan you present to the user **must** follow this format:\n\n        **For a Frontend Change:** ``` The automated tests have passed. For\n        manual verification, please follow these steps:\n\n        **Manual Verification Steps:** 1. **Start the development server with\n        the command:** `npm run dev` 2. **Open your browser to:**\n        `http://localhost:3000` 3. **Confirm that you see:** The new user\n        profile page, with the user's name and email displayed correctly. ```\n\n        **For a Backend Change:** ``` The automated tests have passed. For\n        manual verification, please follow these steps:\n\n        **Manual Verification Steps:** 1. **Ensure the server is running.** 2.\n        **Execute the following command in your terminal:** `curl -X POST\n        http://localhost:8080/api/v1/users -d '{\"name\": \"test\"}'` 3. **Confirm\n        that you receive:** A JSON response with a status of `201 Created`. ```\n\n5.  **Await Explicit User Feedback:**\n\n    -   After presenting the detailed plan, ask the user for confirmation:\n        \"**Does this meet your expectations? Please confirm with yes or provide\n        feedback on what needs to be changed.**\"\n    -   **PAUSE** and await the user's response. Do not proceed without an\n        explicit yes or confirmation.\n\n6.  **Identify Target Commit for Report:**\n\n    -   Do NOT create a new empty commit for checkpointing.\n    -   Identify the hash of the last functional commit made during this phase. This will be the target for the verification report.\n\n7.  **Attach Auditable Verification Report using Git Notes:**\n\n    -   **Step 7.1: Draft Note Content:** Create a detailed verification report\n        including the automated test command, the manual verification steps, and\n        the user's confirmation.\n    -   **Step 7.2: Attach Note:** Use the `git notes` command to attach the full report to the target commit identified in step 6.\n\n8.  **Get and Record Phase Checkpoint SHA:**\n\n    -   **Step 8.1: Get Commit Hash:** Obtain the hash of the *just-created\n        checkpoint commit* (`git log -1 --format=\"%H\"`).\n    -   **Step 8.2: Update Plan:** Read `plan.md`, find the heading for the\n        completed phase, and append the first 7 characters of the commit hash in\n        the format `[checkpoint: \u003csha>]`.\n    -   **Step 8.3: Write Plan:** Write the updated content back to `plan.md`.\n\n9.  **Commit Plan Update:**\n\n    -   **Action:** Stage the modified `plan.md` file.\n    -   **Action:** Commit this change with a descriptive message following the\n        format `conductor(plan): Mark phase '\u003cPHASE NAME>' as complete`.\n\n10. **Announce Completion:** Inform the user that the phase is complete and the\n    checkpoint has been created, with the detailed verification report attached\n    as a git note.\n\n### Quality Gates\n\nBefore marking any task complete, verify:\n\n-   [ ] All tests pass\n-   [ ] Code coverage meets requirements (>80%)\n-   [ ] Code follows project's code style guidelines (as defined in\n    `code_styleguides/`)\n-   [ ] All public functions/methods are documented (e.g., docstrings, JSDoc,\n    GoDoc)\n-   [ ] Type safety is enforced (e.g., type hints, TypeScript types, Go types)\n-   [ ] No linting or static analysis errors (using the project's configured\n    tools)\n-   [ ] Works correctly on mobile (if applicable)\n-   [ ] Documentation updated if needed\n-   [ ] No security vulnerabilities introduced\n\n## Development Commands\n\n**AI AGENT INSTRUCTION: This section should be adapted to the project's specific\nlanguage, framework, and build tools.**\n\n### Setup\n\n```bash\n# Example: Commands to set up the development environment (e.g., install dependencies, configure database)\n# e.g., for a Node.js project: npm install\n# e.g., for a Go project: go mod tidy\n```\n\n### Daily Development\n\n```bash\n# Example: Commands for common daily tasks (e.g., start dev server, run tests, lint, format)\n# e.g., for a Node.js project: npm run dev, npm test, npm run lint\n# e.g., for a Go project: go run main.go, go test ./..., go fmt ./...\n```\n\n### Before Committing\n\n```bash\n# Example: Commands to run all pre-commit checks (e.g., format, lint, type check, run tests)\n# e.g., for a Node.js project: npm run check\n# e.g., for a Go project: make check (if a Makefile exists)\n```\n\n## Testing Requirements\n\n### Unit Testing\n\n-   Every module must have corresponding tests.\n-   Use appropriate test setup/teardown mechanisms (e.g., fixtures,\n    beforeEach/afterEach).\n-   Mock external dependencies.\n-   Test both success and failure cases.\n\n### Integration Testing\n\n-   Test complete user flows\n-   Verify database transactions\n-   Test authentication and authorization\n-   Check form submissions\n\n### Mobile Testing\n\n-   Test on actual iPhone when possible\n-   Use Safari developer tools\n-   Test touch interactions\n-   Verify responsive layouts\n-   Check performance on 3G/4G\n\n## Code Review Process\n\n### Self-Review Checklist\n\nBefore requesting review:\n\n1.  **Functionality**\n\n    -   Feature works as specified\n    -   Edge cases handled\n    -   Error messages are user-friendly\n\n2.  **Code Quality**\n\n    -   Follows style guide\n    -   DRY principle applied\n    -   Clear variable/function names\n    -   Appropriate comments\n\n3.  **Testing**\n\n    -   Unit tests comprehensive\n    -   Integration tests pass\n    -   Coverage adequate (>80%)\n\n4.  **Security**\n\n    -   No hardcoded secrets\n    -   Input validation present\n    -   SQL injection prevented\n    -   XSS protection in place\n\n5.  **Performance**\n\n    -   Database queries optimized\n    -   Images optimized\n    -   Caching implemented where needed\n\n6.  **Mobile Experience**\n\n    -   Touch targets adequate (44x44px)\n    -   Text readable without zooming\n    -   Performance acceptable on mobile\n    -   Interactions feel native\n\n## Commit Guidelines\n\n### Message Format\n\n```\n\u003ctype>(\u003cscope>): \u003cdescription>\n\n[optional body]\n\n[optional footer]\n```\n\n### Types\n\n-   `feat`: New feature\n-   `fix`: Bug fix\n-   `docs`: Documentation only\n-   `style`: Formatting, missing semicolons, etc.\n-   `refactor`: Code change that neither fixes a bug nor adds a feature\n-   `test`: Adding missing tests\n-   `chore`: Maintenance tasks\n\n### Examples\n\n```bash\ngit commit -m \"feat(auth): Add remember me functionality\"\ngit commit -m \"fix(posts): Correct excerpt generation for short posts\"\ngit commit -m \"test(comments): Add tests for emoji reaction limits\"\ngit commit -m \"style(mobile): Improve button touch targets\"\n```\n\n## Definition of Done\n\nA task is complete when:\n\n1.  All code implemented to specification\n2.  Unit tests written and passing\n3.  Code coverage meets project requirements\n4.  Documentation complete (if applicable)\n5.  Code passes all configured linting and static analysis checks\n6.  Works beautifully on mobile (if applicable)\n7.  Implementation notes added to `plan.md`\n8.  Changes committed with proper message\n9.  Git note with task summary attached to the commit\n\n## Emergency Procedures\n\n### Critical Bug in Production\n\n1.  Create hotfix branch from main\n2.  Write failing test for bug\n3.  Implement minimal fix\n4.  Test thoroughly including mobile\n5.  Deploy immediately\n6.  Document in plan.md\n\n### Data Loss\n\n1.  Stop all write operations\n2.  Restore from latest backup\n3.  Verify data integrity\n4.  Document incident\n5.  Update backup procedures\n\n### Security Breach\n\n1.  Rotate all secrets immediately\n2.  Review access logs\n3.  Patch vulnerability\n4.  Notify affected users (if any)\n5.  Document and update security procedures\n\n## Deployment Workflow\n\n### Pre-Deployment Checklist\n\n-   [ ] All tests passing\n-   [ ] Coverage >80%\n-   [ ] No linting errors\n-   [ ] Mobile testing complete\n-   [ ] Environment variables configured\n-   [ ] Database migrations ready\n-   [ ] Backup created\n\n### Deployment Steps\n\n1.  Merge feature branch to main\n2.  Tag release with version\n3.  Push to deployment service\n4.  Run database migrations\n5.  Verify deployment\n6.  Test critical paths\n7.  Monitor for errors\n\n### Post-Deployment\n\n1.  Monitor analytics\n2.  Check error logs\n3.  Gather user feedback\n4.  Plan next iteration\n\n## Continuous Improvement\n\n-   Review workflow weekly\n-   Update based on pain points\n-   Document lessons learned\n-   Optimize for user happiness\n-   Keep things simple and maintainable\n"},{"source":"docs/architecture.md","title":"HeLa MCP Ecosystem Architecture","summary":"This document provides a comprehensive architectural reference for the HeLa MCP Ecosystem, detailing its cellular design principles, dual-backbone topology, component…","category":"reference","archived":false,"link":"library/docs/architecture.html","text":"# HeLa MCP Ecosystem Architecture\n\nThis document provides a comprehensive architectural reference for the **HeLa MCP Ecosystem**, detailing its cellular design principles, dual-backbone topology, component taxonomies, and cross-MCP communication mechanisms.\n\nCurrent package versions and feature-dependent tool counts are generated in [current versions](current-versions.md). Each of the ten MCP cells has its own version; the ecosystem snapshot and optional runtime have independent versions.\n\n![Blotcat acting as a conductor in front of a modular system of interconnected server blocks](../assets/blotcat-architecture.jpg)\n\n---\n\n## 1. Architectural Philosophy: The Cellular Metaphor\n\nThe **HeLa MCP Ecosystem** is architected around a cellular biology metaphor, respectfully recognizing **Henrietta Lacks** (1920–1951) and the immortal legacy of HeLa cells in biomedical research:\n\n1. **Cellular Specialization**: In biological systems, organelles perform distinct biochemical functions while cooperating as a unified organism. In the HeLa ecosystem, servers act as specialized cellular components (Nucleus, Membrane, Enzyme, Ribosome, Plastid) coordinated by a dual backbone.\n2. **Reproducible source selection**: Commit-pinned snapshots under `config/snapshots/` select known revisions. Platform dependencies and external integrations still require separate validation.\n3. **Repository Autonomy**: Underlying repositories, Git histories, and package dependencies remain completely autonomous and uncoupled.\n\n---\n\n## 2. The 4-Tier Naming Architecture\n\nTo balance brand clarity, backwards compatibility, and technical provenance, the ecosystem enforces a 4-tier naming model:\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n    T1[\"Tier 1: Public Identity\u003cbr/>HeLa Genome\"]\n    T2[\"Tier 2: Machine ID\u003cbr/>hela-genome\"]\n    T3[\"Tier 3: Technical Repo\u003cbr/>project-mcp\"]\n    T4[\"Tier 4: Immutable Commit\u003cbr/>72bca15ebe...\"]\n\n    T1 --> T2 --> T3 --> T4\n```\n\n1. **Tier 1 (Public Identity)**: Clean, uniform human-facing persona name (e.g. *HeLa Mitosis*, *HeLa Genome*, *HeLa Membrane*).\n2. **Tier 2 (Machine Identifier)**: Stable hyphenated identifier (`hela-*`) used in configuration files (`mcpServers`), CLI flags, and profile definitions.\n3. **Tier 3 (Technical Source Repo)**: Independent Git repository name on GitHub maintaining upstream authorship and commit history.\n4. **Tier 4 (Immutable Commit Pin)**: Exact SHA-1 hash recorded in `config/snapshots/` ensuring reproducible builds.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Dual-Backbone Coordination Topology\n\nEvery deployed profile in the ecosystem is grounded by a dual backbone:\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TB\n    Client[\"AI Client / Orchestrator\"]\n\n    Mitosis[\"HeLa Mitosis\u003cbr/>(hela-mitosis)\u003cbr/>- Dynamic Routing\u003cbr/>- Step Reasoning\u003cbr/>- Task Decomposition\u003cbr/>- Prompt Catalog\"]\n    Genome[\"HeLa Genome\u003cbr/>(hela-genome)\u003cbr/>- Knowledge Graph\u003cbr/>- Living SQLite DB\u003cbr/>- Entity / Relations\u003cbr/>- Milestones & State\"]\n\n    Membrane[\"HeLa Membrane\u003cbr/>(Files)\"]\n    Nucleus[\"HeLa Nucleus\u003cbr/>(Exec)\"]\n    Ribosome[\"HeLa Ribosome\u003cbr/>(PTY)\"]\n    Enzyme[\"HeLa Enzyme\u003cbr/>(Search)\"]\n    Cytosol[\"HeLa Cytosol\u003cbr/>(Browser)\"]\n    Phenotype[\"HeLa Phenotype\u003cbr/>(Design)\"]\n\n    Client -- \"stdio JSON-RPC\" --> Mitosis & Genome\n    Mitosis \u003c-->|\"Shared Memory\"| Genome\n    Mitosis & Genome --> Membrane & Nucleus & Ribosome & Enzyme & Cytosol & Phenotype\n```\n\n* **HeLa Mitosis (Routing & Planning Backbone)**: Bundled Needle 3 agent runtime (plan → execute → observe with recorded AgentState), suggests multi-step execution paths, Needle-backed sequential analysis, and serves 40 curated domain prompts. Offline-first; OpenRouter is an escalation backend only.\n* **HeLa Genome (State & Memory Backbone)**: Maintains the living SQLite knowledge graph (`memory.db`), restores cross-session context, records task states, and manages project milestones.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Component Taxonomy\n\n### 4.1 Core Backbone Servers\n\n#### 1. HeLa Mitosis (`hela-mitosis` / `chaining-mcp`)\n* **Role**: Orchestration, dynamic routing, and Needle-backed sequential reasoning.\n* **Entrypoint**: `dist/index.js`\n* **Tools**: `agent_run`, `sequentialthinking`, `analyze_with_sequential_thinking`, `analyze_tools`, `generate_route_suggestions`, `llm_decompose_task`, `llm_suggest_route`, `workflow_orchestrator`, `workflow_status`, `workflow_cancel`, `list_skills`, `search_skills`, `get_skill`, `suggest_skill_chain`, plus prompt/resource, time, and validation tools.\n* **Zero-Key Behavior**: The bundled Needle 3 agent runs fully offline. Without `OPENROUTER_API_KEY`, escalation hands control back to the calling agent (`handoff: true`) with the full state trail — never a fabricated answer. `brainstorming` is the one exception: it needs a generative model and fails honestly without a key.\n\n#### 2. HeLa Genome (`hela-genome` / `project-mcp`)\n* **Role**: Living memory graph, entity-relation-observation state tracking.\n* **Entrypoint**: `dist/index.js`\n* **Tools**: `memory_edit` (entity/relation/observation CRUD), `read_graph`, `search_nodes`, `get_session_context`, `sync_central_memory`, `security_scan`, and the three mission tools `create_mission`, `list_missions`, `update_mission`.\n* **Storage**: Embedded SQLite database (`memory.db`) with FTS5 full-text indexing.\n\n---\n\n### 4.2 Core Capability Servers\n\n#### 3. HeLa Membrane (`hela-membrane` / `filesystem-mcp`)\n* **Role**: Sandboxed workspace filesystem operations and search.\n* **Entrypoint**: `dist/index.js`\n* **Tools**: `read_file`, `write_file`, `find_files`, `search_in_files`, `copy_file`, `move_file`, `delete_file`, `archive_files`.\n\n#### 4. HeLa Nucleus (`hela-nucleus` / `terminal-mcp`)\n* **Role**: Isolated command execution and RTK token optimization.\n* **Entrypoint**: `build/index.js`\n* **Tools**: `execute_command`, `transfer_file`, `terminal_ls`, `terminal_grep`, `terminal_cat`, `terminal_session_close`, `terminal_session_list`.\n\n#### 5. HeLa Ribosome (`hela-ribosome` / `menager-mcp`)\n* **Role**: Polyglot interactive PTY session multiplexing and Regex hook engine.\n* **Entrypoint**: `build/index.js`\n* **Tools**: `session_spawn`, `session_write`, `session_read`, `session_hook`, `session_close`, `session_list`, `session_prune`.\n\n#### 6. HeLa Enzyme (`hela-enzyme` / `researcher-mcp`)\n* **Role**: Unified research platform: zero-key dual-tier web search + arXiv + Google News RSS + Wikipedia caching.\n* **Entrypoint**: `dist/index.js`\n* **Tools**: `google_search`, `wikipedia_search`, `wikipedia_get`, `content_summarizer`, `fact_checker`, `keyword_extraction`.\n\n#### 7. HeLa Cytosol (`hela-cytosol` / `browser-mcp`)\n* **Role**: Playwright-based browser automation and DOM accessibility perception.\n* **Entrypoint**: `src/server.js`\n* **Tools**: `browser_navigate`, `browser_screenshot`, `browser_click`, `browser_type`, `browser_get_content`.\n\n---\n\n### 4.3 Specialized Capability Servers\n\n#### 8. HeLa Phenotype (`hela-phenotype` / `designer-mcp`)\n* **Role**: UI/UX design tokens, OKLCH palettes, and Tailwind component synthesis.\n* **Entrypoint**: `dist/index.js`\n* **Tools**: `design_tokens`, `palette`, `design_audit`, `generate_template`, `logo_workflow`.\n\n#### 9. HeLa Receptor (`hela-receptor` / `scrcpy-mcp`)\n* **Role**: Android mobile device control via ADB bridge.\n* **Entrypoint**: `dist/server.js`\n* **Tools**: `start_session`, `ui_dump`, `ui_find_element`, `tap`, `swipe`, `input_text`, `screenshot`.\n\n#### 10. HeLa Plastid (`hela-plastid` / `ll3m-mcp`)\n* **Role**: Autonomous 3D procedural modeling and rendering in Blender.\n* **Entrypoint**: `dist/index.js` (under `brain/`)\n* **Tools**: `generate_modeling_plan`, `execute_blender_code`, `render_output`, `save_blend`, `get_fast_feedback`.\n\n---\n\n### 4.4 Canonical Tool Boundaries (v1.2.0)\n\nSeveral organelles expose overlapping capabilities. Descriptions and the Mitosis capability router now enforce a single canonical choice per intent, keeping a restricted alternative only for its niche:\n\n| Intent | Canonical | Restricted alternative | Restricted use |\n|--------|-----------|------------------------|----------------|\n| Web search | `hela-enzyme:google_search` | `hela-cytosol:browser_search` | Visual browser workflows only |\n| Content extraction | `hela-enzyme:extract_content` | `hela-cytosol:browser_get_content` | Pages requiring client-side JavaScript |\n| Local file read | `hela-membrane:read_file` | `hela-nucleus:terminal_cat` | Remote SSH targets only (`host` required) |\n| Memory-graph inspection | `hela-genome:execute_sql` / `query_data` | `hela-ribosome:opencode_db` | OpenCode session database only |\n\nTool names are unique ecosystem-wide after the Phase 1 rename, so the router matches boundaries by tool name without needing a server field.\n\n---\n\n### 4.5 HeLa Runtime (optional daemon, `hela-runtime`)\n\nThe optional runtime supervises child MCP processes behind one stdio or Streamable HTTP endpoint. Its generated catalog exposes selected-profile schemas without starting cells. The first invocation starts only its target; concurrent first calls share startup. Unknown tool names do not trigger an all-cell scan.\n\n- **Source:** https://github.com/Hela-Ecosystem/hela-runtime\n- **Entry:** `hela-runtime/dist/index.js`\n- **Serve mode:** add `--runtime` to any profile, for example `./setup.sh --profile dev-workspace --client opencode --runtime`. Runtime is not a profile; the default profile remains `dev-workspace`.\n- **Env:** `HELA_ECOSYSTEM_ROOT`, `HELA_PROFILE`, `HELA_HTTP_PORT`, `HELA_NAMESPACE_TOOLS`.\n- **Features:** true on-demand ignition for every selected cell, fingerprinted schema caches, bounded crash/timeout recovery, opt-in namespacing, runtime-internal `hela.pipe`, and HTTP `/healthz` liveness.\n- **Without it:** users keep the individual servers selected by their profile and can use `hela-mitosis:workflow_orchestrator` for cross-cell chaining.\n- **Details:** [on-demand startup, cache invalidation and deployment evidence](on-demand-runtime.md).\n\n## 5. Security & Isolation Model\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TB\n    Host[\"Host Operating System / Terminal Environment\"]\n    Root[\"HeLa MCP Ecosystem Root\"]\n    Sandbox[\"stdio JSON-RPC Sandbox\"]\n    Membrane2[\"Path Canonicalization\u003cbr/>(HeLa Membrane)\"]\n    Nucleus2[\"Explicit CWD & Timeout Limits\u003cbr/>(HeLa Nucleus)\"]\n    Ribosome2[\"Process Group Teardown\u003cbr/>(HeLa Ribosome)\"]\n    Cytosol2[\"SSRF IP Filtering\u003cbr/>(HeLa Cytosol)\"]\n\n    Host --> Root\n    Root --> Sandbox & Membrane2 & Nucleus2 & Ribosome2 & Cytosol2\n```\n\n1. **Process Boundary**: Each server runs in an isolated Node.js child process communicating exclusively over standard input/output (`stdio`) via JSON-RPC 2.0 messages.\n2. **Memory Safety**: SQLite database operations in `hela-genome` use parameterized queries with WAL (Write-Ahead Logging) mode.\n3. **Credential handling**: Doctor reports presence, not values. Generated client configurations can contain explicitly configured credentials; treat those files and generator output as private. Process separation is not a security sandbox, and remote endpoint authentication/authorization remain explicitly deferred; see [security status](security-audit.md).\n\n---\n\n## 6. Profile System & Lifecycle Management\n\nThe ecosystem configuration generator (`scripts/generate-config.mjs`) reads:\n1. **`config/inventory.json`**: Authoritative component catalog, entrypoints, Git repositories, and alias mappings.\n2. **`config/profiles.json`**: Curated stacks tailored to specific agent personas and hardware targets.\n\nThe installer matches the selected profile against the inventory and chosen snapshot, then renders client syntax: `mcpServers` JSON, OpenCode/Kilo `mcp`, Zed `context_servers`, or Codex `[mcp_servers.*]` TOML. Runtime installation also refreshes selected-profile schema catalogs. All eight profiles are supported; lappy's full ten-cell roster and servy's eight-cell roster are installation choices, not code forks.\n"},{"source":"docs/beginner-guide.md","title":"Beginner's Guide: HeLa MCP Ecosystem","summary":"HeLa connects an AI client to tools for memory, files, commands, research, design, browsers, Android, and Blender. Install only the profile you need. Tools can change files and…","category":"gettingStarted","archived":false,"link":"library/docs/beginner-guide.html","text":"# Beginner's Guide: HeLa MCP Ecosystem\n\n![Blotcat beginner setup](../assets/blotcat-beginner.jpg)\n\nHeLa connects an AI client to tools for memory, files, commands, research, design, browsers, Android, and Blender. Install only the profile you need. Tools can change files and run commands with the server account's privileges; review your client's permissions before use.\n\nCurrent software versions are listed in [current versions](current-versions.md). Setup time depends on dependencies and downloads; browser and hardware workflows need extra software.\n\n## Requirements\n\n- Node.js **22 LTS** is the tested full-stack environment. Enzyme requires at least 20.18.1; Receptor requires 22. Individual packages can declare different standalone engine ranges.\n- Git and a terminal. Use a POSIX shell on Linux/macOS or WSL on Windows for `setup.sh`.\n- Optional capabilities: Chromium/Playwright for browser work, ADB and a device for Android, and the Blender bridge for 3D.\n\n## Install a profile\n\n```bash\ngit clone https://github.com/Hela-Ecosystem/hela-mcp-ecosystem.git\ncd hela-mcp-ecosystem\n./setup.sh --profile dev-workspace --client opencode --non-interactive\n```\n\nChange the client to `cursor`, `claude`, `gemini`, `antigravity`, `kilo`, `zed`, or `codex` as appropriate. The installer prints its managed prefix and a private client fragment: review and merge that fragment into your client settings. It does not overwrite existing client files or reuse your working repositories. Run `./setup.sh` without flags for interactive profile/mode/client selection.\n\nAll eight profiles are supported:\n\n- `minimal`: three research/memory cells.\n- `dev-workspace`: eight development/browser/design cells; runtime default.\n- `headless-server`: seven cells without browser/device dependencies.\n- `research`: five research/browser/workspace cells.\n- `web-devops`: six frontend/browser/design cells.\n- `android-testing`: five cells including device control.\n- `3d-modeling`: five cells including Blender.\n- `all`: all ten cells.\n\nSee [profiles](profiles.md) for membership. Your selection is independent of other machines: lappy's ten-cell roster and servy's eight-cell roster are examples, not required configurations for everyone.\n\n## One connection or separate servers\n\nThe installer normally configures separate MCP servers. Add `--runtime` to keep the same profile behind one endpoint:\n\n```bash\n./setup.sh --profile dev-workspace --client opencode --runtime --non-interactive\n```\n\nRuntime is a serve mode, not a profile named `runtime`. Its catalog advertises schemas without starting cells; a first call starts only its target. [Runtime details](on-demand-runtime.md) explain catalog generation, missing metadata, and recovery.\n\nOther mechanisms are `--mode docker` (separate containers), `--mode docker-runtime` (one runtime container), and `--mode nix` (pinned toolchain and packaged runtime). Runtime modes also support `--transport http`. See [installation modes](installation.md) for exact requirements and commands; Docker needs a working daemon, and HTTP listeners are started explicitly.\n\n## Optional keys\n\nOpenRouter, GitHub, and Google keys are optional for discovery and keyless capabilities. Keyless does **not** mean every operation is offline: web research and external design services still need network access, and generative-model features require their configured provider or local engine. See [keys and services](keys-and-secrets.md).\n\nGenerated configuration can contain credentials you explicitly provide. Keep it private, and do not paste the complete file into a chat or issue.\n\n## Verify the selected installation\n\n```bash\n./setup.sh doctor --profile dev-workspace\n# If installed through the runtime:\n./setup.sh doctor --profile dev-workspace --runtime\nnpm run test:matrix\n```\n\nDoctor checks selected paths, versions, revisions, and actual isolated MCP replies. Runtime checking also verifies catalog parity and zero discovery startups. The configuration matrix checks 80 profile/client combinations; it does not log in to GUI agents or exercise hardware.\n\nFor an explicit prefix, use `./setup.sh doctor --prefix /path/to/prefix`. For Docker or Nix defaults, select the same `--mode` and profile as installation. `doctor --url http://127.0.0.1:8080/mcp` checks a running HTTP endpoint; it does not inspect remote source revisions.\n\nOn a full ten-cell checkout you can also run `npm test` and `npm run test:profiles`. On a partial checkout use `npm run test:profiles -- --profile \u003cid>`. Missing unselected hardware repositories are not a reason to change your machine's profile.\n\nReload the relevant client deliberately after reviewing configuration changes. Some clients, including Zed, can auto-reload servers when settings are saved. Do not change live vault hooks or restart unrelated clients merely to silence a warning. See [integration](integration.md) and [troubleshooting](troubleshooting.md).\n"},{"source":"docs/current-versions.md","title":"Current versions and tool surfaces","summary":"Generated from package metadata, inventory, and the schema catalog. Ecosystem 1.5.3; runtime 1.2.0.","category":"reference","archived":false,"link":"library/docs/current-versions.html","text":"# Current versions and tool surfaces\n\nGenerated from package metadata, inventory, and the schema catalog. Ecosystem **1.5.3**; runtime **1.2.0**.\n\n- **HeLa Mitosis** (`hela-mitosis`): server **3.0.0**, 24–29 canonical tools; [source](https://github.com/Hela-Ecosystem/chaining-mcp).\n- **HeLa Genome** (`hela-genome`): server **3.0.0**, 29 canonical tools; [source](https://github.com/Hela-Ecosystem/project-mcp).\n- **HeLa Membrane** (`hela-membrane`): server **3.0.0**, 16 canonical tools; [source](https://github.com/Hela-Ecosystem/filesystem-mcp).\n- **HeLa Nucleus** (`hela-nucleus`): server **3.0.0**, 7 canonical tools; [source](https://github.com/Hela-Ecosystem/terminal-mcp).\n- **HeLa Ribosome** (`hela-ribosome`): server **3.0.0**, 28 canonical tools; [source](https://github.com/Hela-Ecosystem/menager-mcp).\n- **HeLa Enzyme** (`hela-enzyme`): server **3.0.0**, 24 canonical tools; [source](https://github.com/Hela-Ecosystem/researcher-mcp).\n- **HeLa Cytosol** (`hela-cytosol`): server **3.0.0**, 73 canonical tools; [source](https://github.com/Hela-Ecosystem/browser-mcp).\n- **HeLa Phenotype** (`hela-phenotype`): server **3.0.0**, 28 canonical tools; [source](https://github.com/Hela-Ecosystem/designer-mcp).\n- **HeLa Receptor** (`hela-receptor`): server **3.0.0**, 46 canonical tools; [source](https://github.com/Hela-Ecosystem/scrcpy-mcp).\n- **HeLa Plastid** (`hela-plastid`): server **3.0.0**, 15 canonical tools; [source](https://github.com/Hela-Ecosystem/ll3m-mcp).\n\nCounts vary with declared features. Runtime adds `hela.pipe` and an agent alias when available. Profiles choose eligible cells, not an eager startup count. Historical release notes/snapshots keep their original versions.\n"},{"source":"docs/deployment-2026-11-06.md","title":"Servy deployment verification — 2026-11-06","summary":"This records the earlier runtime 1.1.0 rollout. The subsequent runtime 1.2.0 on-demand deployment supersedes its process/startup state; the evidence below remains historical.","category":"archive","archived":true,"link":"library/docs/deployment-2026-11-06.html","text":"# Servy deployment verification — 2026-11-06\n\nThis records the earlier runtime 1.1.0 rollout. The subsequent [runtime 1.2.0 on-demand deployment](on-demand-runtime.md#verified-servy-rollout) supersedes its process/startup state; the evidence below remains historical.\n\nThe v1.4.0 source release (`c9faa9b`) is deployed to servy's systemd-managed HTTP runtime. This record covers that deployment, not authenticated GUI sessions or live vault reconciliation.\n\n## Published source and builds\n\n- Genome: `16fc4f31735e139d690ae0ace8d4feaf1a603563`.\n- Enzyme: `5399e33c7c8a5b75ad620dc33f66a1cbf49972f7`.\n- Runtime: `797bad451b45d0d67631eeb8cdb1c93d259f614c`, version **1.1.0**.\n- Remaining cell pins are in `config/snapshots/v1.4.0.json`. Historical v1.0.0–v1.3.0 snapshots were not modified.\n\nRuntime recovery passes **20/20** isolated fault tests on x86_64-linux and native aarch64-linux; all 29 fixture PIDs per run were reaped. Coverage includes hung RPCs, SIGTERM/SIGKILL, exit-before-restart, pipe routing, bounded retries, profile boundaries, and shutdown cleanup. No live service cell was faulted during deployment.\n\nFinal-source Nix builds use the committed lockfile, fixed-output npm dependencies, production installation, and Node **22.23.4**:\n\n- x86_64-linux: `/nix/store/gzxl62xr29nh36yb75c242gp4mnsibsh-hela-runtime-1.1.0`.\n- aarch64-linux: `/nix/store/y9hqz9kyx860s5xqx3gk23x7acsghis2-hela-runtime-1.1.0`.\n\nGenome's clean-install regression suite passes **19 suites / 242 tests**. Enzyme's **79 tests** and Phenotype's **74 tests** pass. Ten isolated standalone MCP handshakes, 80 client-configuration combinations, three probe regressions, and three isolated direct/runtime read-only parity calls pass. These checks do not establish authenticated GUI or hardware integration.\n\n## Controlled promotion\n\nAll eight selected cells, runtime, and ecosystem checkouts were clean before fast-forward promotion. Validated staged builds were promoted; Genome, Ribosome, Enzyme, and Phenotype received fresh ARM dependency trees. Old build/dependency directories remain in the rollout's `rollback/` directory. All ten deployed checkouts passed `npm ls --depth=0`; source revisions agreed with lappy at source-release verification.\n\nBefore stopping the service, three online SQLite backups passed integrity checks and the live vault was archived again. Earlier backups were hash-verified. No active database file was replaced or restored.\n\nOnly `hela-runtime.service` was stopped/restarted. Its old ten processes, including a duplicate Genome child, exited. The new service owns one runtime plus exactly eight direct child cells; all use Nix Node 22.23.4. At verification, systemd reported active state, zero automatic restarts, and successful exit status.\n\nThe deployed package is rooted by:\n\n`/home/ubuntu/.local/state/hela/deployments/todo102-z65w9kzr/runtime`\n\nThe drop-in `/etc/systemd/system/hela-runtime.service.d/10-nix-package.conf` selects that executable and prepends pinned Node to PATH. The original unit remains intact. Existing profile, vault setting, host, port, and endpoint-security configuration were not changed.\n\n## Discovery and ownership\n\n- Default profile: **dev-workspace**, eight cells; no Receptor or Plastid deployment on servy.\n- Local `/healthz`: HTTP 200, runtime **1.1.0**.\n- Local and public MCP initialize/tools discovery: **236 unique tools** at `/mcp`, including `create_mission`, `list_missions`, `update_mission`, and `hela.pipe`; hardware-only tools absent.\n- Public endpoint: `https://hela-runtime.glassgallery.my.id/mcp`.\n- A local HTTP `terminal_session_list` call passed MCP and payload-level error checks.\n- Isolated strict package/initialize/inventory/snapshot/pin checks passed for eight cells and runtime; deployed doctor reported **8/8 READY**.\n\nThe isolated feature-disabled runtime exposes **230 tools**. The live configuration adds `llm_query`, `llm_decompose_task`, `llm_suggest_route`, `llm_summarize`, `agent_run`, and runtime alias `hela.agent.run`. No isolated tools were missing from live discovery. Those additional model-backed tools were discovered, not invoked.\n\nThe 211 unresolved divergent-note conflicts remain archived. All **633** existing servy conflict archive files retained their pre-rollout hashes. No conflict winner, explicit bulk deduplication, or deletion reconciliation was forced.\n\n## Evidence and retained recovery material\n\nServy rollout directory:\n\n`/home/ubuntu/.local/state/hela/deployments/todo102-z65w9kzr/`\n\nIt contains `manifest.json`, `backup/`, `rollback/`, the runtime GC root, `verify-versions.mjs.log`, `doctor.mjs.log`, `http-verification.json`, and `feature-difference.json`. These are local operational records, not public credential/configuration dumps.\n\nPrevious backups remain at:\n\n- lappy: `/home/azzar/.local/state/hela/backups/pre-10.2-d9nokk5t/`.\n- servy: `/home/ubuntu/.local/state/hela/backups/pre-10.2-m6j95llu/`.\n\nRollback material is retained, but no live rollback was performed. Any rollback must stop the service, preserve current databases/vaults, inspect compatibility, and select retained code/dependencies deliberately. Do not overwrite active databases or hard-reset repositories.\n\n## Remaining work\n\n- Authenticated Zed/Codex ACP session and GUI warning verification remain pending. Direct MCP and isolated Codex discovery passed; credentials were not copied and the optional ACP duplicate-filter override remains unset.\n- Two older stdio runtimes owned by an active servy OpenCode client were preserved. They require a deliberate client reload; the HTTP service rollout does not update code already loaded in unrelated processes. Local client reload state also remains unverified.\n- PM2 has no active or saved individual MCP cell entries. It still owns the legacy `mcp-gateway` on port **8010**, with no child cells and seven retired backends on **8011–8017** reporting down. Historical entries remain in `dump.pm2.bak`. Review consumers/routes before retiring this gateway; it does not serve the verified runtime endpoint on **8090**.\n- Review archived vault conflicts before reconciliation. Deployment is not conflict resolution.\n- True first-call ignition, full revision/configuration manifest invalidation, optional hardware integrations, and deferred endpoint authentication/credential rotation remain separate work.\n"},{"source":"docs/documentation-refresh-2026-11-06.md","title":"Documentation refresh — 2026-11-06","summary":"Ecosystem 1.5.1 is a documentation-only source snapshot. The ten MCP cells remain 3.0.0; runtime remains 1.2.0 at its existing implementation revision. Current versions and…","category":"archive","archived":true,"link":"library/docs/documentation-refresh-2026-11-06.html","text":"# Documentation refresh — 2026-11-06\n\nEcosystem **1.5.1** is a documentation-only source snapshot. The ten MCP cells remain **3.0.0**; runtime remains **1.2.0** at its existing implementation revision. [Current versions](current-versions.md) and [profile counts](generated-profile-counts.md) are generated from canonical metadata.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Audited all 12 repositories. Updated the ten cells' README version banners and generated canonical tool references; the runtime's existing 1.2.0 reference was already current.\n- Completed all 16 English/Indonesian website pages, including current architecture, profiles, requirements, runtime behavior, diagnostics, privacy, and historical showcase labels.\n- Corrected Codex keys and actual repository paths, including Plastid's `brain` entrypoint. Browser previews intentionally omit private environment values and are not byte-identical installation files.\n- Regenerated seven committed client examples with a sanitized environment. The older seven-cell Compose file is explicitly a deployment scaffold, not full-profile or transport acceptance evidence.\n- Preserved v1.0.0–v1.5.0 snapshots, historical release evidence, and independently versioned Blender/HyperFrames/vendor material.\n\n## Verification\n\n- Documentation generation is idempotent; `docs:check` and all **74** documentation/configuration regressions pass. These checks include local web links/anchors, unique IDs, JSON-LD, current versions, canonical tool lists, script ordering, and release cache-busting.\n- CLI matrix: **80/80** profile/backend cases. Probe and inventory regressions: **4/4** each. Strict package/MCP/inventory/snapshot versions and revision pins: **11/11**.\n- Standalone integration and safe cross-cell checks: **15/15** across ten servers, with no tool-name collisions in the feature-disabled probe environment.\n- All eight profiles: **16/16** namespace-mode checks pass, with exact schemas, zero discovery startups, and safe read-only routing.\n- Fresh native ARM probes validate all eight `dev-workspace` cells against the new documentation revisions. Schema parity retains both verified Nucleus build digests; compiled implementation bytes were reused, not rebuilt as a new software release.\n- Browser checks: **64** visible configurator selections per language; **16** pages at **1200 px** and **390 px** widths without document-level horizontal overflow. Desktop/mobile screenshots were inspected. Canonical search links, successful copy, and denied-clipboard feedback were checked; failed clipboard writes no longer claim success.\n- Current Markdown file-link scan: **104** references checked, no missing targets. This is not an exhaustive external-link availability guarantee.\n- Secret scanning found only the pre-existing probe-test sentinel; no new credentials were added. Hardware, providers, and authenticated ACP/GUI sessions were not exercised by this documentation refresh.\n\n## Publication boundaries\n\nPlastid and Receptor had unrelated remote dependency updates. Documentation-only commits were merged into their default branches without discarding those updates. Snapshot v1.5.1 deliberately pins the documentation-only parents, not unvalidated dependency changes.\n\nThe Pages workflow validates generated documentation and its tests before publishing. Source commit: `7f95832b4f0b9e6ba3057ffbb7df84b62e2bcd91`. Published Pages commit: `3077d3f8d02436637b4322407c58621069dfe922`.\n\n- [Ecosystem CI](https://github.com/Hela-Ecosystem/hela-mcp-ecosystem/actions/runs/42233639731): successful.\n- [Validated site build/sync](https://github.com/Hela-Ecosystem/hela-mcp-ecosystem/actions/runs/42233639734): successful.\n- [GitHub Pages deployment](https://github.com/Hela-Ecosystem/hela-mcp-ecosystem/actions/runs/42233666134): successful.\n- Public checks at [hela.my.id](https://hela.my.id/) and its Indonesian pages: all **16** pages return HTTP 200 with v1.5.1 and versioned scripts; all **26** discovered internal resources return success. Generated data, app, stylesheet, counts, and the hero image load successfully.\n- Public browser configurators pass **64 selections per language**, with exact profile membership, current metadata, working canonical search links, and no desktop/mobile document overflow.\n\nNo live runtime was restarted or reconfigured. Servy's service remained active at PID `715090`, with zero automatic restarts during native ARM catalog verification. Active databases, existing vault conflicts, security settings, credentials, and unrelated client configurations were not changed. Deferred endpoint security, authenticated ACP acceptance, deliberate external-client reloads, legacy gateway retirement, and conflict reconciliation remain separate work.\n"},{"source":"docs/generated-profile-counts.md","title":"Generated profile counts","summary":"Regenerate with npm run catalog:generate; includes hela.pipe and the agent alias where enabled.","category":"reference","archived":false,"link":"library/docs/generated-profile-counts.html","text":"# Generated profile counts\n\nRegenerate with `npm run catalog:generate`; includes `hela.pipe` and the agent alias where enabled.\n\n- `minimal`: 3 cells, 78–84 runtime tools.\n- `dev-workspace`: 8 cells, 230–236 runtime tools.\n- `headless-server`: 7 cells, 157–163 runtime tools.\n- `research`: 5 cells, 167–173 runtime tools.\n- `web-devops`: 6 cells, 178–184 runtime tools.\n- `android-testing`: 5 cells, 131–137 runtime tools.\n- `3d-modeling`: 5 cells, 92–98 runtime tools.\n- `all`: 10 cells, 291–297 runtime tools.\n"},{"source":"docs/installation-acceptance.md","title":"Installer acceptance — 2026-11-07","summary":"Local acceptance of the managed installer on Linux x86_64 with Node 22. This records working-tree changes, not a published release or a live-service rollout. Ecosystem release…","category":"operations","archived":false,"link":"library/docs/installation-acceptance.html","text":"# Installer acceptance — 2026-11-07\n\nLocal acceptance of the managed installer on Linux x86_64 with Node 22. This records working-tree changes, not a published release or a live-service rollout. Ecosystem release metadata remains 1.5.1; cells remain 3.0.0 and runtime 1.2.0.\n\n## Real execution\n\n- All eight profiles passed all five modes: `individual`, `runtime`, `docker`, `docker-runtime`, and `nix` — 40 mode/profile checks.\n- Doctor verified 269 cell/runtime endpoints across those checks, including 24 private runtime HTTP probes.\n- All 320 profile/mode/client-format configurations passed structural checks. The generated Cursor-format commands were also executed: 162 actual client connections, including reconnects for persistence.\n- All 40 cases created an isolated Genome entity, restarted the client connection, and read the entity back. Runtime cases started no cells during discovery and only Genome on the first memory call.\n- The matrix reused freshly built isolated full-source artifacts, a Nix runtime package, and a full Docker image. These are **not 40 independent cold builds**. Native fixture repositories are linked into temporary prefixes; production installation still refuses symlinked source repositories.\n\nSeparate real installations passed fresh doctor checks: native individual/minimal, native runtime/all, Nix/minimal with and without the model, Docker-runtime/all with the model, and Docker/minimal without it. The model-free Nix and Docker installations were checked to contain no Needle model. The Docker image default profile was checked as well.\n\nExplicitly started native, Nix, and Docker Compose HTTP listeners passed `doctor --url`, then were stopped. Compose attached stdio also passed a real Genome mutation/reconnect/read cycle using named volumes, with no published stdio ports. The isolated Compose projects and their test volumes were removed. No doctor containers remained after acceptance.\n\nNix runtime output links were verified as GC roots. The runtime derivation can be reused from the local Nix store; cell dependencies are still installed from lockfiles and built in their managed source directories.\n\nThe pinned Needle engine/model completed an actual offline tool-selection smoke test on both the host and the full Docker image, returning a successful `read_graph` call. This supplements engine execution, asset checksums, and license verification; it is not a planner-quality benchmark.\n\n## Regression gates\n\n- 52 installer tests: profile/mode plans, isolated fixture installs and reinstalls, malformed options, snapshot/checkout failures and retry, repository origin/dirty/symlink refusal, output preservation, missing interpreters, changed builds, model checksum failures, HTTP protocol errors, and Docker cleanup on failure.\n- 74 documentation/configurator tests.\n- 80 standalone profile/client configuration cases.\n- Four MCP isolation/failure probes.\n- Documentation metadata consistency, shell syntax, and Git whitespace checks.\n\nThe secret scan completed; its sole finding was the existing synthetic credential sentinel in the isolation test, not a credential. This is not a container CVE audit.\n\n## Reproduce\n\nBuild isolated fixtures first using the [installation guide](installation.md). The source fixture must include all ten cells plus runtime; the Docker fixture must contain the full image. Then run:\n\n```bash\nnpm run test:install\nnpm run test:docs\nnpm run test:matrix\nnpm run test:probe\nnpm run docs:check\nnpm run test:install:modes -- \\\n  --source-prefix /path/to/fresh-runtime-all \\\n  --nix-prefix /path/to/fresh-nix-install \\\n  --docker-prefix /path/to/fresh-docker-all \\\n  --out /path/to/modes.json\n```\n\nThe matrix uses temporary private workspaces/data directories and deletes only its own temporary fixtures. It does not reuse production databases, vaults, or provider credentials. Generated client defaults may advertise more optional tools than doctor's deliberately provider-disabled baseline.\n\n## Boundaries\n\nReal installation acceptance here is Linux x86_64 only. The added CI jobs cover clean Docker/Nix installs and fixture regressions, but remote CI has not yet run for these working-tree changes. macOS, ARM64, and WSL cold installs were not repeated in this acceptance pass; native Windows remains untested.\n\nBrowser executables, Android/Blender bridges, external providers, authenticated GUI/ACP clients, host device/SSH access from containers, and security hardening remain separate checks. `READY` certifies the stated MCP/install checks, not every tool's external integration. Existing services, databases, vault conflicts, and historical release snapshots were not migrated or replaced.\n"},{"source":"docs/installation.md","title":"Installation modes and doctor","summary":"The installer supports eight profiles through five modes: individual, runtime, docker, docker-runtime, and nix. Runtime-based modes support stdio and Streamable HTTP. A mode…","category":"gettingStarted","archived":false,"link":"library/docs/installation.html","text":"# Installation modes and doctor\n\nThe installer supports eight profiles through five modes: `individual`, `runtime`, `docker`, `docker-runtime`, and `nix`. Runtime-based modes support stdio and Streamable HTTP. A mode selects the execution mechanism; a profile selects eligible cells.\n\nSee [local acceptance evidence](installation-acceptance.md) for the tested mechanisms, real client connections, persistence checks, and platform/integration boundaries.\n\n## Requirements and boundaries\n\n- Use Node 22+ and Git. Native modules need Python, make, a C/C++ compiler, and platform headers. `--mode nix` supplies a pinned development toolchain and packages the runtime with Nix.\n- Docker modes require a working Docker daemon and Compose v2. Their images build the selected cells from pinned source; they do not require host npm dependencies or per-cell Dockerfiles.\n- Supported shell entry points are Linux, macOS, and Windows through WSL. Native Windows installation is not acceptance-tested; several upstream build scripts require POSIX tools.\n- The pinned local Needle engine/model is installed by default on Linux x86_64/ARM64 and macOS ARM64. `--without-model` explicitly selects core MCP without that integration; it is needed on platforms without a pinned engine. Assets are fetched from an immutable upstream revision, checksum-verified, and accompanied by their license.\n- Chromium/browser services, Android devices/ADB, Blender/its bridge, provider credentials, and GUI client authentication remain separate integration requirements. `READY` does not certify those operations or provide an OS sandbox.\n\nInstallation does not start a persistent service, change system packages, replace existing repositories, or rewrite live client settings. Endpoint security and credential rotation are not changed by this installer.\n\n## Managed layout\n\nDefault prefix: `${XDG_DATA_HOME:-$HOME/.local/share}/hela/installations/\u003cprofile>-\u003cmode>`. Use `--prefix /absolute/path` to choose another directory.\n\nEach prefix contains its own ecosystem metadata, sibling source repositories, verified installation manifest, workspace, private data directories, and generated client fragments. Plastid is cloned at the repository root and built from `ll3m-mcp/brain`.\n\nThe installer refuses unmanaged nonempty prefixes, dirty repositories, symlinked repositories, origin/pin/version mismatches, failed installs/builds, and existing explicit output files. A failed install retains a pending marker; doctor and launchers will not treat it as ready. Re-running the same installation performs locked dependency installation, rebuilds, and verifies it again. Existing data is not restored, reset, or deleted.\n\n## Individual servers\n\n```bash\n./setup.sh --mode individual --profile dev-workspace --client opencode --non-interactive\n./setup.sh doctor --mode individual --profile dev-workspace\n```\n\nMerge the generated private fragment into the client file after review. Native client entries start an installed launcher, which selects the correct executable, workspace, and persistent data directory. Unrelated client/provider settings are preserved because the installer never overwrites them.\n\n## Single stdio runtime\n\n```bash\n./setup.sh --mode runtime --profile dev-workspace --client codex --non-interactive\n./setup.sh doctor --mode runtime --profile dev-workspace\n```\n\n`--runtime` is an alias for `--mode runtime`. `--with-runtime` additionally builds the daemon for an individual installation without switching its client fragment to one connection.\n\nThe selected-profile catalog is generated from fresh probes. Runtime verification checks exact cold catalog parity and zero cell startup, including its HTTP transport. The first actual cell call starts only its target.\n\n## Docker: separate cells or runtime\n\n```bash\n./setup.sh --mode docker --profile dev-workspace --client cursor --non-interactive\n./setup.sh doctor --mode docker --profile dev-workspace\n\n./setup.sh --mode docker-runtime --profile dev-workspace --client opencode --non-interactive\n./setup.sh doctor --mode docker-runtime --profile dev-workspace\n```\n\n`--client docker` remains a shortcut for a Docker installation with Compose output. Use `--runtime` with it for the aggregated daemon. Both Docker modes build one profile image; client entries call Docker directly with the immutable image ID, launching separate cell containers or one runtime container. Those stdio clients do not need a host Node runtime after installation. `compose.json` provides the equivalent Compose services.\n\nFilesystem, terminal, and process tools operate inside the container and explicitly mounted workspace. Host SSH keys, device access, external browser engines, and Blender bridges are not silently copied or granted; configure those integrations deliberately.\n\nStdio needs attached stdin, not published ports. Compose services have `stdin_open: true`, no TTY, and persistent named volumes. Use `docker compose -f /path/to/compose.json run --rm --no-deps -T \u003cservice>` for an attached stdio connection; `up -d` alone does not connect an MCP client to stdio.\n\nDoctor starts uniquely named temporary containers with no host data/vault mounts or inherited provider keys, verifies actual MCP replies and image source provenance, and removes those containers. It does not stop existing containers or clean global images/volumes.\n\n## Nix runtime\n\n```bash\n./setup.sh --mode nix --profile dev-workspace --client opencode --non-interactive\n./setup.sh doctor --mode nix --profile dev-workspace\n```\n\nNix supplies the pinned Node/native-build toolchain for cell builds and builds the runtime package. The installation's `nix-runtime` output link is retained as a GC root. This is a packaged runtime plus locked cell builds, not a claim that all ten cells are independent Nix derivations. Keep private files untracked; Git-backed flake references exclude ignored credentials and data from Nix source copies.\n\n## HTTP runtime\n\n```bash\n./setup.sh --mode runtime --profile dev-workspace --transport http --port 8080 --client codex --non-interactive\n# Start the installed launcher explicitly; its path is printed by setup:\nnode /path/to/prefix/hela-mcp-ecosystem/scripts/launch.mjs hela-runtime\n./setup.sh doctor --url http://127.0.0.1:8080/mcp --json\n```\n\nThe same transport flags work with `--mode nix` and `--mode docker-runtime`. HTTP listeners bind to loopback on the host. Docker publishes only `127.0.0.1:\u003cport>`; `compose.json` includes the HTTP command and port mapping. Explicitly start it with Compose `up -d` if desired.\n\nHTTP fragments are supported for OpenCode, Kilo, Codex, and Cursor. Other client backends fail clearly rather than emit unverified HTTP syntax; use `--client skip` and configure their MCP URL manually. No listener is auto-started during installation. Doctor's private HTTP probe does not substitute for checking the running URL.\n\n## Snapshots, reconfiguration, and failures\n\n```bash\n./setup.sh --mode runtime --profile minimal --snapshot v1.5.1 --client skip --non-interactive\n./setup.sh --reconfigure --prefix /path/to/existing/prefix --client zed --non-interactive\n./setup.sh doctor --prefix /path/to/existing/prefix --json\n```\n\nSnapshots must declare exact commits and server versions. Incomplete historical metadata is rejected, not silently ignored. `--dev` follows recorded branches for host modes and records the resolved revisions/versions; Docker requires pinned snapshots. Optional provider keys are read from environment, not command-line secret flags. Generated fragments can contain those supplied credentials and are written with private permissions.\n\nReconfiguration infers the existing mode/profile/transport from an explicit prefix and only generates another fragment. To change transport or port, rerun installation with the new settings; a stdio fragment must not point at an HTTP-only launcher.\n\nDoctor exits nonzero for missing or incomplete installations, modified builds, wrong revisions/versions, missing images, failed MCP replies, and runtime catalog/HTTP failures. A remote `--url` check verifies MCP initialize/discovery and the runtime version, not remote filesystem pins or hardware. Existing standalone sibling/nested checkouts remain diagnosable when no managed installation is selected.\n\nNever overwrite live SQLite files or force vault conflicts to resolve installation warnings. Use validated backups and a deliberate migration if you intend to move existing memory into a new managed prefix.\n"},{"source":"docs/integration.md","title":"MCP client integration","summary":"The canonical inventory and selected profile drive configuration. Do not copy old repository paths or hand-maintain a second list of servers. Current versions and counts are in…","category":"gettingStarted","archived":false,"link":"library/docs/integration.html","text":"# MCP client integration\n\n![Blotcat connecting desktop clients](../assets/blotcat-integration.jpg)\n\nThe canonical inventory and selected profile drive configuration. Do not copy old repository paths or hand-maintain a second list of servers. Current versions and counts are in [current versions](current-versions.md).\n\nThe installer now creates a managed prefix and private client fragment instead of replacing live client files. Use `--mode individual`, `runtime`, `docker`, `docker-runtime`, or `nix`; see [installation modes](installation.md). Review and merge the generated fragment. Its launcher supplies the installed executable, workspace, data location, and selected profile.\n\n## Generate configuration\n\nUse Node 22 LTS and build the selected cells first:\n\n```bash\n./setup.sh --profile dev-workspace --client opencode --non-interactive\n```\n\nTo inspect a generated file without installing it into a client:\n\n```bash\nnode scripts/generate-config.mjs --profile dev-workspace --client opencode \\\n  --root /absolute/path/to/MCPservers/hela-mcp-ecosystem --stdout\n```\n\nFor manually maintained repositories, `--root` points to the ecosystem checkout and cell directories normally sit beside it. For a managed installation, add `--prefix /path/to/prefix --mode \u003cmode>` to generate a fragment using its verified launchers and metadata. Plastid's package is `ll3m-mcp/brain`, not its Git repository root.\n\nOutput may contain credentials supplied through environment variables or options. Do not publish generated private configuration. The committed examples use placeholders and no real keys.\n\n## Client formats and locations\n\n- **Cursor:** `mcpServers` JSON in `~/.cursor/mcp.json`.\n- **Claude:** `mcpServers` JSON. Claude Desktop commonly uses `~/Library/Application Support/Claude/claude_desktop_config.json` on macOS, `%APPDATA%/Claude/claude_desktop_config.json` on Windows, and `~/.config/Claude/claude_desktop_config.json` on Linux; CLI integrations can use a different location. Confirm the file used by your installed client.\n- **Gemini / Antigravity:** `mcpServers` JSON; the installer supports the configured Gemini/Antigravity path. Confirm your client version's actual file before merging.\n- **OpenCode / Kilo:** `mcp` JSON. Local entries have `type: \"local\"` and a command array.\n- **Zed:** `context_servers` JSON in `~/.config/zed/settings.json`. Preserve unrelated settings and shared server definitions.\n- **Codex:** TOML under **`[mcp_servers.\u003cname>]`**, not `[mcpServers.\u003cname>]`, in `~/.codex/config.toml`.\n\nGenerate a private output file with `--out \u003cpath>` and review/merge it instead of replacing an existing client configuration blindly. Eight profiles and ten generator backends, including Docker and skip, are checked by the CLI matrix. Browser previews use placeholder paths; the CLI is the installation/configuration source.\n\n## Single-endpoint runtime\n\nKeep the selected profile and add the serve-mode flag:\n\n```bash\n./setup.sh --profile dev-workspace --client opencode --runtime --non-interactive\n```\n\nThere is no `runtime` profile. `HELA_PROFILE` selects eligible cells and defaults to `dev-workspace`. Runtime startup and discovery spawn no cells; a call starts only its target. Standalone server definitions remain supported for installations that do not use the runtime.\n\nThe installer generates the selected-profile schema catalog. After rebuilding a cell:\n\n```bash\nnpm run catalog:generate -- --profile dev-workspace\n./setup.sh doctor --profile dev-workspace --runtime\n```\n\nSee [on-demand runtime](on-demand-runtime.md) for stale-cache refusal and explicit `hela.pipe` bootstrap. A healthy HTTP liveness endpoint alone is not full MCP or external-integration acceptance evidence.\n\n## Zed and Codex ACP ownership\n\nCodex-native MCP entries and Zed-forwarded entries can have the same name. A duplicate warning can refer to the forwarded duplicate rather than failure of the native entry. `codex mcp list` inspects configuration; it does not execute servers or prove connectivity.\n\nDo not remove shared top-level Zed definitions as a workaround: native Zed and other agents may need them. Native agent-profile permissions are not a per-ACP forwarding switch. Authenticated ACP/GUI warning verification remains separate from isolated MCP discovery. See the adapter-version-specific guidance in [troubleshooting](troubleshooting.md#zed-forwards-a-server-that-codex-already-defines); the optional merge override is not enabled by this guide.\n\n## Keys and optional services\n\nGitHub tokens are optional for live remote prompt/catalog operations. Enzyme can use keyless network fallbacks when Google CSE credentials are absent; configured CSE uses `GOOGLE_API_KEY` and **`GOOGLE_CSE_ID`**. Model-backed tools require their configured engine/provider. SQLite remains Genome's base engine; Obsidian and Redis are optional. Read [keys and services](keys-and-secrets.md) and the selected server's generated canonical tool reference.\n\n## Docker installations\n\nUse the installer to build and verify a selected-profile image before generating/using its client fragment:\n\n```bash\n./setup.sh --mode docker --profile headless-server --client docker --non-interactive\n./setup.sh doctor --mode docker --profile headless-server\n```\n\n`compose.json` uses the built image ID, actual entrypoints, attached stdio without a TTY, and persistent volumes. `--mode docker-runtime` selects one runtime container. Add `--transport http` only for runtime-based modes; the installer generates loopback-only HTTP settings and leaves listener startup explicit. The older seven-cell `config/docker-compose.yml` is historical scaffolding, not the supported installer path. Hardware and provider integrations are still separate.\n\n## Acceptance and reloads\n\n```bash\n./setup.sh doctor --profile dev-workspace --runtime\nnpm run test:matrix\nnpm run docs:check\n```\n\nUse `--runtime` only when that runtime is installed. Run full-stack integration/profile tests on a machine with the required cells, or select the installed subset. Reload only the relevant clients after review; some clients auto-reload immediately on settings changes. Back up live databases/vaults before changing synchronization hooks. Do not restore over active SQLite files, force a vault conflict winner, or kill unrelated processes during verification.\n\nPowerful filesystem, command, browser, and agent tools run with their server account's privileges. Current verification is not a security sandbox or authenticated-public-endpoint guarantee; see [security status](security-audit.md).\n"},{"source":"docs/keys-and-secrets.md","title":"HeLa MCP Ecosystem: Keys & Secrets Guide","summary":"This document explains prerequisites and optional provider credentials. Keyless operation is not the same as offline operation or a guarantee of free/unlimited external…","category":"gettingStarted","archived":false,"link":"library/docs/keys-and-secrets.html","text":"# HeLa MCP Ecosystem: Keys & Secrets Guide\n\n![Blotcat Secrets & Credentials Vault](../assets/blotcat-secrets.jpg)\n\nThis document explains prerequisites and optional provider credentials. Keyless operation is not the same as offline operation or a guarantee of free/unlimited external services. See [current versions](current-versions.md) for software metadata and [security status](security-audit.md) for unresolved access-control work.\n\n---\n\n## 1. System Prerequisites\n\nBefore running setup, ensure your system meets the baseline requirements:\n\n- **Node.js:** 22 LTS is the tested full-stack environment. Enzyme requires at least 20.18.1 and Receptor requires 22; other packages declare their own standalone ranges.\n- **npm:** use the version bundled with the selected Node runtime and install locked dependencies with `npm ci`; no global npm upgrade is required by this guide.\n- **Git:** use a current version (release snapshots specify at least 2.30).\n- **SQLite:** Genome uses Node SQLite bindings; the SQLite CLI is optional for inspection. Do not replace active database files during upgrades.\n- **Docker/Compose:** optional, and generated Compose files are scaffolds requiring transport/device/persistence review.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Keys & Secrets Catalog\n\nProvider credentials are optional for MCP discovery and keyless capabilities. A selected external operation can still require credentials, network access, an installed local engine, or a live external service.\n\n```\n┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│                        HELA MCP KEY MATRIX                             │\n├───────────────────────┬──────────────────────┬─────────────┬───────────┤\n│ Secret Name           │ Target Component     │ Necessity   │ Fallback  │\n├───────────────────────┼──────────────────────┼─────────────┼───────────┤\n│ OPENROUTER_API_KEY    │ HeLa Mitosis         │ Optional    │ Local*    │\n│ GITHUB_TOKEN          │ HeLa Mitosis         │ Optional    │ Bundled   │\n│ GOOGLE_API_KEY        │ HeLa Enzyme          │ Optional    │ Wikipedia │\n│ GOOGLE_CSE_ID         │ HeLa Enzyme          │ Optional    │ Wikipedia │\n└───────────────────────┴──────────────────────┴─────────────┴───────────┘\n```\n\n`Local*` depends on the bundled/configured Needle engine and model. The matrix is not a guarantee that every tool can complete without its required provider or hardware.\n\n---\n\n### 1. `OPENROUTER_API_KEY`\n\n* **Target Component**: **HeLa Mitosis** (`hela-mitosis` / `chaining-mcp`)\n* **Purpose**: Powers dynamic LLM intelligence for task decomposition (`llm_decompose_task`), AI route ranking (`llm_suggest_route`), and query routing (`llm_query`).\n* **Where to get it**:\n  1. Visit [OpenRouter API Keys](https://openrouter.ai/keys).\n  2. Create a free account.\n  3. Generate an API key (`sk-or-v1-...`).\n  4. Provider model availability, quotas, and pricing can change; check the provider before relying on a free route.\n* **If you DO NOT have it**:\n   * **Consequence**: Provider-backed calls need some configured provider credentials; another configured provider can still be used when supported. Local planning can work without keys when its engine/model are available.\n   * **Fallback Behavior**: Needle planning/execution and budgeted escalation are separate. Unavailable escalation can hand control back to the caller; `brainstorming` needs a generative model and fails when none is configured. This is not a universal latency, crash-freedom, or offline guarantee.\n\n---\n\n### 2. `GITHUB_TOKEN`\n\n* **Target Component**: **HeLa Mitosis** (`hela-mitosis` / `chaining-mcp`)\n* **Purpose**: Synchronizes the latest community prompt collections, guidelines, and chatmodes from GitHub repositories at runtime.\n* **Where to get it**:\n  1. Visit [GitHub Personal Access Tokens](https://github.com/settings/tokens).\n  2. Generate a token with public read-only access.\n* **If you DO NOT have it**:\n  * **Consequence**: Live GitHub syncing is disabled.\n   * **Fallback Behavior**: Bundled prompt/instruction catalogs remain available. This does not make unrelated research, browser, provider, or remote-runtime operations offline.\n\n---\n\n### 3. `GOOGLE_API_KEY` & `GOOGLE_CSE_ID`\n\n* **Target Component**: **HeLa Enzyme** (`hela-enzyme` / `researcher-mcp`)\n* **Purpose**: Optional Tier 1 web search queries via the Google Custom Search JSON API.\n* **Where to get them**:\n   1. **API Key**: Use [Google Cloud Console](https://console.cloud.google.com/apis/credentials) and check current Custom Search API availability and quotas for your account.\n  2. **CSE ID**: Create a Search Engine ID at [Programmable Search Engine](https://programmablesearchengine.google.com/controlpanel/all) (set Search the entire web = ON).\n* **If you DO NOT have them**:\n   * **Consequence**: Configured Google CSE is unavailable; supported keyless network fallbacks may still work. Network failures, rate limits, and provider availability remain possible.\n  * **Fallback Behavior**: Live web search cascades to keyless parallel search (DuckDuckGo, Bing, etc.) and Wikipedia fallback. `academic_search` queries arXiv's public OpenSearch API, `news_monitor` queries Google News RSS feeds, `search_trends` queries Hacker News Algolia, and content extraction falls back to Jina Reader (`r.jina.ai`).\n\n---\n\n## 3. Configuration & Diagnostics\n\n### Initial Setup\nRun the setup script:\n```bash\n./setup.sh\n```\nThe installer reads optional provider keys from environment and never prompts for or accepts secret values as CLI flags. Keyless defaults can still use the network. Generated private client fragments can contain supplied credentials; review them before sharing, and merge them into client settings deliberately.\n\n### Reconfiguring Keys Anytime\nTo update keys or change clients on an installed ecosystem:\n```bash\n./setup.sh --reconfigure\n```\n\n### Safe Diagnostic Health Check\nTo verify your environment and check which fallbacks are active without exposing keys in logs:\n```bash\n./setup.sh doctor\n```\n"},{"source":"docs/on-demand-runtime.md","title":"On-demand runtime and canonical discovery","summary":"Runtime 1.2.0 implements §10.3: no cell starts during runtime startup or tools/list. The first call starts only its target; concurrent calls share that startup. Cells already…","category":"reference","archived":false,"link":"library/docs/on-demand-runtime.html","text":"# On-demand runtime and canonical discovery\n\nRuntime **1.2.0** implements §10.3: no cell starts during runtime startup or `tools/list`. The first call starts only its target; concurrent calls share that startup. Cells already started retain the existing crash/timeout supervisor. Standalone MCP servers are unchanged, all eight profiles remain supported, and the runtime default stays `dev-workspace`.\n\n## One inventory, generated schemas and counts\n\n`config/inventory.json` remains the source of cell paths, revisions, versions, environment declarations, and conditional tool features. `config/profiles.json` selects membership. Config generation, doctor, version verification, integration, and profile tests use the same inventory loader/path resolution.\n\nThe generated `config/tool-catalog.json` contains actual standalone MCP schemas, revision/version metadata, verified build digests, and feature variants. It is a derived artifact, not another server registry. Mitosis currently has four LLM/agent feature combinations; other cells have one schema variant. The generator enables registration flags only for discovery and never invokes model-backed tools.\n\nBuild outputs can differ between machines even at one source revision. Multiple digests are retained together only if fresh probes return identical schemas for every declared variant. New or changed conditional tool registration must declare its switches under the cell's `toolFeatures`; other relevant settings belong in its `env` list. Configuration changes require a new runtime instance.\n\nRun from the ecosystem checkout after building the selected cells and runtime:\n\n```bash\nnpm run catalog:generate -- --profile dev-workspace\nnpm run catalog:check -- --profile dev-workspace\n./setup.sh doctor --profile dev-workspace --runtime\nnpm run test:profiles -- --profile dev-workspace\n```\n\nThe installer refreshes selected-profile catalog entries when installing the runtime. Partial installations do not need unrelated hardware cells. An installation with all cells can omit the profile argument to generate/check all entries.\n\nThe same generator writes [profile-count documentation](generated-profile-counts.md) and `docs/site/data/tool-counts.json`. Counts include `hela.pipe` and the optional agent alias; they are feature-dependent ranges, not fixed totals copied into each client configuration.\n\n## Cache isolation and stale metadata\n\nThe format-2 private cache separates ecosystem roots, profiles, and namespace modes. Per-cell fingerprints cover declared and actual revisions, build contents, effective feature flags, and declared environment settings. Cache files contain fingerprints and schemas only, not environment values. Atomic publication prevents partial reads.\n\nLegacy, corrupt, and mismatched cache entries are ignored. A valid generated catalog supplies cold schemas without child processes. Missing/stale catalog entries are not advertised. Unknown calls do not scan other cells. Explicit `hela.pipe` routing can start an uncatalogued named cell and learn its schemas; regenerating the catalog restores full cold discovery.\n\nDoctor validates selected paths/builds, real isolated MCP handshakes, package/inventory/snapshot versions and revisions, and—when `--runtime` is requested—exact catalog/tool agreement with zero discovery spawns. Hardware checks are limited to the selected profile and remain separate from MCP readiness. API-key checks report presence, never values or assumed provider readiness.\n\n## Verification scope\n\n- **32/32** runtime regressions pass on x86_64 Linux and native ARM, covering timeout recovery, lazy startup, 24 concurrent first calls, cache invalidation, profile/root/namespace switching, legacy/corrupt caches, explicit bootstrap, and shutdown. **41 fixture PIDs** per run were verified reaped.\n- All eight profiles pass **16/16** namespace-off/on discovery/schema/routing checks. Discovery starts zero cells; only the safe Genome/Nucleus probes start those cells. Cold discovery, cold first-call, and warm-call durations are recorded in JSON output without asserting machine-independent latency limits.\n- The **80** client-configuration combinations, four probe regressions, and four canonical inventory regressions pass. Default standalone integration now excludes the multiplexing runtime from collision comparisons.\n- Eight additional runtime discovery checks cover all four Mitosis feature combinations in both namespace modes, without invoking model tools or starting cells. Doctor reports READY with cold catalog parity for all eight profiles.\n- Reproducible Nix packages use Node 22.23.4 and pinned/fixed-output inputs on both Linux architectures. Runtime versions derive from package metadata.\n\nThese checks do not authenticate GUI ACP sessions, reconcile live vault conflicts, exercise Android/Blender hardware, invoke model providers, or complete deferred endpoint security. Lappy retains its ten-server roster; servy retains its eight-cell `dev-workspace` selection.\n\n## Verified servy rollout\n\nRuntime source `9c92dbf5612db43fe80f1680ba4af97c4b5ea9b0` and ecosystem source release `1c47ca1c202c6a2acea79d7c6fdac5a19ef3c0bf` are deployed. Final-source Nix outputs are:\n\n- x86_64-linux: `/nix/store/r15rmywrswf01nj2xrvx753p1fh4f6rh-hela-runtime-1.2.0`.\n- aarch64-linux: `/nix/store/bhm781bld50yi9brmy2mn74p1ybzdzcy-hela-runtime-1.2.0`.\n\nThe ARM package is rooted under `/home/ubuntu/.local/state/hela/deployments/todo103-psnzx8x4/runtime`. Promotion fast-forwarded clean source checkouts, retained the old build/drop-in, and took fresh integrity-checked online backups of three databases plus a vault archive. Existing profile, environment, host, port, and security settings were preserved; only the selected runtime executable changed.\n\nLocal and public HTTP initialize/discovery report runtime **1.2.0**, **236 unique tools**, all three Genome mission tools, and no hardware-only tools. Both discovery checks left the service with **zero child cells**. A safe `terminal_session_list` call started only Nucleus; Genome did not start. Observed Nucleus calls were approximately **471 ms cold** and **13 ms warm** in this one run, including HTTP/MCP overhead—not a portable benchmark.\n\nDeployed doctor reports READY, catalog parity is exact, revision checks pass, and the GC root is verified. All previous HTTP-service PIDs exited. All **633** pre-rollout conflict archive files retained their hashes. No database file was restored/replaced, and unrelated client-owned runtimes and the legacy PM2 gateway were not stopped.\n\nThe rollout directory contains `manifest.json`, `http-verification.json`, `doctor.log`, `versions.log`, `catalog.log`, `backup/`, `rollback-dist/`, and the package GC root. Native committed-source staging/test evidence remains under `/home/ubuntu/.local/state/hela/staging/todo103-release-ah25pauz/`; local test/measurement logs are under `/var/tmp/scratch/opencode/todo103/`. Authenticated ACP, deliberate external-client reloads, gateway consumer review, vault reconciliation, and deferred security remain separate.\n"},{"source":"docs/profile-verification-2026-11-06.md","title":"All-profile verification — 2026-11-06","summary":"HeLa changes apply to the shared servers/runtime, not a hardcoded lappy or servy configuration. Each installation keeps its selected profile. The runtime default remains…","category":"archive","archived":true,"link":"library/docs/profile-verification-2026-11-06.html","text":"# All-profile verification — 2026-11-06\n\nHeLa changes apply to the shared servers/runtime, not a hardcoded lappy or servy configuration. Each installation keeps its selected profile. The runtime default remains `dev-workspace`; the distribution does not force everyone to `all`.\n\nMachine configurations were preserved:\n\n- Lappy has all ten standalone HeLa servers, equivalent to the `all` roster. Ten native Codex definitions were confirmed without printing environment values or credentials. This does not mean every client uses a single `HELA_PROFILE=all` runtime.\n- Servy's systemd HTTP runtime selects `dev-workspace`, eight cells. The profile is not named `dev-workflow`. Receptor and Plastid remain intentionally absent there.\n- Other installations can select any of the eight profiles in `config/profiles.json`.\n\n## Acceptance checks\n\nThe new `npm run test:profiles` command passed on lappy for all eight profiles: `minimal`, `dev-workspace`, `headless-server`, `research`, `web-devops`, `android-testing`, `3d-modeling`, and `all`.\n\nEach profile runs twice, with namespacing off and on: **16/16 runtime checks**. The verifier obtains actual tool schemas from the ten standalone cells, then checks that each runtime:\n\n- Advertises exactly the selected cells. Runtime 1.2.0 starts none during discovery and only starts the safely invoked cells during routing checks; see the [on-demand follow-up](on-demand-runtime.md).\n- Exposes exactly their tools plus `hela.pipe`, without collisions or out-of-profile tools.\n- Preserves each tool's input schema, including namespaced tool names.\n- Includes all three Genome mission tools.\n- Routes a safe Genome read-only call, plus a Nucleus session-list call where selected.\n\nWith model-backed features disabled, the `all` runtime exposes **291 tools**: 290 standalone tools plus `hela.pipe`. `dev-workspace` exposes **230** under the same conditions. Servy's feature-enabled live runtime exposes **236**; those feature-dependent differences are not profile failures.\n\nStrict package/initialize/inventory/snapshot/revision verification also passes for all ten cells and runtime. The client-generation matrix passes **80/80** profile/client combinations. Probe regressions pass **4/4**, including explicit profile/namespacing isolation from the host environment.\n\nAll probes use disposable HOME/project/central-memory locations, excluding inherited credentials and live vault hooks. Model, Android, and Blender operations were not invoked. These checks establish MCP startup/discovery/schema/routing support, not hardware integration or authenticated GUI sessions. Published release snapshots were not rewritten to backdate this additional evidence.\n\n## Repeating the checks\n\nFrom the ecosystem checkout, using Node 22:\n\n```bash\nnpm run test:profiles\nnpm run test:profiles -- --json\nnode scripts/verify-versions.mjs --profile all\nnpm run test:matrix\nnpm run test:probe\n```\n\nThe full matrix requires all ten cells to be installed and built. A machine with only one selected profile can test that profile without installing unrelated hardware cells:\n\n```bash\nnpm run test:profiles -- --profile dev-workspace\n```\n\nMissing required builds fail explicitly rather than silently skipping a profile. This command starts isolated test processes; it does not rewrite client configuration, change the live profile, or restart a service.\n"},{"source":"docs/profiles.md","title":"HeLa MCP Ecosystem — Profile & Agent Persona Catalog","summary":"Blotcat Profile Selection & Specialization","category":"reference","archived":false,"link":"library/docs/profiles.html","text":"# HeLa MCP Ecosystem — Profile & Agent Persona Catalog\n\n![Blotcat Profile Selection & Specialization](../assets/blotcat-profiles.jpg)\n\nThe HeLa MCP Ecosystem organizes capabilities into **agent-oriented profiles**. Each profile pairs the two foundational backbone servers (**HeLa Mitosis** for orchestration and **HeLa Genome** for persistent state) with a tailored set of specialized capability servers.\n\n---\n\n## The Backbone Foundation\n\nEvery profile in the ecosystem includes the core dual backbone:\n\n* **HeLa Mitosis (`hela-mitosis` / `chaining-mcp`)**: Intelligent tool discovery, Needle-planned routing, Needle-backed sequential thinking, task decomposition, skills catalog, and workflow orchestration.\n* **HeLa Genome (`hela-genome` / `project-mcp`)**: Living SQLite knowledge graph (`memory.db`), task tracking, architectural decisions, and cross-session context restoration.\n\n---\n\n## Profile Catalog\n\nThere are eight profiles. The canonical membership is `config/profiles.json`; current versions and generated tool counts are in [current versions](current-versions.md) and [profile counts](generated-profile-counts.md).\n\n### `minimal` — HeLa Minimal\n\nThree servers: `hela-mitosis`, `hela-genome`, and `hela-enzyme`. This is the smallest research/memory stack; it does not require browser, Android, or Blender dependencies. Keyless research still uses the network.\n\n### 1. `dev-workspace` — HeLa Dev Workspace\n\n* **Target System:** GUI / Desktop Workstation\n* **Agent Persona:** Full-Stack Software Engineer & Desktop Workstation Agent\n* **Purpose:** Complete autonomous software development, testing, UI design, and browser verification.\n* **Included Servers (8 Servers):**\n  * Backbone: `HeLa Mitosis` (`hela-mitosis`), `HeLa Genome` (`hela-genome`)\n  * Workspace: `HeLa Membrane` (`hela-membrane`), `HeLa Nucleus` (`hela-nucleus`), `HeLa Ribosome` (`hela-ribosome`)\n  * Knowledge: `HeLa Enzyme` (`hela-enzyme`)\n  * Design & Web: `HeLa Phenotype` (`hela-phenotype`), `HeLa Cytosol` (`hela-cytosol`)\n* **Standard Workflow:**\n  1. Genome restores project history and open tasks.\n  2. Mitosis decomposes feature requirements into steps.\n  3. Membrane and Nucleus inspect code, run builds, and execute tests.\n  4. Phenotype generates design tokens and Tailwind components.\n  5. Cytosol validates web UI visually via Playwright.\n  6. Genome records decisions, test results, and observations.\n\n---\n\n### 2. `headless-server` — HeLa Headless Server (Core 7)\n\n* **Target System:** Headless Linux Server / CI/CD Runner / Remote VM\n* **Agent Persona:** Cloud Infrastructure, Headless Server & CI/CD Automation Agent\n* **Purpose:** Autonomous server management, CI/CD pipeline execution, background harness orchestration, and repository maintenance without requiring display servers.\n* **Included Servers (7 Servers):**\n  * Backbone: `HeLa Mitosis` (`hela-mitosis`), `HeLa Genome` (`hela-genome`)\n  * Workspace: `HeLa Membrane` (`hela-membrane`), `HeLa Nucleus` (`hela-nucleus`), `HeLa Ribosome` (`hela-ribosome`)\n  * Knowledge: `HeLa Enzyme` (`hela-enzyme`)\n  * Design: `HeLa Phenotype` (`hela-phenotype`)\n* **Standard Workflow:**\n  1. Genome restores environment state.\n  2. Mitosis plans deployment or maintenance sequence.\n  3. Ribosome spawns and monitors long-running background PTY tasks.\n  4. Nucleus executes terminal commands and RTK-optimized queries.\n  5. Enzyme researches technical documentation or package dependencies.\n  6. Genome tracks task completion.\n\n---\n\n### 3. `research` — HeLa Research Terminal\n\n* **Target System:** Headless / Desktop\n* **Agent Persona:** Deep Research Investigator & Technical Knowledge Synthesizer\n* **Purpose:** Multi-source research, web scraping, literature analysis, fact-checking, and structured documentation generation.\n* **Included Servers (5 Servers):**\n  * Backbone: `HeLa Mitosis` (`hela-mitosis`), `HeLa Genome` (`hela-genome`)\n  * Knowledge: `HeLa Enzyme` (`hela-enzyme`)\n  * Workspace: `HeLa Membrane` (`hela-membrane`)\n  * Interaction: `HeLa Cytosol` (`hela-cytosol`)\n* **Standard Workflow:**\n  1. Genome restores research topic and prior facts.\n  2. Mitosis constructs research query plan.\n  3. Enzyme queries Google Search and Wikipedia.\n  4. Cytosol extracts full article content from web pages.\n  5. Membrane writes research synthesis to markdown files.\n  6. Genome stores discovered facts into the knowledge graph.\n\n---\n\n### 4. `web-devops` — HeLa Web Dev + Browser Automation\n\n* **Target System:** GUI / Desktop\n* **Agent Persona:** Frontend Engineer & Web Verification Specialist\n* **Purpose:** Frontend component creation, design token synchronization, automated browser testing, and visual regression checking.\n* **Included Servers (6 Servers):**\n  * Backbone: `HeLa Mitosis` (`hela-mitosis`), `HeLa Genome` (`hela-genome`)\n  * Workspace: `HeLa Membrane` (`hela-membrane`), `HeLa Nucleus` (`hela-nucleus`)\n  * Design: `HeLa Phenotype` (`hela-phenotype`)\n  * Interaction: `HeLa Cytosol` (`hela-cytosol`)\n* **Standard Workflow:**\n  1. Genome restores frontend specifications.\n  2. Phenotype generates OKLCH tokens and Tailwind configuration.\n  3. Membrane creates component files.\n  4. Nucleus starts local development server.\n  5. Cytosol navigates, interacts, and captures screenshot verification.\n  6. Genome records UI validation results.\n\n---\n\n### 5. `android-testing` — HeLa Android Testing Rig\n\n* **Target System:** GUI / Mobile Automation Host (requires `adb` and USB/Wi-Fi connected device)\n* **Agent Persona:** Mobile QA Tester & Android Device Automation Engineer\n* **Purpose:** Mobile application testing, UI interaction, ADB automation, and mobile debugging.\n* **Included Servers (5 Servers):**\n  * Backbone: `HeLa Mitosis` (`hela-mitosis`), `HeLa Genome` (`hela-genome`)\n  * Workspace: `HeLa Nucleus` (`hela-nucleus`)\n  * Mobile: `HeLa Receptor` (`hela-receptor`)\n  * Knowledge: `HeLa Enzyme` (`hela-enzyme`)\n* **Standard Workflow:**\n  1. Genome restores issue ticket.\n  2. Nucleus inspects build outputs and logcat streams.\n  3. Receptor connects to device, inspects UI XML tree, and executes tap/swipe gestures.\n  4. Enzyme investigates error codes or crash logs.\n  5. Genome records reproduction steps and bug resolution.\n\n---\n\n### 6. `3d-modeling` — HeLa 3D / Blender Station\n\n* **Target System:** GUI / Workstation (requires local Blender 3.6+ installation)\n* **Agent Persona:** Generative 3D Artist & Blender Scene Architect\n* **Purpose:** Autonomous 3D modeling, procedural mesh generation, material assignment, scene composition, and rendering in Blender.\n* **Included Servers (5 Servers):**\n  * Backbone: `HeLa Mitosis` (`hela-mitosis`), `HeLa Genome` (`hela-genome`)\n  * 3D: `HeLa Plastid` (`hela-plastid`)\n  * Workspace: `HeLa Membrane` (`hela-membrane`), `HeLa Nucleus` (`hela-nucleus`)\n* **Standard Workflow:**\n  1. Genome restores 3D project requirements.\n  2. Mitosis breaks down 3D asset generation stages.\n  3. Plastid generates and executes Python scripts inside Blender.\n  4. Membrane saves generated `.blend` scene and rendered `.png` images.\n  5. Genome records asset metadata and scene relations.\n\n---\n\n### 7. `all` — HeLa All (Full 10-MCP Stack)\n\n* **Target System:** Any / Fully-Equipped Workstation\n* **Agent Persona:** Complete HeLa Autonomous Engineering Multi-Agent Stack\n* **Purpose:** Unrestricted access to all 10 servers in the ecosystem.\n* **Included Servers (10 Servers):**\n  * `hela-mitosis`, `hela-genome`, `hela-membrane`, `hela-nucleus`, `hela-ribosome`, `hela-enzyme`, `hela-cytosol`, `hela-phenotype`, `hela-receptor`, `hela-plastid`\n\n### Runtime serve mode (orthogonal to profiles)\n\nThe HeLa Runtime is **not a profile** — it is a serve method. Any profile can be served through the runtime:\n\n```bash\n./setup.sh --profile dev-workspace --client opencode --runtime   # builds dev-workspace cells + daemon\nHELA_PROFILE=minimal node hela-runtime/dist/index.js             # run with minimal profile\n```\n\nThe runtime advertises only the selected cells' schemas and starts each cell only when invoked. Discovery starts none. Use `--runtime` with any profile; `HELA_PROFILE` selects eligible cells and defaults to `dev-workspace`. Standalone servers remain supported.\n\n---\n\n## Profile Compatibility Matrix:\n\n| Profile ID | Target System | Total Servers | Backbone Servers | Headless Compatible | Requires External Runtime / Device |\n|---|---|:---:|:---:|:---:|---|\n| `minimal` | Any / Headless | 3 | Mitosis + Genome | Yes | Network for online research |\n| `dev-workspace` | GUI / Desktop | 8 | Mitosis + Genome | Yes (Playwright) | Playwright / Chromium |\n| `headless-server` | Headless | 7 | Mitosis + Genome | Yes | None |\n| `research` | Headless / GUI | 5 | Mitosis + Genome | Yes | None |\n| `web-devops` | GUI / Desktop | 6 | Mitosis + Genome | Yes (Playwright) | Playwright / Chromium |\n| `android-testing` | Device Host | 5 | Mitosis + Genome | No | `adb` + Android Device |\n| `3d-modeling` | Workstation | 5 | Mitosis + Genome | No | Blender CLI |\n| `all` | Full Host | 10 | Mitosis + Genome | Partial | Playwright, `adb`, Blender |\n"},{"source":"docs/security-audit.md","title":"Security status and verification scope","summary":"This is an operational status document, not a completed security certification. Earlier architecture descriptions overstated guarantees about sandboxing, credential isolation,…","category":"operations","archived":false,"link":"library/docs/security-audit.html","text":"# Security status and verification scope\n\n![Blotcat security reference](../assets/blotcat-security.jpg)\n\nThis is an operational status document, **not a completed security certification**. Earlier architecture descriptions overstated guarantees about sandboxing, credential isolation, offline operation, and test coverage. A process boundary, a profile, or a passing health endpoint does not establish those guarantees.\n\n## Explicitly deferred work\n\nEndpoint authentication, per-client authorization, exposed-credential rotation, public-exposure review, and resource/session limits remain deferred by the operator's development decision. Deferred does not mean implemented or safe for unrestricted public use. The documentation refresh does not change access controls or rotate credentials.\n\nFilesystem, shell, PTY, browser, model-agent, and device operations can act with the server account's privileges. Scope and deploy them accordingly. Runtime profiles select eligible cells; they are not a substitute for an authorization policy or an OS sandbox.\n\n## What the current checks establish\n\n- Source snapshots pin revisions. Runtime schema catalogs additionally validate versions/build digests and declared feature variants; private caches separate profiles, roots, namespace modes, revisions, builds, and declared configuration.\n- Isolated tests cover runtime timeouts, process exit before restart, bounded retries, first-call startup, concurrent calls, stale-cache refusal, and shutdown. This is lifecycle/reliability evidence, not complete adversarial testing.\n- Doctor checks real isolated MCP initialize/discovery, selected paths/versions/revisions, and optional runtime catalog parity. Hardware, provider, and authenticated GUI checks remain separate.\n- Genome's online backups include committed WAL data and validate integrity; restore refuses existing destinations and sidecars. This does not authorize overwriting an active database.\n- Vault synchronization preserves divergent notes in conflict archives. Archived conflicts still need deliberate review; transport success does not prove reconciliation.\n\nSee [runtime verification](on-demand-runtime.md), [profile verification](profile-verification-2026-11-06.md), and each server's current tool reference for exact scope. Do not promote historical benchmark/test totals into current security claims.\n\n## Credentials and generated output\n\nDoctor and schema-generation probes do not display credential values; isolated probes exclude inherited keys and live vault hooks. Generated client configuration, however, can contain credentials explicitly supplied by its user. Treat configuration files, generator stdout, backups, and issue attachments as private. Never paste a raw client configuration or process environment into a public issue.\n\nPresence of an API key does not prove provider readiness. Keyless research can still make network requests. Needle's local execution depends on its engine/model, and some generative operations need a configured model provider. Optional remote runtime/vault deployment also changes where data is processed and stored.\n\n## Carry-forward validation\n\nPTY orphan handling, SSH failures/transfers, browser network boundaries, archive/symlink policies, Android hardware, Blender execution, live search fallbacks, and external design services still need their own targeted checks. Tests for some of these exist, but full coverage is not implied by ecosystem configuration or discovery checks.\n\nCurrent package versions and generated counts are in [current versions](current-versions.md). Historical release notes and immutable snapshots retain their original versions and evidence; they are not current acceptance guarantees.\n"},{"source":"docs/troubleshooting.md","title":"HeLa MCP Ecosystem Troubleshooting & Diagnostic Guide","summary":"This guide provides systematic diagnostic procedures, error code explanations, and recovery workflows for the HeLa MCP Ecosystem.","category":"operations","archived":false,"link":"library/docs/troubleshooting.html","text":"# HeLa MCP Ecosystem Troubleshooting & Diagnostic Guide\n\nThis guide provides systematic diagnostic procedures, error code explanations, and recovery workflows for the **HeLa MCP Ecosystem**.\n\n![Blotcat wearing a hard hat, looking at a smoking server block while consulting a thick manual](../assets/blotcat-troubleshooting.jpg)\n\n---\n\n## 1. Fast Diagnostic Workflow: `setup.sh doctor`\n\nBefore manual debugging, always run the automated diagnostic health check:\n\n```bash\n./setup.sh doctor\n# or\nnpm run doctor\n```\n\nTo run diagnostics against a specific profile:\n```bash\n./setup.sh doctor --profile dev-workspace\n```\n\nTo output machine-readable JSON:\n```bash\n./setup.sh doctor --json\n```\n\nFor managed installations, select the installed `--mode` and profile, or use an explicit prefix:\n\n```bash\n./setup.sh doctor --prefix /path/to/prefix --json\n./setup.sh doctor --url http://127.0.0.1:8080/mcp --json\n```\n\nThe URL form checks an already-running HTTP endpoint, not its remote filesystem. See [installation modes](installation.md) for Docker, Nix, listener startup, and private client fragments.\n\n### Understanding Diagnostic Output\n\n* **`READY`**: Managed source/build/image identity and isolated MCP initialize/discovery checks passed. Runtime modes also check cold catalog parity and private HTTP discovery.\n* **`DEGRADED` / server `ERROR`**: A required check failed; inspect the JSON report for its server/error. Pending installations, changed builds, missing interpreters/images, and bad MCP replies fail readiness and return nonzero.\n* **Integration scope**: Discovery does not verify Playwright executables, Android/Blender bridges, provider credentials, GUI authentication, or arbitrary workspace access. Legacy unmanaged checkout diagnostics have a different report layout.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Common Issues & Recovery Procedures\n\n### 2.1 Diagnostic Failure: Missing Entrypoint or Build Artifacts\n\n**Symptom**: `[ERROR] Entrypoint file missing` or `dist/index.js not found`.\n\n**Root Cause**: The TypeScript source code has not been compiled into JavaScript build artifacts.\n\n**Fix**:\nFor a managed prefix, rerun the original setup command with the same mode/profile/prefix. Locked dependencies and selected builds are recreated and probed. Preserve dirty working-tree changes rather than resetting them. A pending marker is cleared only after successful verification.\n\nFor manually maintained sibling checkouts:\n```bash\n# Rebuild all 10 servers automatically\nnpm run build:all\n\n# Or build a single server manually\ncd \u003cserver-directory>\nnpm run build\n```\n\n---\n\n### 2.2 Server Stdio JSON-RPC Hang or Crash\n\n**Symptom**: `[ERROR] Exited early with code 1` or tool timeouts during execution.\n\n**Root Cause**: Node module dependencies are missing, or environment variables are malformed.\n\n**Fix**:\n```bash\n# Reinstall dependencies for the failing server\ncd \u003cserver-directory>\nnpm ci\nnpm run build\n```\n\nVerify stdio responsiveness manually:\n```bash\nnode \u003centrypoint-file>\n# The process should listen on stdin without throwing an uncaught exception. Press Ctrl+C to exit.\n```\n\n---\n\n### 2.3 Client Does Not Recognize Configured MCP Servers\n\n**Symptom**: Cursor, Claude, Gemini, or Zed reports that tools are not available.\n\n**Root Cause**: Configuration file was written to the wrong path, contains relative paths, or the client was not restarted.\n\n**Fix**:\n1. **Regenerate configuration with absolute paths**:\n   ```bash\n   ./setup.sh --reconfigure --prefix /path/to/prefix --client cursor --non-interactive\n   ```\n2. **Review and merge the printed private fragment into the correct client file**. Setup does not write these live files:\n   * **Cursor IDE**: `cat ~/.cursor/mcp.json`\n   * **Claude Desktop**: `cat ~/.claude.json`\n   * **Gemini / Antigravity**: `cat ~/.gemini/antigravity-cli/mcp_config.json`\n   * **OpenCode**: `cat ~/.config/opencode/opencode.json`\n   * **Kilo CLI**: `cat ~/.config/kilo/config.json`\n   * **Zed Editor**: `cat ~/.config/zed/settings.json`\n   * **Codex / ChatGPT**: `cat ~/.codex/config.toml`\n3. **Check reload behavior**: some clients require a restart; Zed can restart context servers immediately when settings are saved. Back up active memory/vault state before changing sync hooks. Never paste raw client configuration containing credentials into an issue or chat.\n\n### Zed forwards a server that Codex already defines\n\nThe message `MCP server hela-… was not started, because the Codex config already defines an MCP server with this name` refers to the forwarded duplicate. It does not prove that Codex failed to start its native entry. `codex mcp list` inspects configuration, not connectivity; check MCP startup/discovery or an actual safe tool call separately.\n\nZed 1.27.0 forwards project context servers to external agents; native `agent.profiles.*.context_servers` controls are not a per-ACP forwarding switch. Do not remove shared top-level `context_servers` just to silence Codex warnings: native Zed and other agents may need them.\n\nThe installed codex-acp 2.5.0 supports an optional per-agent environment override:\n\n```json\n\"env\": { \"DISABLE_MCP_CONFIG_FILTERING\": \"true\" }\n```\n\nPlace it under `agent_servers.codex-acp` only if deliberately choosing merged forwarded/native ownership. This disables adapter duplicate filtering, **not forwarding**; it can fail when future definitions disagree. Identical definitions passed isolated merge/discovery checks, but authenticated Zed ACP sessions still require verification. Keeping Codex-owned entries and leaving this toggle unset preserves native ownership, with the duplicate warning still possible.\n\nSource references: [Zed forwarding](https://github.com/zed-industries/zed/blob/c84af5b99cbc366d675064e52b23664a015b6e35/crates/agent_servers/src/acp.rs#L44-L65), [agent settings schema](https://github.com/zed-industries/zed/blob/c84af5b99cbc366d675064e52b23664a015b6e35/crates/settings_content/src/agent.rs#L842-L904), and [adapter toggle](https://github.com/agentclientprotocol/codex-acp/blob/v2.5.0/src/CodexAcpClient.ts#L1620-L1623).\n\n---\n\n### 2.4 Pinned Snapshot Recovery & Rollback\n\n**Symptom**: Upstream repository changes broke a server build or altered expected tool schemas.\n\n**Root Cause**: Moving `main`/`master` branch contains unreleased or breaking changes.\n\n**Fix**:\nRestore known-good, audited release snapshot:\n```bash\n./setup.sh --snapshot v1.5.1 --profile dev-workspace --non-interactive\n```\n\n---\n\n### 2.5 SQLite Database Lock or Corruption (`HeLa Genome`)\n\n**Symptom**: `database is locked` or `SQLite error` in `project-mcp`.\n\n**Possible causes**: Competing writers, a long transaction, permissions, filesystem failures, or a damaged database. A lock error alone does not prove corruption or missing WAL mode.\n\n**Fix**:\n1. Identify the actual database path and owning processes. Inspect integrity read-only:\n   ```bash\n   sqlite3 -readonly /absolute/path/to/memory.db \"PRAGMA integrity_check;\"\n   ```\n2. Take an online, validated backup that includes committed WAL data. Do not copy only the main file while SQLite is active; no daily-backup schedule is implied by the package.\n3. Restore only to a new, absent destination with no SQLite sidecars, verify integrity, and review a controlled switchover. Never overwrite an active database. Preserve divergent Obsidian notes and existing conflict archives instead of choosing a winner automatically.\n\n---\n\n### 2.6 External Hardware Driver Issues\n\n#### HeLa Receptor (Android / ADB)\n* **Verify ADB availability**: `adb version`\n* **Verify device connectivity**: `adb devices` (ensure device is authorized with USB debugging enabled).\n\n#### HeLa Cytosol (Browser Automation)\n* **Install Playwright browsers**:\n  ```bash\n  npx playwright install chromium\n  ```\n\n#### HeLa Plastid (Blender 3D)\n* **Verify Blender CLI in PATH**:\n  ```bash\n  blender --version\n  ```\n* If installed outside standard PATH, create a symlink:\n  ```bash\n  sudo ln -s /path/to/blender /usr/local/bin/blender\n  ```\n\n---\n\n## 3. Getting Further Support\n\n* **Run Integration Tests**: `npm test` discovers the installed cells; use `-- --profile \u003cid>` for a selected subset. Tool counts depend on enabled features.\n* **Run Matrix Tests**: `npm run test:matrix` verifies 80 profile/client configuration combinations, not GUI authentication or hardware operations.\n* **File an Issue**: Open an issue at [`Hela-Ecosystem/hela-mcp-ecosystem/issues`](https://github.com/Hela-Ecosystem/hela-mcp-ecosystem/issues) with reviewed/redacted output from `./setup.sh doctor --json` and the failed check. Do not attach private configs or environments.\n"},{"source":"docs/workflows.md","title":"HeLa MCP Ecosystem: Workflows & Integration Guide","summary":"This document defines the standard operational workflows and cross-MCP coordination patterns enabled by the HeLa MCP Ecosystem.","category":"reference","archived":false,"link":"library/docs/workflows.html","text":"# HeLa MCP Ecosystem: Workflows & Integration Guide\n\n![Blotcat Workflows Orchestration](../assets/blotcat-workflows.jpg)\n\nThis document defines the standard operational workflows and cross-MCP coordination patterns enabled by the **HeLa MCP Ecosystem**.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Architectural Foundation: Dual-Backbone Coordination\n\nEvery profile in the HeLa MCP Ecosystem is anchored by two coordinating backbone servers:\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TB\n    Agent[\"Agent / LLM Orchestrator\"]\n\n    Mitosis[\"HeLa Mitosis\u003cbr/>(Orchestrator)\u003cbr/>- Dynamic Routing\u003cbr/>- Step Reasoning\u003cbr/>- Tool Planning\"]\n    Genome[\"HeLa Genome\u003cbr/>(State & Memory)\u003cbr/>- Knowledge Graph\u003cbr/>- Entities / Relations\u003cbr/>- Observations\"]\n\n    Membrane[\"HeLa Membrane\u003cbr/>Workspace FS\"]\n    Nucleus[\"HeLa Nucleus\u003cbr/>Command Exec\"]\n    Ribosome[\"HeLa Ribosome\u003cbr/>PTY Harness\"]\n    Enzyme[\"HeLa Enzyme\u003cbr/>Research / Wiki\"]\n    Cytosol[\"HeLa Cytosol\u003cbr/>Browser DOM\"]\n\n    Agent --> Mitosis & Genome\n    Mitosis \u003c-->|\"Shared Context\"| Genome\n    Mitosis & Genome --> Membrane & Nucleus & Ribosome & Enzyme & Cytosol\n```\n\n- **HeLa Mitosis (`hela-mitosis`)**: Acts as the cognitive orchestrator. It decomposes complex prompts, analyzes tool performance, and runs Needle-backed `sequentialthinking` step-by-step logic (each thought is an observe → decide → execute agent step recorded in AgentState).\n- **HeLa Genome (`hela-genome`)**: Acts as the immutable project memory. It stores entities, relations, observations, and session milestones in `memory.db`.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Canonical Workflows Catalog\n\n### Workflow A: Autonomous Feature Engineering\n\n**Primary Profiles**: `dev-workspace`, `headless-server`\n**Components**: `hela-genome` → `hela-mitosis` → `hela-membrane` → `hela-nucleus` → `hela-genome`\n\n```mermaid\nsequenceDiagram\n    participant Agent\n    participant Genome as HeLa Genome\n    participant Mitosis as HeLa Mitosis\n    participant Membrane as HeLa Membrane\n    participant Nucleus as HeLa Nucleus\n\n    Agent->>Genome: get_session_context / search_nodes(task)\n    Genome-->>Agent: Previous architecture & constraints\n    Agent->>Mitosis: analyze_with_sequential_thinking(feature_req)\n    Mitosis-->>Agent: Step-by-step implementation plan\n    Agent->>Membrane: read_file / write_file(source_code)\n    Membrane-->>Agent: Code written to workspace\n    Agent->>Nucleus: execute_command(\"npm test\" / \"pytest\")\n    Nucleus-->>Agent: Test run output\n    Agent->>Genome: memory_edit(action=\"add_observations\", observations=[…])\n```\n\n#### Step-by-Step Tool Sequence:\n1. **Context Restoration**: Call `get_session_context` on `hela-genome` to retrieve active architectural decisions and dependencies.\n2. **Decomposition**: Call `llm_decompose_task` or `sequentialthinking` on `hela-mitosis` to generate an execution plan.\n3. **Workspace Modification**: Call `read_file` and `write_file` on `hela-membrane` to apply minimal, production-ready code changes.\n4. **Validation**: Call `execute_command` on `hela-nucleus` (or `rtk` wrapper) to run automated unit tests.\n5. **State Recording**: Call `memory_edit` on `hela-genome` to commit progress to the knowledge graph.\n\n---\n\n### Workflow B: Deep Research & Architecture Synthesis\n\n**Primary Profiles**: `research`, `dev-workspace`\n**Components**: `hela-enzyme` → `hela-cytosol` → `hela-mitosis` → `hela-genome` → `hela-membrane`\n\n```mermaid\nsequenceDiagram\n    participant Agent\n    participant Enzyme as HeLa Enzyme\n    participant Cytosol as HeLa Cytosol\n    participant Mitosis as HeLa Mitosis\n    participant Genome as HeLa Genome\n    participant Membrane as HeLa Membrane\n\n    Agent->>Enzyme: wikipedia_search / google_search(query)\n    Enzyme-->>Agent: Structured technical summaries & citations\n    Agent->>Cytosol: browser_navigate(spec_url) -> browser_get_content(format=markdown)\n    Cytosol-->>Agent: Live technical documentation content\n    Agent->>Mitosis: llm_summarize(extracted_data)\n    Mitosis-->>Agent: Synthesized architectural comparison\n    Agent->>Genome: memory_edit(action=\"create_entities\") + memory_edit(action=\"add_observations\")\n    Agent->>Membrane: write_file(\"docs/architecture/spec-x.md\")\n```\n\n#### Step-by-Step Tool Sequence:\n1. **Knowledge Retrieval**: Query `wikipedia_search` or `google_search` via `hela-enzyme` for background literature and reference APIs.\n2. **Live Web Extraction**: Use `browser_navigate` and `browser_get_content(format=markdown)` via `hela-cytosol` to extract documentation from official docs sites.\n3. **Synthesis**: Run `llm_summarize` and `analyze_with_sequential_thinking` on `hela-mitosis` to synthesize key architectural trade-offs.\n4. **Knowledge Ingestion**: Call `memory_edit` on `hela-genome` linking findings to existing system components.\n5. **Artifact Generation**: Save the final report using `write_file` on `hela-membrane`.\n\n---\n\n### Workflow C: End-to-End Web Feature with Design & Browser Verification\n\n**Primary Profiles**: `web-devops`, `dev-workspace`\n**Components**: `hela-phenotype` → `hela-membrane` → `hela-nucleus` → `hela-cytosol` → `hela-genome`\n\n```mermaid\nsequenceDiagram\n    participant Agent\n    participant Phenotype as HeLa Phenotype\n    participant Membrane as HeLa Membrane\n    participant Nucleus as HeLa Nucleus\n    participant Cytosol as HeLa Cytosol\n    participant Genome as HeLa Genome\n\n    Agent->>Phenotype: design_tokens(action=\"theme\") / design_tokens(action=\"tailwind\")\n    Phenotype-->>Agent: Design tokens & OKLCH color palette\n    Agent->>Membrane: write_file(\"tailwind.config.js\" & component files)\n    Agent->>Nucleus: execute_command(\"npm run dev\")\n    Nucleus-->>Agent: Local dev server active on port 3000\n    Agent->>Cytosol: browser_navigate(\"http://localhost:3000\") -> browser_screenshot\n    Cytosol-->>Agent: Rendered visual screenshot & accessibility tree\n    Agent->>Genome: memory_edit(action=\"add_observations\", observations=[\"Design verified on DOM & visual snapshot\"])\n```\n\n#### Step-by-Step Tool Sequence:\n1. **Design System Token Generation**: Call `design_tokens`, `palette`, or `logo_workflow` on `hela-phenotype`.\n2. **Component Implementation**: Write JSX/Tailwind files using `write_file` on `hela-membrane`.\n3. **Dev Server Launch**: Launch background local web server using `execute_command` on `hela-nucleus`.\n4. **Visual & Accessibility Verification**: Navigate to `http://localhost:3000` via `browser_navigate` on `hela-cytosol`, verify DOM via `browser_get_content(format=axtree)`, and take visual snapshot with `browser_screenshot`.\n5. **Milestone Recording**: Save verification screenshot path and milestone to `hela-genome`.\n\n> **Live Production References Generated by HeLa Phenotype (`the-designer`):**\n> * [Ellis UI Archive / The Collection](https://demo-designer.glassgallery.my.id/) — 24 bespoke anti-slop design systems (*Game Boy 8-bit, Top Secret Dossier, Nutrition Facts, Library Card, Gothic Tome, Swiss Archival*).\n\n---\n\n### Workflow D: Mobile App Test Automation & Verification\n\n**Primary Profiles**: `android-testing`, `all`\n**Components**: `hela-genome` → `hela-nucleus` → `hela-receptor` → `hela-genome`\n\n```mermaid\nsequenceDiagram\n    participant Agent\n    participant Genome as HeLa Genome\n    participant Nucleus as HeLa Nucleus\n    participant Receptor as HeLa Receptor\n\n    Agent->>Genome: get_session_context (device test suite)\n    Agent->>Nucleus: execute_command(\"adb devices\")\n    Nucleus-->>Agent: Connected device ID detected\n    Agent->>Receptor: start_session(deviceId) -> ui_dump\n    Receptor-->>Agent: XML/axtree hierarchy of active mobile screen\n    Agent->>Receptor: tap(element_id) / input_text(\"user_input\")\n    Receptor-->>Agent: UI transition complete\n    Agent->>Receptor: screenshot(\"artifacts/test-pass.png\")\n    Agent->>Genome: memory_edit(action=\"add_observations\", observations=[\"Mobile flow X verified on physical Android\"])\n```\n\n#### Step-by-Step Tool Sequence:\n1. **Device Discovery**: Call `device_list` on `hela-receptor` or `execute_command(\"adb devices\")` on `hela-nucleus`.\n2. **Session Initialization**: Call `start_session` on `hela-receptor`.\n3. **UI Hierarchy Inspection**: Call `ui_dump` or `ui_find_element` to inspect Android view hierarchy token-efficiently via XML rather than screenshots.\n4. **Interaction**: Execute `tap`, `input_text`, `scroll`, or `key_event` to execute test scenario.\n5. **Visual Proof & Persistence**: Call `screenshot` and record test result observation in `hela-genome`.\n\n---\n\n### Workflow E: Autonomous 3D Asset Creation & Render Pipeline\n\n**Primary Profiles**: `3d-modeling`, `all`\n**Components**: `hela-genome` → `hela-mitosis` → `hela-plastid` → `hela-membrane` → `hela-genome`\n\n```mermaid\nsequenceDiagram\n    participant Agent\n    participant Genome as HeLa Genome\n    participant Mitosis as HeLa Mitosis\n    participant Plastid as HeLa Plastid\n    participant Membrane as HeLa Membrane\n\n    Agent->>Genome: search_nodes(\"3D Assets\")\n    Agent->>Mitosis: generate_route_suggestions(\"Procedural chair generation\")\n    Agent->>Plastid: generate_modeling_plan(\"Modern lounge chair\")\n    Plastid-->>Agent: Phased Python Blender script\n    Agent->>Plastid: execute_blender_code(script) -> render_output\n    Plastid-->>Agent: High-resolution render output PNG\n    Agent->>Plastid: save_blend(\"assets/chair.blend\")\n    Agent->>Genome: memory_edit(action=\"add_observations\", observations=[\"Generated 3D chair asset and render\"])\n```\n\n#### Step-by-Step Tool Sequence:\n1. **Asset Requirement Analysis**: Retrieve scene requirements from `hela-genome`.\n2. **Plan Synthesis**: Call `generate_modeling_plan` on `hela-plastid`.\n3. **Procedural Execution**: Run `execute_blender_code` to generate geometry, apply materials, and setup lights.\n4. **Render Output**: Trigger `render_output` and `get_fast_feedback` for visual QA.\n5. **Persistence**: Save `.blend` file via `save_blend` and register the asset entity in `hela-genome`.\n\n---\n\n### Workflow F: Long-Horizon Complex Refactor with PTY Harness\n\n**Primary Profiles**: `dev-workspace`, `headless-server`\n**Components**: `hela-ribosome` → `hela-nucleus` → `hela-genome`\n\n```mermaid\nsequenceDiagram\n    participant Agent\n    participant Ribosome as HeLa Ribosome\n    participant Nucleus as HeLa Nucleus\n    participant Genome as HeLa Genome\n\n    Agent->>Ribosome: session_spawn(\"long-build\", \"mvn clean install -T 4\")\n    Ribosome-->>Agent: Session spawned (ID: sess-101)\n    Agent->>Ribosome: session_read(\"sess-101\") (bounded polling)\n    Ribosome-->>Agent: Streamed stdout/stderr chunk\n    Agent->>Nucleus: execute_command(\"git status\")\n    Agent->>Genome: memory_edit(action=\"add_observations\", observations=[\"Long refactor build verified via PTY session\"])\n    Agent->>Ribosome: session_close(\"sess-101\")\n```\n\n#### Step-by-Step Tool Sequence:\n1. **Interactive Session Creation**: Call `session_spawn` on `hela-ribosome` for interactive, long-running processes (compilers, debuggers, REPLs).\n2. **Session Monitoring**: Read process output with `session_read` or send input with `session_write`.\n3. **Session Termination**: Clean up process with `session_close`.\n4. **Memory Recording**: Log completed refactor state to `hela-genome`.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Best Practices & Token Economy\n\n1. **Structured Memory Over Raw History**: Always record key findings and milestones to `hela-genome` so subsequent agent turns do not need to re-read thousands of lines of raw logs.\n2. **Hierarchy Inspection Over Full Screenshots**: For web (`hela-cytosol`) and mobile (`hela-receptor`), prefer accessibility trees and XML dumps for element location, reserving bitmap screenshots for final visual verification.\n3. **Fail-Fast Pre-Validation**: Always verify prerequisite binary availability via `./setup.sh doctor` before launching complex cross-MCP pipelines.\n"}];
